\par\par \uc2 MICHELLE GOLDBERG\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 America's Post-Roe Chaos Has Arrived\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michelle Goldberg \par \uc2 955 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 July 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 22\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 It's chaos out there.\par \par \uc2 The end of Roe v. Wade was foreseen, but in wide swaths of the country, it has still created wrenching and potentially tragic uncertainties. There have been numerous reports of patients screaming and sobbing in desperation when clinics canceled their appointments. Recipes for potentially deadly herbal abortions are going viral on TikTok. A group of hospitals, pharmacies and clinics in Missouri, a state where a so-called trigger law immediately banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  upon Roe's demise, briefly stopped providing emergency contraception. In some states, doctors who perform in vitro fertilization fear they might be prosecuted for discarding unused embryos.\par \par \uc2 Every state with an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  prohibition provides an exemption to save a mother's life, but they're not generally well-defined, so doctors and their attorneys have to decide on their own which interventions they can legally justify. What if a pregnancy might kill a woman? What if doctors believe it's going to become life-threatening, but not immediately? How sick does a patient have to be before medical professionals can act? Some doctors, said Lisa Larson-Bunnell, a health care regulatory attorney at a Missouri hospital, want to know if they can use methotrexate, a drug used to treat some cancers, lupus and rheumatoid arthritis that is also an abortifacient. ''The answers for these questions are going to be somewhat case specific,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 The vagueness in these laws is an expression of contempt. The people who wrote them either don't know enough about how reproduction interacts with other health conditions to think through what they're prohibiting, or they don't care. In the coming weeks and months, a lot of Americans who don't think anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws apply to them are likely to be shocked at how their health care is curtailed, often at the most vulnerable moments of their lives.\par \par \uc2 ''The far-reaching implications of this Supreme Court decision and its impact on women's health and people's health in a broad range of ways is just beginning to be understood,'' said Senator \uc2 Tina Smith\uc2 , a Minnesota Democrat who was once vice president at Planned Parenthood Minnesota, North Dakota, South Dakota.\par \par \uc2 The legal turmoil unleashed by the Supreme Court's decision will fundamentally change the relationship between the states. As The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  reported, a conservative legal group called The Thomas More Society is drafting model legislation based on Texas' \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bounty bill. It would let people in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is illegal sue those who help residents get abortions elsewhere. Model state legislation proposed by the \uc2 National Right to Life Committee\uc2  would create what the organization likened to RICO-style laws to target \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers who try to circumvent state bans, as well as those who publish information about where to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Under the N.R.L.C.-proposed law, transporting a minor out of state for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  without the knowledge of a parent would be a felony. It's not clear whether, say, a teenager's mother could be charged for taking her daughter across state lines for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  without telling the girl's father. This could become a particularly ugly issue in divorce and custody battles.\par \par \uc2 For those on the wrong end of one of these attempted prosecutions, traveling in red states could become dicey. Already, Planned Parenthood of Montana has stopped providing medication abortions to people from states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, citing ''the potential for both civil and criminal action.''\par \par \uc2 I keep thinking that Democrats in Congress should make Republicans vote on each element of the regime the right is trying to impose on us. Before they had the votes on the Supreme Court to overturn Roe, Republicans were skilled at putting Democrats on the defensive while chipping away at \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights; think of the so-called Partial-Birth \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Ban Act of 2003, which outlawed a late-term \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  technique. There were good reasons for Democrats to oppose that law -- it forced women facing health risks, or carrying fetuses with severe abnormalities, to undergo more dangerous procedures. But descriptions of the abortions at issue were gruesome, helping Republicans to paint pro-choice Democrats as extremists.\par \par \uc2 There are Democratic staffers who want to do something similar. One Democratic aide recently told me she wishes the Senate would try to codify the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in cases of rape, incest and threats to a mother's health, exemptions that are overwhelmingly popular with the public, but controversial among Republicans. Such a measure could both divide Republicans and, assuming they attempt to block it, highlight their fanaticism.\par \par \uc2 But the aide pointed out that pro-choice advocacy groups generally oppose such an approach, seeing it as a retreat. And Smith was skeptical, in part because she doesn't think Republicans would let such bills even get to the debate stage. ''I see no evidence that we have 10 Republicans who would be willing to provide those protections to women,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 So until Democrats win enough Senate seats to finally get rid of the filibuster, Congress is unlikely to do anything to stave off the coming catastrophe. Plan A is for Democrats to win in November. As far as I can tell there is no plan B.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTopinion) and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY Portra Images/Getty Images FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220702ei720003o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
