\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Court's Term Was Its Most Conservative Since 1931\b0\par\par\uc2 By Adam Liptak and Alicia Parlapiano \par \uc2 1,877 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 July 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The blockbuster decisions -- on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , guns, religion and climate -- told part of the story. But the court's abrupt rightward shift ran through its entire docket.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- The Supreme Court moved relentlessly to the right in its first full term with a six-justice conservative majority, issuing far-reaching decisions that will transform American life. It eliminated the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , recognized a Second Amendment right to carry guns outside the home, made it harder to address climate change and expanded the role of religion in public life.\par \par \uc2 But those blockbusters, significant though they were, only began to tell the story of the conservative juggernaut the court has become. By one standard measurement used by political scientists, the term that ended on Thursday was the most conservative since 1931.\par \par \uc2 ''The data provide stunning confirmation of the Republican-conservative takeover of the Supreme Court,'' said Lee Epstein, a law professor and political scientist at the \uc2 University of Southern California\uc2  who oversees the Supreme Court Database.\par \par \uc2 The last time the rate of conservative decisions even rivaled those in the term that ended Thursday was during Chief Justice \uc2 John G. Roberts Jr\uc2 .'s first term, which started in 2005.\par \par \uc2 Since then, the final days of Supreme Court terms have tended to end with a mix of decisions pointing in different ideological directions. That changed this week, with a string of outcomes that left conservatives jubilant and energized about the court's direction and liberals distraught.\par \par \uc2 ''Every year since \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  became chief justice, the court's results at the end of the term have been less conservative than many court watchers feared they would be at the term's outset,'' said David Cole, the national legal director of \uc2 the American Civil Liberties Union\uc2 . ''This time, the doomsayers got it exactly right, as the court traded caution for raw power.''\par \par \uc2 That can only be the consequence of the three justices President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  named to the court and particularly of his appointment of Justice Amy Coney Barrett, who joined the court after the death in 2020 of Justice \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In the decades before Justice Barrett's arrival, the court was closely divided. That meant the member of the court at its ideological center -- Justice \uc2 Anthony M. Kennedy\uc2  and then Chief Justice Roberts -- wielded enormous power. They both leaned right, but they tended to deliver a couple of major liberal victories each term.\par \par \uc2 The dynamic on the new court is different and lopsided, with six Republican appointees and three Democratic ones. The median justice appears to be Justice \uc2 Brett M. Kavanaugh\uc2 , appointed by Mr. Trump to replace the more liberal Justice Kennedy. In the term that just ended, Justice Kavanaugh moved to the right, voting in a conservative direction 79 percent of the time in divided cases in which the court heard arguments and issued signed opinions. In the prior term, that number was 58 percent.\par \par \uc2 The court's shift to the right included all sorts of legal issues, said Melissa Murray, a law professor at \uc2 New York University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''Although most Americans will focus on the cataclysmic \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  decision, there were actually a number of consequential decisions this term,'' she said. ''On critical questions like gun rights, religious liberty, federal remedies, government speech and federal regulatory authority, we saw a conservative bloc eager to make the most of its 6-3 supermajority.''\par \par \uc2 The term was also notable for its divisiveness. There was at least one dissent in 71 percent of the court's signed decisions in argued cases, the highest rate in almost four decades, according to data compiled by Professor Epstein, Andrew D. Martin of \uc2 Washington University in St. Louis\uc2  and Kevin Quinn of the \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The court's three liberals were perfectly aware that they had been marginalized by what Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  called, in dissenting from a decision that made it harder to sue federal officials for constitutional violations, ''a restless and newly constituted court.''\par \par \uc2 In their joint dissent in the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case, the three liberal justices said the court had replaced reason with power.\par \par \uc2 ''The majority has overruled Roe and Casey for one and only one reason: because it has always despised them, and now it has the votes to discard them,'' they wrote. ''The majority thereby substitutes a rule by judges for the rule of law.''\par \par \uc2 The court decided 58 cases, a slight uptick from the last two terms, which had been affected by the pandemic. But the number of signed decisions in argued cases was nonetheless the third lowest since 1937.\par \par \uc2 Nineteen decisions were decided by 6-to-3 votes, and in 13 of them all three Democratic appointees dissented. Those cases included ones on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , gun rights, climate change, school prayer, government aid to religious schools, the death penalty, campaign finance and limits on suits against government officials.\par \par \uc2 ''The Supreme Court went a lot farther a lot faster than I expected it to this term,'' said Tara Leigh Grove, a law professor at the \uc2 University of Texas at Austin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 There were, however, some divisions on the right. ''The conservative wing of the court is not a monolith,'' said Roman Martinez, a Supreme Court specialist with \uc2 Latham & Watkins\uc2 , ''and there are real and significant differences between how far to push the law in a more originalist direction, and how fast.''\par \par \uc2 The most significant example of this was Chief Justice Roberts's opinion in the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case, which would have upheld the restrictive Mississippi law at issue but would have stopped short of overruling Roe in so many words. The chief justice's failure to attract a single vote for that approach was telling, Professor Epstein said.\par \par \uc2 ''The court has morphed into the divided, partisan, maximalist, activist court that Roberts has pushed back against for nearly two decades,'' she said. ''At least for now he's lost the fight.''\par \par \uc2 The members of the court who agreed most often in divided rulings were the chief justice and Justice Kavanaugh, at 98 percent. The two justices least apt to vote together in such cases were Justices Sotomayor and \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , at 14 percent.\par \par \uc2 Among appointees of presidents of different parties, the highest rate of agreement was between Chief Justice Roberts and Justice Elena Kagan, at 48 percent.\par \par \uc2 But the larger story of the term was the powerlessness of the court's liberals, who cast votes with the majority in divided cases just 48 percent of time. Conservative justices voted with the majority 81 percent of the time. The 33 percentage point gap between the two blocs is about double the average of previous terms.\par \par \uc2 The cases and statistics discussed so far concern the so-called merits docket, where the court receives full briefing, hears arguments and issues reasoned decisions. The court also decided scores of cases on what critics call its shadow docket, where the justices often issue terse but consequential orders soon after receiving emergency applications and without hearing oral arguments.\par \par \uc2 Cases on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , voting and vaccines all reached the court by way of emergency applications this term. So did a request from Mr. Trump to block the release of White House records concerning the Jan. 6 attack on the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 The court rejected Mr. Trump's emergency application in January, with only Justice Thomas noting a dissent. Two months later, it emerged that the justice's wife, Virginia Thomas, had been sending text messages to Mr. Trump's chief of staff urging him to take steps to overturn the vote. Experts in legal ethics said Justice Thomas should have recused himself.\par \par \uc2 Analyzing emergency applications is tricky, but one trend is clear: In significant cases referred to the full court, three of its members -- Justices Thomas, \uc2 Samuel A. Alito Jr\uc2 . and \uc2 Neil M. Gorsuch\uc2  -- voted in a conservative direction at a very high rate. ''The suggestion here is one of extreme activism rushing to push through conservative interests and causes,'' Professor Epstein and Pablo Aabir Das, a recent graduate of the \uc2 University of Southern California\uc2 's law school, wrote in an analysis of the data.\par \par \uc2 The term was a triumph for the theory of constitutional interpretation known as originalism, which seeks to identify the original meaning of constitutional provisions using the tools of historians.\par \par \uc2 In a ruling that a coach at a public high school could pray on the 50-yard line after his team's games, the majority looked to ''historical practices and understandings.'' In expanding gun rights, the majority told lower courts to ''assess whether modern firearms regulations are consistent with the Second Amendment's text and historical understanding.''\par \par \uc2 And in ruling that there is no constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the majority focused on ''how the states regulated \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the 14th Amendment was adopted'' in 1868.\par \par \uc2 The court's three liberals dissented in all three cases, calling originalism cramped and wooden. In a joint opinion in the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case, they wrote that ''the framers defined rights in general terms, to permit future evolution in their scope and meaning.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Martinez, the Supreme Court specialist at \uc2 Latham & Watkins\uc2 , said the developments were telling in two ways.\par \par \uc2 ''First,'' he said, ''it's clear a majority of the court is firmly committed to an originalist understanding of the Constitution rooted in the document's text and history. Second, that majority will act boldly to apply its originalist philosophy in ways that curb certain perceived excesses of 20th-century 'living constitutionalism,' even when doing so is controversial and at odds with public opinion polls.''\par \par \uc2 Justice Alito, writing for the majority in the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case, said public opinion should play no role in the court's decision making. ''We cannot allow our decisions to be affected by any extraneous influences such as concern about the public's reaction to our work,'' he wrote.\par \par \uc2 The court's public approval is certainly plummeting. In a Gallup poll taken after the leaked draft of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opinion but before the formal decision, for instance, public confidence in the court fell to 25 percent, the lowest in the nearly 50 years over which the survey has been conducted.\par \par \uc2 Professor Grove said the court's authority could not withstand a lasting loss of public trust.\par \par \uc2 ''When you lose enough institutional legitimacy, people just aren't going to obey your decisions,'' she said. ''We're not close to that point yet. But we could get to that point.''\par \par \uc2 The court is not slowing down. In its next term, which starts in October, it will decide the fate of affirmative action in higher education, how to interpret the Voting Rights Act in the context of redistricting and whether a web designer has a First Amendment right to refuse to work on projects involving same-sex weddings.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, as they were about to begin their summer breaks, the justices agreed to hear one more blockbuster, one that could radically reshape American elections, on the power of state legislatures to set voting rules.\par \par \uc2 (A1); The Supreme Court ruled on 58 cases in its most recent term, 19 of which were decided by 6-to-3 votes. In 13 of those, all three Democratic appointees dissented. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ERIN SCHAFF/THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A17) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220702ei720003j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
