\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Fears That Texas Isn't Prepared for a Rise of Births Post-Roe\b0\par\par\uc2 By Elizabeth Williamson and Erin Schaff \par \uc2 1,915 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 July 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 18\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ARGYLE, Texas -- Two days after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, a 27-year-old woman delivered her fourth child, a boy she named Cason. Born after his mother fled from domestic abuse and was denied an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he is among the first of many post-Roe babies expected in Texas.\par \par \uc2 ''I love my kids and I feel like I'm a really good mom,'' said Cason's mother, who asked to be identified by her first initial, T. ''But due to this pregnancy, I couldn't provide for them.''\par \par \uc2 One in 10 people of reproductive age in America lives in Texas, which will soon join half of all the states in outlawing almost all abortions. Texas' conservative leadership has spent decades narrowing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access while cutting social spending and publicly funded health care. Now, even some anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  adherents say their state is woefully unprepared for a likely surge in births among poor women.\par \par \uc2 The overturning of Roe ''creates the sense of urgency that now will create, hopefully, the resources. But unfortunately, there's that gap,'' said Aubrey Schlackman, founder of Blue Haven Ranch, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nonprofit that is providing housing and other assistance for T.'s family.\par \par \uc2 ''We do want to limit abortions,'' Ms. Schlackman continued. ''But we personally weren't ready to handle an influx, and I know so many of the other nonprofits that we work with aren't ready for that, either.''\par \par \uc2 Texas is one of the most dangerous states in the nation to have a baby. The state's maternal mortality rate is one of the worst in the country, with Black women making up a disproportionate share of deaths. The state's infant mortality rate, at more than five deaths per thousand births in 2020, translates into nearly 2,000 infant deaths annually.\par \par \uc2 Texas opted not to expand Medicaid under the Affordable Care Act, which helped lead to hospital closures and the formation of rural health care ''deserts,'' where obstetricians are scarce and prenatal care scarcer still. More than a quarter of women of childbearing age are uninsured, the highest rate in the nation. Medicaid covers low-income women through pregnancy and for two months postpartum, compared with 12 months in most states.\par \par \uc2 A proposal in the Texas House to expand postpartum coverage to 12 months was cut to six months by the State Senate. Tens of thousands of children born to low-income parents languish on the waiting list for subsidized child care.\par \par \uc2 In September of last year Texas passed Senate Bill 8, banning abortions for patients with detectable embryonic cardiac activity, which generally begins at about six weeks. A recent Times analysis suggests that Texas' \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate declined by only 10 percent after the bill passed, as more women traveled out of state or ordered medication abortions by mail. But poor patients often lack those options.\par \par \uc2 ''Assuming just 10 percent of women aren't able to to secure an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , that's a massive rise in fertility,'' said Elizabeth Sepper, a law professor at the \uc2 University of Texas at Austin\uc2 , who studies religious liberty, health law and equality.\par \par \uc2 ''There's no way there are any institutions prepared to meet that demand.''\par \par \uc2 Three years ago, T. was a bookkeeper for a chain of fitness centers. At $36 an hour, it was the best-paying job she had ever held. She was proud to become her family's main breadwinner after her partner, whom she has been with since high school, lost his construction job during the pandemic. But early in her pregnancy with Cason, she developed complications that eventually forced her to quit her job.\par \par \uc2 The family economized, moving into smaller and smaller homes until late last year, when they finally had to move in with the mother of her partner. The couple were unloading their belongings, with their infant daughter in her stroller nearby, when ''he snapped on me,'' T. said. Her partner choked her, she said, until she lost consciousness. When she was revived by a stranger she had trouble speaking, and a ring of bruises circled her neck. Terrified for her children, she fled the next morning to a shelter for domestic violence victims, she said.\par \par \uc2 She said she had never sought an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before. But the prospect of raising four young children on her own, and of giving birth alone, filled T. with desperation. She agonized about the needs of her three children, and about sacrifices. ''If I do this, I will make sure they're always good, are always taken care of,'' she said she recalled thinking.\par \par \uc2 ''It was a very difficult decision, but I felt like it was a smart one for me.''\par \par \uc2 Her sister drove her to Southwestern Women's Surgery Center, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider in Dallas. But Texas had just enacted Senate Bill 8, and the providers told T. that she was about seven weeks pregnant -- too far along for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Texas. Could she travel to New Mexico? In the waiting room, T. sobbed. The trip was impossible. She had no money, and so few child care options that she had brought her baby daughter with her to the appointment. She didn't know about medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 T. rejoined her sister, who was waiting in the parking lot. She was sitting in the car, distraught, when an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ''sidewalk counselor'' approached.\par \par \uc2 '''You are not alone. If you are pregnant and you need help, we can help you,''' the sidewalk counselor told her, T. recalled.\par \par \uc2 ''I just started crying,'' T. said, ''in a sense of relief.''\par \par \uc2 The next day the woman T. had met in the parking lot guided her to Birth Choice, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pregnancy resource center located in the same office complex as the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider.\par \par \uc2 Some anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis pregnancy centers have come under scrutiny for misleading or misinforming women seeking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care. But in that moment, ''They asked me the perfect questions,'' T. said of the Birth Choice counselor. ''Am I OK? Are my kids doing OK? What did I need?''\par \par \uc2 ''Mind you, I had left everything,'' she said. ''They provided me with everything right there: baby bag, diapers, formula, clothes for me. They even gave me a couple of little clothes for my daughter and a toy,'' T. said.\par \par \uc2 ''Then my counselor comes back and says, 'I found you a place.'''\par \par \uc2 The place was Blue Haven Ranch, based in Argyle, about 45 minutes from Dallas.\par \par \uc2 Blue Haven provides housing, help with household bills, job training, and financial and other counseling for up to a year or more after delivery for pregnant women with existing children. Among Americans who seek \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, 60 percent are already mothers, and half have two or more children. Most are in their late 20s, and poor.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Schlackman, 34, a former dental hygienist, evangelical Christian and mother of two, founded Blue Haven in 2020.\par \par \uc2 She grew up believing that women seek \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care for the sake of convenience. ''Now I can understand why they would choose it,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Schlackman requires women to attend group informational sessions with a strong religious component in a community church on Monday nights. Blue Haven does not seek money from the government or anyone else that might question its religious approach. It takes in donations from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters as well as opponents, Ms. Schlackman said, reading a note from one, who sent $50: '''I don't share your beliefs about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Christianity, but I do hope you'll use your strength to encourage similar initiatives elsewhere.'''\par \par \uc2 Blue Haven supports five families, and there are 12 on the waiting list. The cost is about $2,500 per family per month for housing and utilities, plus gas and unexpected household expenses. A financier in Boston who read about Blue Haven and offered to help recently negotiated a deal on a used car for a mother with a poor credit score.\par \par \uc2 Currently there is no ranch; the families live in rented apartments. Ms. Schlackman and her husband Bryan have plans to buy a patch of rolling acreage outside Denton, Texas, and build a compound with small homes, a meeting house and group kitchen, plus open spaces and livestock for ''farm therapy.''\par \par \uc2 Standing in the wheat field where she envisions the houses will stand, Ms. Schlackman estimated that she would need to raise $13 million for the land, construction and three years' operating funds. After Roe was overturned, Blue Haven received $25,000 in donations in two days.\par \par \uc2 Its focus on the Bible and emphasis on Christian family ideals make some Blue Haven mothers uncomfortable. But for T., the group offered a lifeline in a time of dwindling options. One recent Monday night she attended a group session while her children played on the church's pristine playground, supervised by grandparent volunteers. Other volunteers laid out a communal supper.\par \par \uc2 Blue Haven threw a baby shower for T., and its supporters bought everything on a registry that Ms. Schlackman created. (T. chose a zoo animal theme for her son's layette, in shades of blue and green.) When Cason was born Ms. Schlackman was there, attending to T. in the spalike birthing center where she had delivered her own sons.\par \par \uc2 Blue Haven's assistance will end about a year after Cason's first birthday.\par \par \uc2 ''The pressure is really on,'' T. said on a Thursday, four days after she gave birth to Cason. ''I have one year to rebuild my life while my body heals, and four kids to take care of at the same time. It's scary. I try not to think about what will happen when I leave the program. I know I can be a great mom, it's just, can I provide for my children, keep the kids healthy and safe and have a roof over our head, and food?''\par \par \uc2 She is hoping, she said, to get another job as a bookkeeper and eventually move into her own home.\par \par \uc2 She said she has a message for the Texas Legislature.\par \par \uc2 ''You don't know what is best for any family, you didn't protect me or my kids. I protect my kids. Only a mom can know what is best for herself and her family. And if you're going to force women to have all of these babies that they are not equipped to have, then you need to provide support for women and their children after the babies are born.''\par \par \uc2 Earlier in the week, just a day and a half after giving birth, T. had something else to say.\par \par \uc2 ''Women, all we really have is our dignity and our voices,'' she said. ''And you're taking them away.''\par \par \uc2 Erin Schaff contributed reporting from Argyle, and Margot Sanger-Katz from Washington.\par \par \uc2 Aubrey Schlackman, the founder of Blue Haven Ranch, left, attended to T. during her labor. Blue Haven supports five families.; ''Sidewalk counselors'' offer antiabortion advice outside of clinics.; ''I have one year to rebuild my life while my body heals, and four kids to take care of at the same time. It's scary,'' (A18); T. said.; T., a 27-year-old mother in Texas, top, was denied an \uc2  after she fled domestic violence. Her fourth child, Cason, above in an herbal bath, is among the first of many post-Roe babies expected in the state. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY ERIN SCHAFF) (A18-A19) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220702ei720003d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
