\par\par \uc2 GAIL COLLINS\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 The Supreme Court Takes Us Back ... Way Back\b0\par\par\uc2 By Gail Collins \par \uc2 1,065 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 22\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Everybody's been talking about the comments Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  made during his portion of the Dobbs decision. You know, where he basically said birth control and gay marriage are up next.\par \par \uc2 What's your take?\par \par \uc2 A. Very, very worried he's right.\par \par \uc2 B. Comforted by the fact that the other justices in the majority didn't seem to agree.\par \par \uc2 C. This whole thing is ... aaaaahhhhhhhhhhhh.\par \par \uc2 OK, stop banging your head against the wall. Nothing is going to be made better by putting welts on your forehead.\par \par \uc2 And because I can see how crazy this is making you, I will refrain from mentioning that Donald Trump quickly dispatched a fund-raising email taking credit for the Roe decision, which began, ''You're welcome.''\par \par \uc2 Not to be outdone, \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 's folks sent out a video highlighting his longtime effort to defund Planned Parenthood. In one small way this is sort of a relief. Really, we've had to be grateful to Pence for his role in officially recognizing the results of the presidential election for ages now. Time to get back to remembering him as Mr. Abstinence Only.\par \par \uc2 The other justices dissociated themselves from Thomas's predictions about Repressions for Tomorrow, but face it -- politicians and political activists who've built their careers fighting women's right to choice won't declare victory, retire and suddenly take up knitting.\par \par \uc2 They've already started their next moves. Tennessee's ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after about six weeks of pregnancy went into effect soon after the decision overturning Roe came down. Lawmakers in some states have started targeting morning-after pills and IUDs.\par \par \uc2 Where do they want to take us? Think about what the world was like back when women had little or no ability to control reproduction. There was, of course, the spinsterhood option. But as much as later generations venerated \uc2 Susan B. Anthony\uc2 , her way of life didn't have widespread appeal. Most went for marriage, which generally meant centering your life around making your husband happy and your house spick-and-span.\par \par \uc2 Anything else was ... selfishness. ''There is such a struggle among women to become artists that I wish some of their gifts could be illustrated in clean, orderly, beautiful homes,'' sniffed Elizabeth Cady Stanton. Who nevertheless teamed up with Anthony after discovering a life dedicated to domesticity was boring her to tears.\par \par \uc2 Totally understandable, however, that most women earlier in our history would yearn to be homemakers. By the middle of the 19th century the cities were filling up with lower-income women putting in 13 or 14 hours a day on the job. One of them, Hester Vaughn, became a feminist cause. As suffragists told her story, she was raped by her employer in Philadelphia, left pregnant and abandoned in one cold attic room with no food. Eventually she went into labor alone and was found lying on the floor next to her dead baby. She was tried for infanticide and sentenced to be hung, then finally pardoned by the governor. Becoming an excellent example of the need for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  option.\par \par \uc2 Maybe it's not fair to pin Hester Vaughn's fate on \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , but we ought to look back at the time he seems to feel was a golden era in \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 . We're talking about the land before Griswold v. Connecticut, the Supreme Court ruling in 1965 that held, in part, that it was unconstitutional for states to ban the sale of contraceptives.\par \par \uc2 At the time, anyone convicted of using a birth control device in Connecticut -- even a married couple already raising six kids -- could be sentenced to up to a year in prison. In one of the very few times the matter ever came up for official debate in the state legislature, The Times reported a motion to change the law was defeated on a voice vote that ''took less than a minute.''\par \par \uc2 When it came to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the whole country was talking about Sherri Finkbine, the host of a children's TV show in Arizona. She was pregnant with her fifth child in 1962 when she discovered that a sedative her husband had brought back from an overseas trip contained thalidomide, and that she'd taken enough to cause damage to the fetus.\par \par \uc2 Finkbine scheduled an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but she felt obliged to let the world know how dangerous those sedatives could be. Her attempt to be an anonymous source was a total failure, and when her story became public, the hospital canceled her procedure, the courts refused to give her any support and she lost her job hosting ''Romper Room.''\par \par \uc2 Finally, after a lot of publicity, she succeeded in getting an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Sweden, where the physician who performed the procedure said the fetus was massively deformed. But when she returned home, she discovered she'd been deemed ''unfit to work with children'' by a local TV station.\par \par \uc2 Obviously things are different now. Nevertheless, overturning Roe has pushed us back in time, and before we get shoved any further it's a good idea to remember that control over reproduction is at the absolute center of the story of women in the modern world.\par \par \uc2 When it came to things like employment and careers, the great, cosmic dividing line between men and women was always that employers didn't have to worry about men becoming pregnant. Then along came contraception. Everything changed and a father who dreamed about having his child take over the family business didn't fall into despair when he heard the baby was going to be a girl. Soon, new mothers had the joy -- and yeah, the challenge -- of knowing they'd be able to mesh parenthood with careers of their own.\par \par \uc2 While nobody's admitting it, this is what the anti-choice movement wants to retract. Let's remind the Supreme Court of it every day.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTopinion) and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY Mark Peterson/Redux for The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220630ei6u0003o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
