\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Rifts Abound Over Enforcing  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Bans\b0\par\par\uc2 By J. David Goodman and Jack Healy \par \uc2 1,798 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A reluctance by some liberal district attorneys to bring criminal charges against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers is already complicating the legal landscape in some states.\par \par \uc2 HOUSTON -- Deep rifts have emerged among the hundreds of elected district attorneys who will be charged with enforcing the expanding restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , creating a Balkanized new legal system within states that are banning the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Dozens of Democratic prosecutors who represent liberal pockets in conservative states already have vowed to resist bans by refusing to bring charges against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers. But in many rural areas and outlying suburbs, conservative prosecutors have said they will enforce their state bans.\par \par \uc2 An opening salvo came this week, just days after the Supreme Court struck down the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , when a prominent anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group in Texas urged the police and prosecutors in Dallas and Houston to open criminal investigations into three \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics that the group said were preparing to violate the law by scheduling abortions.\par \par \uc2 ''It is your duty to uphold the law and investigate crimes,'' the group, Texas Right to Life, said in its letter, in which it claimed to have phone recordings of clinic employees. ''We urge you and your officers to investigate this \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic.''\par \par \uc2 The demand was quickly made moot when a judge temporarily blocked attempts to resurrect the state's long-dormant, century-old \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law in the weeks before a new ban takes effect, but it marked the beginning of an explosive new battle over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in America: How prosecutors and the police will enforce laws that criminalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in two dozen states, some of them carrying penalties of up to 15 years in prison.\par \par \uc2 More than 80 elected prosecutors have already vowed not to. In a joint statement released after the Supreme Court's decision last week, they said that enforcing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans on what had for decades been a legal medical procedure would degrade public trust, especially for sexual assault victims, and drain resources from prosecuting what they called serious crimes.\par \par \uc2 ''Criminalizing and prosecuting individuals who seek or provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care makes a mockery of justice,'' the prosecutors said. ''Prosecutors should not be part of that.''\par \par \uc2 But such protection could be limited. In some states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, Republican attorneys general could intercede to bring \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cases even if local prosecutors refuse. In Texas, where district attorneys in five of the state's most populous counties have said they will not ''criminalize personal health care decisions,'' a Republican legislator has proposed a law that could empower conservative rural prosecutors to charge urban residents for violating Texas' \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws.\par \par \uc2 Other schisms are erupting between neighboring cities and counties.\par \par \uc2 In Michigan, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers could soon face felony charges under an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban that has remained on the books since 1931. But Karen McDonald, the Oakland County prosecuting attorney, said the law was useless and antiquated, comparing it to old statutes banning cows running at large. She said she would not enforce an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 Ms. McDonald, a Democrat who supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, said her office was already overburdened trying to address epidemics of gun violence, fentanyl overdoses and domestic violence. She said she would not peel away resources to prosecute people for what she considered a constitutional right.\par \par \uc2 ''I will never take part in a prosecution that is designed to take away a woman's right to decide her reproductive freedom,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 But next door in Macomb County, Prosecutor Peter Lucido, a Republican, has said publicly that he would enforce the ban if it is upheld in court. The law has been suspended by a state judge after challenges from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups.\par \par \uc2 And in Grand Rapids, the Kent County prosecutor vowed to enforce the existing 1931 ban and prosecute doctors, saying he would not ''turn a blind eye'' and ignore what he called a valid law.\par \par \uc2 It is unlikely that even the most vociferous promises of protection from Democratic prosecutors will persuade \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics to remain open in defiance of state bans.\par \par \uc2 So far, there have been few concrete actions by prosecutors, in part because many \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics in states that had adopted bans quickly stopped providing services after the Supreme Court's decision.\par \par \uc2 ''I believe that most providers, and certainly Planned Parenthood, intend to follow the law,'' said Helene Krasnoff, vice president for public policy litigation and law at \uc2 Planned Parenthood Federation of America\uc2 . At the same time, she said, some people will violate the bans because ''patients are going to be desperate.''\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups said there could be serious consequences if law enforcement officials moved to aggressively investigate and prosecute abortions as crimes.\par \par \uc2 While existing laws largely exempt women who get abortions, defense lawyers and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups are worried that zealous prosecutors could bring cases against anyone who helps them get abortions, or \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills. Advocates say the 2014 case of a Pennsylvania mother who was jailed for ordering \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills for her teenage daughter is a harbinger.\par \par \uc2 ''It's open season,'' said C. Melissa Owen, a criminal defense lawyer in Charlotte, N.C., who has studied laws criminalizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ''Best friends, aunts, mothers, boyfriends, receptionists, nurses -- anyone providing care or assistance falls under the umbrella of being a co-conspirator.''\par \par \uc2 In Oklahoma, where an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban from the moment of fertilization went into effect on Friday, the attorney general, John O'Connor, promised that there would be immediate enforcement, including against those who ''solicit'' abortions, and said that could include companies that have said they would support employees traveling out of state for abortions.\par \par \uc2 ''I would say if you put up a billboard, or if you advertise that you're going to provide abortions in Oklahoma or in another state, that you're soliciting an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' Mr. O'Connor told reporters.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights researchers say prosecutors have for years employed homicide and child-abuse laws to charge women who induce abortions, suffer miscarriages or use drugs during pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Some 1,300 women have faced such charges or arrests since 2006, according to National Advocates for Pregnant Women.\par \par \uc2 In one of the most recent cases, a 26-year-old woman in a Texas border county was briefly charged with murder in April in connection with what authorities called a ''self-induced \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .'' A statement from a sheriff's official had said the woman had been indicted on a murder charge for ''intentionally and knowingly'' causing an individual's death, but the local district attorney quickly scuttled the charge amid a public outcry, saying she had not violated Texas law.\par \par \uc2 An Indiana woman was convicted of ''feticide'' in 2015 and sentenced to 20 years in prison for a self-induced \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , before her conviction was overturned. Though feticide laws are ostensibly written to address violence inflicted on pregnant women, activists say prosecutors often use the laws to charge women themselves.\par \par \uc2 Even in California, which has burnished itself as a lifeboat for women in need of abortions, there have been two recent cases in which women were charged with homicide after delivering stillborn babies.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court's Roe decision could also put new muscle into ''personhood'' laws that give full legal rights to embryos from the earliest stage of development. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups fear that pregnant women could be at greater legal risk of being charged with homicide or child abuse if they miscarry, induce an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  themselves or use recreational drugs or even prescription medication during pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 There are also privacy worries that prosecutors will subpoena women's medical records and private social media files as part of criminal investigations into \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers. Already, some \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates have urged women to delete apps that track menstrual cycles for fear their data could end up in a law enforcement dragnet.\par \par \uc2 ''The women themselves are the source of evidence, whether it's their body through exams or insisting on testimony and putting them up for public testimony in court,'' said Leslie Reagan, a professor of history at the \uc2 University of Illinois\uc2 , Urbana-Champaign, who has looked at how \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was criminalized in the years before Roe v. Wade. ''Women were punished by the state through that interrogation process.''\par \par \uc2 In Dallas, the local district attorney, John Creuzot, has vowed to use his prosecutorial discretion when it comes to cases under a new Texas law, set to take effect this summer, that will ban nearly all abortions. ''My office will not stand in the way of them seeking the health care they need,'' Mr. Creuzot said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 But there was a very different message from prosecutors just over the county line in Tarrant County, a traditionally conservative bastion that includes the city of Fort Worth, the last major Texas city with Republican leadership.\par \par \uc2 ''We do not choose which laws we follow,'' the district attorney, Sharen Wilson, said in a statement. ''If the facts warrant prosecution, then the case will be presented to a grand jury for consideration.''\par \par \uc2 Some Democratic prosecutors are uneasy about declaring their districts safe havens for abortions, worried that any explicit vow not to enforce the law could be used as grounds for their own removal by Republican leaders.\par \par \uc2 ''Prosecutors and police should have no role in matters between doctors and patients,'' said Kim Ogg, the district attorney for Harris County, which includes Houston. ''As in every case, we will evaluate the facts and make decisions on a case-by-case basis.'' She added that her office ''did not want to take the chance of being found to be in dereliction of our duty or to risk having \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cases turned over to Texas Attorney General \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 Others, like the Travis County district attorney, Jos\u233\'00\'E9 Garza, have been more direct and categorical.\par \par \uc2 ''We will not prosecute people who seek abortions or people who provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services,'' Mr. Garza said in a news conference on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The split among district attorneys could likely affect how the law is enforced by the local police, for whom the situation is fraught and still evolving.\par \par \uc2 Chief Jimmy Perdue of North Richland Hills, Texas, said he anticipated that investigations would include an examination of online and digital records related to potential violations of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, as is common in most criminal cases. Any need to obtain health records would add to the difficulty, he said.\par \par \uc2 ''I don't foresee anyone, any law enforcement agency striking out on their own to take overt, aggressive actions,'' said Chief Perdue, who is also president of the Texas Police Chiefs Association. ''I think it will be a very slow, wait-and-see the landscape of what guidance we're given.''\par \par \uc2 Karen McDonald, a Michigan prosecutor, said she would refuse anti-\uc2  cases. (PHOTOGRAPH BY NICK HAGEN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A18) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220630ei6u0003i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
