\par\par \uc2 GLOBAL HEALTH\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Brazilians Tap A Black Market In  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Pills\b0\par\par\uc2 By Stephanie Nolen \par \uc2 2,155 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 With Roe v. Wade overturned, states banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are looking to prevent the distribution of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication. Brazil shows the possible consequences.\par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO -- Last November, Xaiana, a 23-year-old college student in northern Brazil, began exchanging text messages with a drug dealer in the south of the country. Following the dealer's instructions, she transferred 1,500 reais ($285), her living expenses for several months. Then, she waited three agonizing weeks for the arrival in the mail of a blister pack of eight unmarked white pills.\par \par \uc2 When she took them, they had the effect she was hoping for: She underwent a medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at home with her boyfriend, ending an eight-week pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 But Xaiana kept bleeding for weeks, an unusual but not rare complication. ''It was like a murder scene every time I had a shower,'' she said. She was afraid to get help because it is illegal for a woman in Brazil to use the drug, misoprostol, to trigger an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . If she went to a clinic, she feared, the staff might figure out she had induced the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and report her. The penalty for having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Brazil is up to three years in jail.\par \par \uc2 ''It's the loneliest feeling I've ever felt in my life,'' she said, asking to be identified only by her first name out of fear of prosecution.\par \par \uc2 After seven weeks, she went to a women's clinic and admitted to having terminated a pregnancy. She was given a simple cauterization, and no one reported her.\par \par \uc2 Proponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the United States have suggested that a post-Roe America would differ in a key way from the era before \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was legalized nationally. Women seeking abortions today have the option of a medical termination, using hormone pills to trigger the body to expel the fetus in private, a practice approved by the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But the wave of state trigger laws that have begun to take effect after the Supreme Court's ruling overturning Roe on Friday bar all \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including medication abortions. To get the pills legally, women will have to travel to states where it is allowed, for a medical consultation, even if it is by video or phone, as required by the F.D.A.\par \par \uc2 The trajectory of access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills in Brazil may offer insight into how medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  can become out of reach and what can happen when it does.\par \par \uc2 While surgical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was the original target of Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, the proscription expanded after medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  became more common, leading to the situation today where drug traffickers control most access to the pills. Women who procure them have no guarantee of the safety or authenticity of what they are taking, and if they have complications, they fear seeking help.\par \par \uc2 Today, black market misoprostol, brought in from India, Mexico and Argentina, is sold for anywhere from about $200 to $400 for the eight tablets recommended for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , compared with less than $15 for a 60-pill bottle in the United States. It took a \uc2 New York Times\uc2  reporter under one minute of asking to find someone willing to sell eight pills for $300, in a Rio neighborhood known for the sale of black market goods.\par \par \uc2 ''You buy it from a dealer, you don't know what it is, the whole process is made frightening, it's secret, it's not a medicine any more,'' said Maira Marques, who is the director of campaigns for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access advocacy organization called Milhas pelas Vidas das Mulheres. ''This is supposed to be the straightforward, less complicated way to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  but now instead it's buying contraband.''\par \par \uc2 It has been illegal in Brazil to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since 1890, although exceptions were added in 1940 for women who were pregnant as a result of rape or incest and in cases where a woman's life was endangered by the pregnancy; more recently, access was added for women carrying a fetus with anencephaly (missing parts of its brain).\par \par \uc2 But starting in the late 1980s, word spread that an ulcer medication called Cytotec could ''bring on a period.'' In fact, it was Brazilian women's experience with off-label use of the drug that led to research and eventual global adoption of medical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a lower-cost, less invasive way to end pregnancies that could increase access, especially in developing countries.\par \par \uc2 Cytotec is misoprostol, one half of the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2 's recommended combination of hormones (the other is mifepristone) to carry out a medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Mifepristone has never been approved for use in Brazil, and women, unaware of the drug, do not seek it on the black market. Misoprostol is usually enough to induce a safe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; a study published in The Lancet found that 8 percent of women who used misoprostol to terminate a pregnancy experienced complications, including bleeding and abdominal pain requiring medical attention.\par \par \uc2 The drug was sold in pharmacies without a prescription until 1991, and then it was regulated to require prescription, although the prescription rules were lax.\par \par \uc2 The availability of the pills sharply reduced the number of women turning up in hospitals with the life-threatening infections or hemorrhages from abortions they had tried to induce with the castor root or bleach or coat hangers, said Dr. Ana Teresa Derraik, an obstetrician-gynecologist in Rio. ''It was a big relief for those of us who didn't think women should be punished like this.''\par \par \uc2 But misoprostol was becoming a focus of attention for anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaigners in Brazil and beyond. In 1998, Brazil's health regulatory agency, \uc2 ANVISA\uc2 , included misoprostol on the list of controlled drugs, alongside opiates, which meant a prison sentence of up to 15 years for anyone caught importing or buying it. International pharmaceutical companies that made misoprostol were hit with boycotts and stopped producing it; a small domestic company took over manufacturing a generic version of the drug to sell only to the Ministry of Health for hospital use.\par \par \uc2 In 2006, the law prohibiting misoprostol distribution was strengthened to ban selling or publishing information about the drug on the internet.\par \par \uc2 When \uc2 Jair Bolsonaro\uc2  was elected Brazil's president in 2018, with the enthusiastic support of Brazil's fast-growing evangelical Christian community, access became even more scarce.\par \par \uc2 International \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  organizations such as Women on Web used to mail \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to Brazil, and local feminist groups used to source them and supply them, along with instructions for safe use, said Juliana Reis, director of Milhas. Now, they have almost entirely stopped.\par \par \uc2 ''Because of the political climate, it's much more difficult to get safe products and to get proper counseling, because the networks that used to do that are much more afraid,'' said Sonia Corr\u234\'00\'EAa, a researcher of \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  technologies in Rio.\par \par \uc2 New guidelines issued by the Ministry of Health this month include the assertion that ''inducing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  by telemedicine, using drugs from the special control list, can cause irreversible damage to the woman.''\par \par \uc2 Dr. Helena Paro, a gynecologist in the city of Uberl\u226\'00\'E2ndia, who introduced telemedicine consultations for legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients during the Covid pandemic, called the guideline ''completely ideological and contrary to the scientific evidence.'' The W.H.O. considers the practice safe.\par \par \uc2 In response to questions from The Times, the ministry said the guidelines reflect that ''misoprostol is authorized only in hospital establishments, that its use outside this environment is not allowed'' by law and that use of misoprostol for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  via telemedicine meant women would not have ''timely access to health services that can manage the possible clinical or surgical complications resulting from the procedure.''\par \par \uc2 Dr. Derraik says she has seen an intensification of scrutiny for use of misoprostol in the hospitals where she offers \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services to women who qualify as well as a simultaneous increase in the level of investigation of women who report miscarriages.\par \par \uc2 Other women have fallen into police traps. In 2012, a Rio sociologist decided she could not continue her pregnancy -- she was already struggling to parent a 12-year-old with intense special needs. The sociologist (who asked to be identified by her first initial, A., because her family does not know about her \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) went to her gynecologist.\par \par \uc2 ''He said, 'This medication exists, Cytotec, but I can't give it to you: You'll have to buy it from the black market,''' she recalled.\par \par \uc2 She found a website, ordered the drug and paid several months' salary for it, but the package never arrived: She was tracking it online, and watched it stall when it entered the country.\par \par \uc2 She found a drug trafficking contact through a friend of a friend and bought a second batch, took the drug at home and ended the pregnancy with no complications -- her only regret being that she had to be alone through a frightening process.\par \par \uc2 A year later, a letter arrived summoning her to the police. She thought it was about her car, which had been stolen the year before. But when she arrived, a male officer asked her, ''Do you know what Cytotec is?''\par \par \uc2 She said she did. He asked if she had bought it. She could see he had her credit card information from the purchase on the paper in front of him, so she admitted she had.\par \par \uc2 He asked if she had carried out the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . She replied, ''Of course not -- the medication never arrived.''\par \par \uc2 It turned out that police were monitoring the website where she bought misoprostol, traced the package and said they would charge her with unlawful purchase of a controlled substance. After several years of hearings, she entered an alternative sentencing program and performed 60 hours of community service. She continues to have to report her whereabouts to police and cannot leave her state.\par \par \uc2 Women's reliance on the black market for access to medication abortions means they may not follow best medical practice. When C., a 24-year-old teacher in Recife, bought misoprostol from a drug dealer last year, she searched \uc2 Google\uc2  to figure out how to take it. ''Because it was illegal, there was no information about how to take it or what to take,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Her search found recommendations to insert the tablets in her vagina, as a doctor would if she were in a clinic, but cautioned that traces might be left behind and give her away if she wound up in hospital; instead, she dissolved them under her tongue, a method that also works but less quickly.\par \par \uc2 C., who asked to be identified by only her middle initial out of fear of prosecution, bled for weeks after and wanted to ask her mother, a gynecologist, for advice. But her mother is an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activist. Finally, C. said she thought she had miscarried, and her mother took her to see a colleague who performed a dilation-and-curettage under anesthetic.\par \par \uc2 ''When I was having the curettage, I had to keep saying over and over to myself, 'Don't say anything, you can't say anything' -- it was torture,'' she said. ''Even though I was totally sure that I wanted an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , I had no doubts, you still feel like you've done something wrong because you can't talk about it.''\par \par \uc2 The restriction on misoprostol has complicated regular obstetric care, which uses the drug for induction of labor, said Dr. Derraik. At the Rio public maternity hospital where she is medical director, a doctor must fill out a request in triplicate for the drug, have it signed by Dr. Derraik, take it to the pharmacy where the supervisor must also sign before taking it out of a locked cabinet, and then the physician must administer the drug with a witness, to ensure it is not diverted for black market sale.\par \par \uc2 ''Not all of these steps are officially required,'' Dr. Derraik said. ''But hospitals do them because of the intense paranoia around the drug.''\par \par \uc2 President Jair Bolsonaro of Brazil, center, raising an image of Jesus at a gathering with anti-\uc2  activists in Bras\u237\'00\'EDlia in 2020. Access to \uc2  drugs tightened after he was elected in 2018. (PHOTOGRAPH BY SERGIO LIMA/AGENCE FRANCE-PRESSE -- GETTY IMAGES); Juliana Reis, director of an \uc2  access advocacy group in Brazil, with artwork being sold to raise money. Local feminist groups used to get \uc2  pills from abroad but it's much harder now.; XAIANA, who kept bleeding for weeks after a medication \uc2  and was afraid to get help.; At hospitals, misoprostol is locked up, and scrutiny of its use is intense even for doctors whose patients legally qualify for it.; Misoprostol was sold in Brazil's pharmacies without a prescription until 1991. In 1998 it was added to the list of controlled drugs. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY DADO GALDIERI FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) ( \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220630ei6u00032\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
