\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Biden Administration Wrestles With Options To Safeguard Access\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sheryl Gay Stolberg and Charlie Savage \par \uc2 1,435 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 19\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 With the White House under pressure to push back on the Supreme Court's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ruling, the health secretary unveiled steps aimed at preserving access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- As Democrats and \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates clamored for President Biden to forcefully counter the Supreme Court's decision overturning Roe v. Wade, his health secretary, \uc2 Xavier Becerra\uc2 , stepped up to a lectern here on Tuesday to list the steps his department would take to preserve and expand access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The list, for now anyway, is short.\par \par \uc2 ''There is no magic bullet,'' Mr. Becerra said at a morning news conference, ''but if there is something we can do, we will find it and we will do it.''\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court's decision on Friday eliminating the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not unexpected, yet neither the White House nor Mr. Becerra's agency had immediate policy responses at the ready. Officials inside the administration say they are still wrestling with the prospect of a mainstream area of women's health care suddenly becoming illegal in roughly half the country, and will need time to sort through their options.\par \par \uc2 Yet Mr. Biden is under intense political pressure to act, and after his news conference some advocates accused Mr. Becerra of sounding tepid. Some Democrats, such as Senator Elizabeth Warren of Massachusetts and Representative \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2 , have been pushing the Biden administration to explore the prospect of building \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics on federal land and paying for people from out of state to travel there for the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Those were not among the measures that Mr. Becerra announced. Instead, he said that at Mr. Biden's direction he had instructed his agency to take steps such as making sure that federal insurance programs cover medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in cases of rape or incest or when the life of the mother is at risk. Although the Hyde Amendment bars taxpayer funding for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it includes exceptions for those three instances.\par \par \uc2 ''We can't meet scorched earth with milquetoast,'' said Andrea Miller, president of the National Institute for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Health\b0 \highlight\uc2 , an advocacy organization. ''I am not asking for scorched earth, but I am saying you need to be willing to stop drawing within the lines. You need to be willing to take some risks.''\par \par \uc2 In addition to setting up \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics on federal lands, Ms. Miller said the administration should figure out ways to support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics that are on the verge of closing, perhaps by repurposing them into logistical hubs to help women who need to cross state lines. About half the states are expected to allow bans or other limits on the procedure to take effect in the wake of the ruling, or already have.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Miller acknowledged that the administration does have limitations, and said she sympathized with Biden officials. But the nation is in a crisis, she said, adding, ''Why not push the envelope?''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Becerra said his agency would work with the Justice Department to ensure that women have access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills -- a pair of two different drugs, taken 24 to 48 hours apart and authorized for the first 10 weeks of pregnancy -- in places where state law conflicts with the judgment of the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 , which has approved the drugs for use and determined that they are safe and effective.\par \par \uc2 The secretary did not go into detail. But in December, the F.D.A. approved a regulation allowing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to be prescribed during telemedicine visits and distributed by mail. Some advocates also want the F.D.A. to declare that its regulations pre-empt state laws banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- a move that the Justice Department might have to defend in court.\par \par \uc2 It will also require hospital emergency rooms to comply with a federal law mandating that they stabilize patients experiencing a medical emergency -- including by performing abortions, if necessary. And the agency will take steps to ensure that patients' records are private, to keep state or local officials from identifying women who have had abortions.\par \par \uc2 In sounding a note of caution about what the administration can and cannot do, Mr. Becerra said there were still complex legal issues it needed to sort out to ensure it does not violate the court's ruling in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization.\par \par \uc2 ''It was a long decision and it did upend 50 years of precedent, and so you want to make sure that what you do is within the confines of the law,'' Mr. Becerra said. ''We're not interested in going rogue.''\par \par \uc2 He called the ruling ''despicable,'' and at one point said he wanted to offer ''my apologies'' that the administration cannot do more.\par \par \uc2 The administration has studied, but remains skeptical about, the idea of allowing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics on federal enclaves like military bases and national parks -- where state prosecutors lack jurisdiction -- in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now or will be a crime.\par \par \uc2 The White House press secretary, \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2 , dismissed the idea on Tuesday, telling reporters aboard Air Force One that it could have ''dangerous ramifications'' for women and doctors.\par \par \uc2 The problem, according to officials familiar with internal deliberations, is that the federal government could not ensure that doctors who are not federal employees performing official duties -- and potentially patients -- would not be at risk of prosecution.\par \par \uc2 That is because the Justice Department has the authority to prosecute certain state crimes. If a Republican were to win the presidency in 2024, his or her Justice Department could charge people with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related crimes dating back to 2022, because the statute of limitations would not have run out. States could strip doctors of their medical licenses. And state prosecutors could try to charge people with related conduct that took place outside the enclave -- like that of helping women get there -- under a theory of aiding and abetting or conspiracy.\par \par \uc2 Offering financial help to women to cross state lines to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  could also be problematic for the administration, because it might violate the Hyde Amendment, which bars federal funds from being used to pay for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  except in cases of rape, incest or where the life of the mother is at risk.\par \par \uc2 When asked on Tuesday if the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  might provide such financial help, Mr. Becerra said that once officials know ''exactly what we believe we are able to do, and have the money to do, we will let you know.'' He added, ''But until then, what I could simply say to you is every option is on the table.''\par \par \uc2 In the wake of Friday's ruling, members of Mr. Biden's own party have become increasingly vocal in demanding that he take action. On Saturday, more than 30 Democratic members of the Senate sent him a letter telling him there was ''no time to waste'' and pressing him to use the ''full force of the federal government'' to protect access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care.\par \par \uc2 ''Now is the time for bold action to protect the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' they wrote, adding, ''You have the power to fight back and lead a national response to this devastating decision.''\par \par \uc2 One area where the administration can act is in ensuring that women have access to emergency contraception -- including so-called morning-after pills, like Plan B -- and intrauterine devices. Both are common methods of contraception, but \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents regard them as ''abortifacients'' and have tried in some states to restrict access to them.\par \par \uc2 Some family-planning clinics in states that are banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  say their supplies of Plan B are now running short, because women -- fearful that the pills will be outlawed -- are stocking up. Hailey Kramer, a nurse practitioner at Tri-Rivers Family Planning in Rolla, Mo., said on Monday that the clinic's supplier is grappling with soaring demand and the pills have been back-ordered since a draft of the opinion overturning Roe was leaked last month.\par \par \uc2 Missouri is one of 13 states that had ''trigger'' laws banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after Roe was overturned; Mr. Becerra was visiting a Planned Parenthood clinic in the state when \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  suddenly became illegal there. Missouri is also one of four states that has excluded Planned Parenthood, a major provider of birth control, from Medicaid family planning programs that reimburse for such services.\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood has said the move violates federal law. Mr. Becerra said on Tuesday that he had directed the Centers for Medicaid and Medicare Services to ''make clear that family planning providers are able to participate in the Medicaid program.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220629ei6t0003u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
