\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Court Wins and Losses in States Are Defining the Shifting  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Landscape\b0\par\par\uc2 By Neelam Bohra and Shawn Hubler \par \uc2 1,038 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 18\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 An absolute \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban that Texas wrote before Roe v. Wade is on hold while a long-ago ban in Wisconsin reaches the courts. A previously blocked six-week ban in Tennessee has taken effect.\par \par \uc2 A state-by-state legal battle in the wake of the Supreme Court's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ruling widened on Tuesday as a judge in Texas temporarily blocked a statewide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, a federal court let Tennessee tighten existing restrictions, Iowa Republicans said they would ask courts to clear the way for a ban from the moment fetal cardiac activity could be detected, and Wisconsin Democrats announced they were filing suit to halt the resurrection of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws dating to the 19th century.\par \par \uc2 The actions came as Americans absorbed last week's watershed decision to reverse Roe v. Wade, ending a half-century-old constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Since then, roughly half of states have sought to outlaw or considerably restrict the procedure while others have sought to bolster \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 A blizzard of litigation has ensued, mostly from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups seeking to block new bans with the argument that they are vague or violate state constitutions, or to prevent old bans from going into effect by arguing that they are antiquated. On Monday, advocates persuaded judges in Louisiana and Utah to temporarily block enforcement of so-called trigger laws that were timed to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as soon as the Supreme Court reversed Roe. Similar lawsuits are pending in Arizona, Kentucky, Mississippi and Idaho.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday's developments focused on older \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions that were not explicitly tied to the ruling but suddenly had new meaning as a result of it.\par \par \uc2 In Tennessee, the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit lifted a nearly two-year-old injunction that had blocked an attempt to ban abortions after about the sixth week of pregnancy. In Texas, a Harris County judge prevented the automatic resurrection of a 1925 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in that state after clinics argued that it had been repealed ''by implication'' after Roe and that it conflicts with a trigger law slated to take effect in a matter of weeks.\par \par \uc2 In Iowa, Gov. \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2  and legislative leaders said they would ask a state court to reinstate a previously blocked law -- similar to Tennessee's -- that bans abortions as soon as fetal cardiac activity can be detected. The governor said that outside counsel, rather than Attorney General \uc2 Tom Miller\uc2 , would represent the state pro bono. Mr. Miller, a Democrat, said on Tuesday that he could not ''zealously'' defend the law, which he felt would undermine women's protections and rights, and was withdrawing his office from the case.\par \par \uc2 In Wisconsin, Gov. \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2  authorized his attorney general to sue the state's Legislature to remove a pre-Roe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban from the 1800s, going beyond his initial promise to grant clemency to doctors violating that same law.\par \par \uc2 The Texas and Tennessee decisions maintained access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in those states through about the sixth week of pregnancy -- a deadline that often has passed by the time many women realize they are pregnant.\par \par \uc2 ''This six-week ban forces people to carry pregnancies to term against their will and to face the resulting life-altering consequences,'' said Hedy Weinberg, the executive director of A.C.L.U. of Tennessee, one of the plaintiffs that two years ago fought Tennessee's effort to impose a six-week limit.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates in Texas did welcome the temporary halt of a complete ban that would have punished those who perform abortions with imprisonment for two to five years.\par \par \uc2 The law had gone unenforced after the Supreme Court ruled in Roe v. Wade, in 1973, that women had a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but it had never been taken off the books. \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 , the Texas attorney general, had contended that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers could now be prosecuted under it.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday's ruling means that abortions can resume in Texas in the near term, to the extent that the law allows them. Even before last week's Supreme Court decision, the state permitted abortions only up to about six weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 ''The judge's decision is wrong,'' Mr. Paxton tweeted on Tuesday. ''I'm appealing. I'll ensure we have all the legal tools to keep TX pro-life!''\par \par \uc2 The restraining order is ''very narrow,'' Kim Schwartz, a spokeswoman for the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group Texas Right to Life, said. The protection applies only to the providers listed in the lawsuit and does not account for all Texas laws that could be grounds for prosecuting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The American Civil Liberties Union\uc2  and health care providers had sought the delay, arguing that the old law had not been enforced in nearly a half-century. The judge set a hearing for July 12 to consider a more permanent injunction.\par \par \uc2 ''We are contacting the patients on our waiting lists and we will resume services in our four Texas clinics and help as many people as soon as possible,'' \uc2 Amy Hagstrom Miller\uc2 , chief executive of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider Whole Woman's Health, said in a statement. She added that because the clinics must still comply with Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws currently in place, access was ''heavily restricted.''\par \par \uc2 Andrea Gallegos, executive administrator of Alamo Women's Reproductive Services, said the San Antonio-based clinic planned to take as many patients as possible over the next few days in anticipation that the state would appeal the ruling.\par \par \uc2 Texas also has the trigger ban, written before the court ruling. That ban, which makes exceptions to save the life of the mother or prevent ''substantial impairment of major bodily function,'' is set to take effect in a few weeks.\par \par \uc2 After the trigger ban takes effect, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Texas will be outlawed from the point of fertilization. Women who get abortions will not be prosecuted, but doctors who perform the procedure will face potential sentences of up to life in prison or fines of up to $100,000.\par \par \uc2 Tory Mills leading a Bans Off Our Bodies rally in Knoxville, Tenn. (PHOTOGRAPH BY JESSICA TEZAK FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220629ei6t0003j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
