\par\par \uc2 Metropolitan Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 At Jan. 6 Hearings, Reliving History\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ian Prasad Philbrick \par \uc2 1,780 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 For many people on Capitol Hill, the Jan. 6 hearings are personal.\par \par \uc2 The House Jan. 6 committee's hearings have revealed unseen footage, unheard testimony and new details about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's efforts to overturn the 2020 presidential election. They've also stirred painful memories for those who experienced the attack firsthand. I asked my colleague Emily Cochrane, who's covered Congress since 2018 and was inside the Capitol on Jan. 6, about how the hearings have landed.\par \par \uc2 How did Jan. 6 change Capitol Hill?\par \par \uc2 The Capitol is like a little city. It doesn't matter if you're a lawmaker, a staff member, a police officer, a reporter, someone who works in the cafeteria or the person who delivers the mail: You end up spending a good portion of your life there. And to see it breached in that manner, to see this mob come in, to see the violence, to see them disrespect a place you've come to respect, is difficult. A lot of people are wrestling with that as these hearings go on, publicly and privately.\par \par \uc2 How do these hearings differ from others you've covered?\par \par \uc2 Obviously, the substance is extraordinary. But they're also produced in a way that congressional hearings normally aren't: the videos, the tightly worded statements, the teasers of what's to come. They're structured like television episodes.\par \par \uc2 They also present much more of a seamless narrative because the Republicans aren't participating, with the exception of two handpicked by Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 . Most congressional hearings are so polarized, with questions designed to tease out political points rather than information. Here, there's none of that partisan bickering. They're just dropping new information and speeding right along.\par \par \uc2 We've all seen big congressional hearings on TV. What's it like to be in the room?\par \par \uc2 The hearings are generally somber. The videos of the attack were hard to watch during the first hearing, particularly for people who were in the House chamber on Jan. 6. The hearing I attended, about the behind-the-scenes efforts to tell Trump he'd lost the election, wasn't as visceral, but when \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  made the reference to ''an apparently inebriated \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 ,'' there was some chuckling.\par \par \uc2 During breaks, particularly after emotional testimony, people who are in the back watching often make a point of thanking the witnesses and checking in with each other.\par \par \uc2 Who's been there watching?\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers, police officers, aides, people who want to witness history, essentially, or who experienced the riot personally. The House chaplain has been there regularly. There's the ''Gallery Group'' -- they're Democratic House members who were trapped in the upper gallery of the House chamber during the attack. At least a couple have been at every hearing. Their presence is a reminder of how personal this is.\par \par \uc2 You hid with them during the attack, right?\par \par \uc2 I was on the opposite side of the chamber. At one point, I was behind a chair with other reporters because they had stopped the evacuation, and I wasn't sure if the chamber was going to be breached. We could see rioters on the other side of the door.\par \par \uc2 Eventually, \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2  resumed the evacuation. I'm still not sure why -- I think they felt they had stopped enough rioters for lawmakers to leave safely -- but all of a sudden, the people in front of me started moving again, climbing over chairs and banisters, so I did the same.\par \par \uc2 We were eventually able to leave the chamber, and, as we did, we merged into one evacuation line with the lawmakers across from us.\par \par \uc2 It must be difficult to watch videos and hear testimony that dredge up memories of the attack.\par \par \uc2 The hearings are bringing many people back to the rawness of the day. People have found coping mechanisms -- they've talked to therapists, they've checked in with others. The Gallery Group lawmakers stay in touch. An informal Capitol Hill support group started meeting more frequently. I've had people ask me how I'm doing, and I've reached out to a couple others. These aren't the easiest hearings to cover, but then you compartmentalize and do your job.\par \par \uc2 You and your colleagues have written about how the attack prompted a spike in threats against lawmakers, led some congressional staffers to quit and caused others to push for a union . How did it bring about these big changes?\par \par \uc2 Capitol Hill has never been an easy place to work. It's unpredictable. The hours are long. The workload is intense. When you layer on the pandemic, the mad dash to get legislation through and everything that happened on the 6th, they all put the jobs in perspective for lawmakers and their aides. For them, there are now questions like, do you want to stay on Capitol Hill where you had this traumatic experience? Can you work with the Republicans in Congress who have downplayed what happened that day?\par \par \uc2 Congressional staffers keep Capitol Hill running. When someone picks up a phone to threaten a lawmaker, the person on the other end is not the lawmaker. It's a staff member, probably a junior staff member, sitting on the phone, listening to threats and reporting them to police. It's not a part of the job you sign up for. Unionization has been batted around for a while, but Jan. 6 helped push it to the forefront. People are more open to it.\par \par \uc2 More about Emily: She grew up in Miami and studied journalism at the \uc2 University of Florida\uc2 . She lives in Washington, where she likes to listen to musicals and take her two majestic Siberian cats, Hercules and Yuzu , on walks.\par \par \uc2 For more\par \par \uc2 The Jan. 6 committee has redefined congressional hearings.\par \par \uc2 Join Times reporters for a live online event with Representative Jamie Raskin, a member of the Jan. 6 committee, tomorrow at 11 a.m.\par \par \uc2 NEWS\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Ruling\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers began canceling appointments out of fear of prosecution a day after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Demand for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills quickly soared.\par \par \uc2 Conservative Christians celebrated the decision as a political and spiritual victory.\par \par \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  laid out a strategy decades ago to overturn Roe.\par \par \uc2 The beginning of the end of Roe came 12 years ago.\par \par \uc2 War in Ukraine\par \par \uc2 Russian forces fired missiles across Ukraine in one of their most widespread aerial assaults in weeks.\par \par \uc2 American and allied commandos and spies are providing weapons and training to Ukrainian troops.\par \par \uc2 During a break from the war, a Ukrainian couple got married in 11 minutes.\par \par \uc2 Other Big Stories\par \par \uc2 President Biden signed the gun safety bill into law.\par \par \uc2 A devastating earthquake was the latest blow to one of Afghanistan's poorest regions.\par \par \uc2 A judge's order widened the scope of deportations from the U.S., leaving millions of people vulnerable to removal.\par \par \uc2 FROM OPINION\par \par \uc2 The end of Roe is the result of men gaining power by trading away women's rights, Maureen Dowd writes.\par \par \uc2 You might have forgotten how to vacation, Jenni Avins writes. That's OK.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm not completely free'': Isvett Verde spoke to participants of the 10-year-old DACA program that protects undocumented immigrants from deportation.\par \par \uc2 The Sunday question: With Roe gone, what should activists on both sides do now?\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists should appeal to political moderates and covertly help women get abortions, says The Atlantic's Helen Lewis. The anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement should seek policies that support families so women can flourish without \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Karen Swallow Prior argues in The Times.\par \par \uc2 MORNING READS\par \par \uc2 Eastern Shore pilgrimage: Climate change is quickly remaking the terrain that was the site of Harriet Tubman's earliest exploits.\par \par \uc2 Sleep debt: Late nights and early mornings may cause lasting damage to your brain.\par \par \uc2 A Times classic: The mysterious interior world of exercise.\par \par \uc2 Sunday routine: The artistic director of the Moth finds inspiration in ''American Ninja Warrior.''\par \par \uc2 Advice from Wirecutter: This $28 garden tool cut our weeding time in half.\par \par \uc2 BOOKS\par \par \uc2 Literary guide: Read your way through Stockholm.\par \par \uc2 By the Book: Patrick Radden Keefe insists ''The Hard Sell'' is like a Scorsese film.\par \par \uc2 Our editors' picks: Ada Calhoun's new memoir, ''Also a Poet,'' and nine other books.\par \par \uc2 Times best sellers: Ibram X. Kendi is a best seller in hardcover nonfiction, combined nonfiction and children's picture books. See all our lists here.\par \par \uc2 The Book Review podcast: Ed Yong discusses his new book, ''An Immense World.''\par \par \uc2 THE SUNDAY TIMES MAGAZINE\par \par \uc2 On the cover: Inside the push to diversify the book industry.\par \par \uc2 Advice from Judge John Hodgman: A woman's fianc\u233\'00\'E9 wants to drag her along to a Phish show. Must she go?\par \par \uc2 Tip: How to imprint ducklings.\par \par \uc2 Eat: A crispy won-ton chicken salad inspired by a shuttered all-you-can-eat buffet.\par \par \uc2 Read the full issue.\par \par \uc2 THE WEEK AHEAD\par \par \uc2 What to Watch For\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court will soon rule on the final cases of its term, including a major climate decision.\par \par \uc2 President Biden will meet with other Group of 7 leaders in Germany early this week, then travel to Spain for a \uc2 NATO\uc2  summit.\par \par \uc2 Colorado, Illinois, New York, Oklahoma and Utah will hold primary elections on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Ghislaine Maxwell, the former companion to the disgraced financier Jeffrey Epstein, will be sentenced on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 White House aides are weighing whether to ban new oil and gas drilling off U.S. coasts. A decision was promised by Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Broadway theaters will be allowed to drop their mask mandates on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Wimbledon begins tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 The Olympic Committee will vote today on the slate of contests for the 2026 games. Nordic combined is facing elimination.\par \par \uc2 What to Cook This Week\par \par \uc2 ''Who doesn't like having simple dishes and brilliant techniques at their fingertips?,'' Emily Weinstein asks. She offers weeknight dinner options for beginner cooks and pros alike: broiled salmon with mustard and lemon, vegetable tofu curry or a crispy-edged quesadilla.\par \par \uc2 NOW TIME TO PLAY\par \par \uc2 Here's a clue from the Sunday crossword:\par \par \uc2 90 Across: It's a small world\par \par \uc2 Take the news quiz to see how well you followed the week's headlines.\par \par \uc2 Here's today's Spelling Bee. Here's today's Wordle. After, use our bot to get better.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for spending part of your weekend with The Times. -- Ian\par \par \uc2 Claire Moses, Tom Wright-Piersanti and Ashley Wu contributed to The Morning. You can reach the team at themorning@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Sign up here to get this newsletter in your inbox .\par \par \uc2 Emily Cochrane, in glasses, with other reporters and Senator John Cornyn of Texas on June 7. (PHOTOGRAPH BY JASON ANDREW FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220629ei6t0002z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
