\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 What Law Could the Court's Conservative Bloc Revisit Next?\b0\par\par\uc2 By David Leonhardt \par \uc2 1,837 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Five Republican-appointed justices are ambitious and impatient to change American law.\par \par \uc2 My colleague Adam Liptak, who covers the Supreme Court, describes the five Republican-appointed justices besides Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  as ''an impatient, ambitious majority.''\par \par \uc2 They have largely rejected Roberts's more cautious approach of deciding cases narrowly and shifting the law slowly. The five instead prefer to set American law as they believe it should be set, even when they must overrule longstanding precedent. To do otherwise, they believe, is dishonest.\par \par \uc2 After the court overturned Roe v. Wade on Friday, one obvious question was: What other legal changes might soon be coming? Initial attention has focused on the possibility that the court may soon restrict L.G.B.T. rights, contraception access or interracial marriage. All those issues involve some of the same logic that led to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  decision, as both Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  and the three liberal justices pointed out in their writings accompanying the decision.\par \par \uc2 But those are not actually the hot-button issues that the court is likely to consider next. In today's newsletter, I want to focus on the divisive decisions that are more likely to come soon. One of those rulings could happen today; the court is scheduled to announce some of its final rulings of the term shortly after 10 a.m. Eastern.\par \par \uc2 Kavanaugh's position\par \par \uc2 The first reason to doubt that the court is on the verge of overturning the constitutional right to same-sex marriage, interracial marriage or contraception access comes straight from Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ruling. In a separate concurrence explaining his vote, Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  -- one of the impatient, ambitious five -- explicitly signaled that those other rights were safe.\par \par \uc2 In his 12-page concurrence, Kavanaugh wrote that he wanted to address ''how this decision will affect other precedents involving issues such as contraception and marriage.'' He then listed four cases dealing with those issues, including the 2015 ruling establishing a right to same-sex marriage. ''Overruling Roe does not mean the overruling of those precedents, and does not threaten or cast doubt on those precedents,'' Kavanaugh explained.\par \par \uc2 Unless Kavanaugh changes his mind -- or Roberts decides to overturn those precedents -- there is no majority to do so. For now, only Thomas has said that he favors revisiting the earlier cases. ''I don't think there are five votes for overturning any of those decisions,'' Adam Liptak said on a weekend episode of ''The Daily.''\par \par \uc2 The second reason to think that other divisive issues will come first is that the court has already announced many of the cases it will hear next year. They tend to involve other topics -- namely affirmative action, election laws and business regulation.\par \par \uc2 Affirmative action\par \par \uc2 It's an issue that seems likely to define the court's next term in the way that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  did this term. The court has agreed to hear two cases, one challenging the use of race in admissions at a public university (the University of North Carolina) and one at a private university (Harvard).\par \par \uc2 I have been writing about this subject for the past two decades, and university officials I've spoken with are far more worried that the court will outlaw their current admissions approach than they were during the run-up to previous Supreme Court cases. If that happens, the number of Black students at selective colleges seems especially likely to decline.\par \par \uc2 The core argument for affirmative action is simple: In a society where racism and racial inequities remain defining problems, ignoring race in admissions or hiring decisions is fundamentally unfair.\par \par \uc2 Yet affirmative action -- at least as it has typically been practiced in the U.S. -- tends to be unpopular. When the policy appears on the ballot in state referendums, it usually loses, even in liberal states like California. (Poll questions, depending on their wording, point in contradictory directions.)\par \par \uc2 One problem may be that traditional affirmative action has put nearly all of its focus on race, with little to no weight on economic class. That approach has probably hurt the policy's support among many white, Asian and even Latino voters. Its thin popular support, in turn, would make it easier for conservative justices to ban a policy they have long opposed.\par \par \uc2 ''The way to stop discrimination on the basis of race is to stop discriminating on the basis of race,'' Roberts once wrote.\par \par \uc2 Is there any chance the court will stop short of outlawing affirmative action? Sure. Many corporate executives and military leaders support the policy, and they could plausibly sway the justices. But most court watchers consider that outcome improbable.\par \par \uc2 In a 2003 ruling upholding affirmative action, Justice \uc2 Sandra Day O'Connor\uc2  -- a more moderate Republican appointee -- suggested that she thought the policy might no longer be necessary ''25 years from now.'' If the court scraps the policy in 2023, the current impatient, ambitious majority would be only five years ahead of O'Connor's timetable.\par \par \uc2 And two more\par \par \uc2 Two other contentious subjects on the court's docket are election law and business regulation. On both, the court -- including Roberts -- has recently leaned strongly to the political right.\par \par \uc2 Election laws. The court has already agreed to hear a case about whether Alabama can draw a congressional map that packs many Black voters into a single congressional district, effectively diluting their political power. About 27 percent of Alabama's residents are Black, and the state has seven House districts.\par \par \uc2 The court may also decide to hear a case that could limit the ability of state courts to review how state legislatures draw districts and otherwise oversee elections. Adam Liptak recently wrote an article that explains why the issue is so important -- especially when many Republican legislators have signaled a willingness to overturn election results.\par \par \uc2 Business regulation. Even before President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's three appointees shifted the court to the right, it tended to take a laissez-faire approach, limiting Congress's ability to regulate corporate behavior. The current court may go even further, especially on climate policy, and rule that federal agencies cannot limit pollution unless Congress has given them specific authority to do so.\par \par \uc2 The court will hear one case involving the Clean Water Act in October and will likely issue a ruling involving the E.P.A. this week. I'll go into more detail on this subject once that ruling is announced.\par \par \uc2 More on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists are pushing more states to adopt bans and prohibit traveling for abortions, while opponents have pledged to fight so-called trigger laws in court.\par \par \uc2 The Jackson Women's Health Organization, the only clinic in Mississippi, was at the center of the case that overturned Roe. It will close in a few days.\par \par \uc2 In Opinion, Pamela Paul writes that America's most exceptional trait is its lack of mercy. And Ross Douthat argues that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents have a longer-term battle to win.\par \par \uc2 THE LATEST NEWS\par \par \uc2 War in Ukraine\par \par \uc2 Russian forces rained missiles on Kyiv for the first time in weeks, shattering the Ukrainian capital's sense of security.\par \par \uc2 Leaders of \uc2 G7\uc2  nations are close to an agreement to impose price caps on Russian oil, among other new economic penalties, Biden administration officials said today.\par \par \uc2 The war has hampered the fight against climate change as countries focus on finding fossil fuels to make up for lost Russian oil.\par \par \uc2 Primary Elections\par \par \uc2 In New York, the Democratic candidates for governor are focusing on two Supreme Court rulings -- on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and guns -- ahead of Tuesday's primary.\par \par \uc2 A far-right candidate could win the Republican primary for Illinois governor, thanks to a Trump-loving base -- and Democrats' cash.\par \par \uc2 Other Big Stories\par \par \uc2 In China, police technology uses vast surveillance data to predict crime before it happens, targeting people whose behavior an algorithm deems suspicious.\par \par \uc2 At a meeting of the Proud Boys before Jan. 6, the far-right group's leaders stressed obeying police lines and keeping away from ordinary protesters. Those instructions were later ignored.\par \par \uc2 Fifty years ago, girls' soccer became an easy way to comply with the new Title IX law. Now the U.S. is a women's soccer powerhouse.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Colorado Avalanche\uc2  won their first Stanley Cup since 2001, ending the \uc2 Tampa Bay Lightning\uc2 's bid for a third consecutive title.\par \par \uc2 Opinions\par \par \uc2 China's economic expansion is winning over small countries that the U.S. ignores, writes Dorothy Wickham, a journalist from the Solomon Islands.\par \par \uc2 Gail Collins and Bret Stephens discuss last week's Supreme Court decisions.\par \par \uc2 MORNING READS\par \par \uc2 Mr. Happy Face: This Chihuahua mix with a mohawk was crowned World's Ugliest Dog.\par \par \uc2 Supercars: Would you buy an electric Ferrari?\par \par \uc2 Quiz time: The average score on our latest news quiz was 8.9. Try to beat it.\par \par \uc2 A Times classic: Where decision fatigue comes from.\par \par \uc2 Advice from Wirecutter: How to apply sunscreen for maximum effect.\par \par \uc2 Lives Lived: Baxter Black was America's cowboy poet (some dubbed him the ''Poet Lariat''), celebrating life on the range in books, speeches and frequent NPR appearances. Black died at 77.\par \par \uc2 ARTS AND IDEAS\par \par \uc2 The brands on your screen\par \par \uc2 Advertising has become easier to avoid, thanks to commercial-free streaming services, skippable \uc2 YouTube\uc2  ads and more. Companies and marketers are responding with an increase in product placement.\par \par \uc2 When done well, a bit of product placement can seem natural; after all, real kitchens are stocked with branded products. But the repeated appearance of certain items can start to look odd. Why is it that so many TV characters are drinking that one blue-capped water? And why do they keep referring to the same real estate app?\par \par \uc2 Read more about the trend -- including examples from your favorite shows.\par \par \uc2 PLAY, WATCH, EAT\par \par \uc2 What to Cook\par \par \uc2 This recipe for roasted vegetables is easy to make, but good enough to become a go-to when fresh veggies are around.\par \par \uc2 The Ros\u233\'00\'E9 Lifestyle\par \par \uc2 An owner of W\u246\'00\'F6lffer Estate has turned pink wine into a symbol of the Hamptons life that she epitomizes.\par \par \uc2 World Through a Lens\par \par \uc2 A photographer documents the ornamental birdhouses of India, known as chabutras.\par \par \uc2 Now Time to Play\par \par \uc2 The pangram from yesterday's Spelling Bee was bilingual. Here is today's puzzle.\par \par \uc2 Here's today's Mini Crossword, and a clue: Cheesy dip (5 letters).\par \par \uc2 And here's today's Wordle. After, use our bot to get better.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for spending part of your morning with The Times. See you tomorrow. -- David\par \par \uc2 P.S. Megha Rajagopalan and Justin Scheck are joining The Times's international investigations team.\par \par \uc2 Here's today's front page.\par \par \uc2 ''The Daily'' is about the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ruling. ''Sway'' features Andrew Ross Sorkin.\par \par \uc2 Ian Prasad Philbrick, Tom Wright-Piersanti, Ashley Wu and Chris Stanford contributed to The Morning. You can reach the team at themorning@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Sign up here to get this newsletter in your inbox .\par \par \uc2 Five conservative justices have largely rejected the cautious approach of Chief Justice John Roberts. (PHOTOGRAPH BY SAMUEL CORUM FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220628ei6s0004m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
