\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 The Early Days After Roe Were Strikingly Similar To the Struggles of Today\b0\par\par\uc2 By Fahima Haque \par \uc2 1,355 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Three women lived through a time without Roe, and the early years after it. Their experiences working for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights mimic the fight that continues today.\par \par \uc2 Catherine Starr was 17 when she attended her first demonstration to push for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights outside City Hall in St. Louis. It was May 24, 1973. Just a few months earlier, the Supreme Court had ruled on Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Women had gained a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but America would continue arguing about it for another 50 years. The Supreme Court's decision last week to overturn Roe has once again thrust the country into turbulence that feels all too familiar to those who lived it the first time around. Three women on the front lines of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights movement before Roe was the law and in the early years after the ruling told their stories to \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 When Ms. Starr protested that day in St. Louis, she was joined by her mother and grandmother. The three generations of women rallied together to protest Mayor John Poelker's forbidding of city hospitals to perform abortions.\par \par \uc2 In the early days after Roe, legal access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was still difficult or unavailable in many states. It was only the year before, in 1972, that unmarried men and women had been granted the right to access birth control.\par \par \uc2 The Roe decision had come too late for Ms. Starr. A year earlier, at 16, she was pregnant. Without the option of a safe, legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , she said, she gave birth to a baby boy and then gave him up for adoption.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Starr went to the rally because she ''wanted to be able to make sure that the next little girl that gets pregnant has an option,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 ''Giving up a child, it's like losing one to death but in a way it's worse because you don't know anything about the child,'' Ms. Starr, now 66, said. ''I had a little boy, and years would go by and I'd sit back and wonder if he is even still alive, is he happy, is he healthy?''\par \par \uc2 About 10 years ago, Ms. Starr's son found her and they reconnected. The conversation was initially awkward, she said, but ultimately therapeutic. Her son told her that he was grateful for her decision and that he had a pretty good life. He also told her that he is in favor of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, she said, a pleasant surprise for her.\par \par \uc2 ''Most kids would probably not want to know that their mother thought about aborting them,'' she said. ''But I did. I was 15 when I was pregnant, and 16 when I had him and I was awfully young.''\par \par \uc2 ''He asked me, 'You could have gotten an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , why didn't you do it?' And I said, 'Well, actually, I couldn't have, it wasn't legal at the time,''' she continued. ''I said I didn't want to get an illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and once I started feeling him in there, I just couldn't do it.''\par \par \uc2 The Clinic Worker\par \par \uc2 Susan Bilyeu was counseling a patient at an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic when she heard screaming. When she opened the door, she saw flames, and a nursing assistant on the floor, holding her eyes.\par \par \uc2 An arsonist had attacked the Concerned Women's Clinic in Cleveland on Feb. 15, 1978, a busy Saturday.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Bilyeu, who was 25 at the time, was swept up in an escalation of violence around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics in the late 1970s. Legal challenges to thwart \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept failing. People would chain themselves to the doors of clinics and shout at women and staff members as they entered the facilities. ''It was really quite nasty,'' said Karissa Haugeberg, assistant professor of history at Tulane University.\par \par \uc2 At Ms. Bilyeu's clinic, a man posing as a delivery worker had splashed gasoline on the assistant's face and set the building on fire. Ms. Bilyeu helped carry the injured worker out of the burning building. There was also a 16-year-old in the midst of having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . They called an ambulance and took her to the women's hospital two blocks away.\par \par \uc2 ''Nobody changed their mind about having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that day,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Bilyeu said she felt fundamentally connected to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights movement because of the stories her mother, born in 1917, told her, including about her aunt nearly dying from an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''I got involved because I knew people who were struggling,'' she said. ''I'm not pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , I'm pro-choice. No one should be forced to have a child, and I certainly don't want someone to die from it.''\par \par \uc2 The Organizer\par \par \uc2 Loretta J. Ross grew up in a conservative household in the 1960s. She got pregnant at 14 after her cousin raped her. Her only choice at that time, she said, was to raise the child herself, or give him up for adoption. She gave birth to her son in 1969, and kept him.\par \par \uc2 The experience formed Ms. Ross, now a professor at \uc2 Smith College\uc2 , as an activist and a Black feminist, she said.\par \par \uc2 ''I went from being a scared teenager to being an active teenage mother,'' she said, ''so that had a definite impact on my consciousness and it separated me from the rest of the kids in school.''\par \par \uc2 She enrolled at \uc2 Howard University\uc2  in 1970. Washington, D.C., was in turmoil after the assassination of the Rev. Dr. \uc2 Martin Luther King Jr\uc2 . Ms. Ross was tear-gassed when she attended her first demonstration at 16. She also became pregnant again. Her older sister forged their mother's signature on the permission slip, but because Washington had legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1971, she was able to get one.\par \par \uc2 Yet for Ms. Ross and her fellow classmates, other issues were priorities, such as apartheid and gentrification. There was not a sense of urgency around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights for Ms. Ross, she said, until the Hyde Amendment passed in 1976, banning federal funding for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which disproportionately affected low-income women.\par \par \uc2 For Ms. Ross, her activism on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights dovetailed and sometimes complicated her political coming-of-age as a Black woman.\par \par \uc2 ''When I was with the Black Nationalist movement people, I actually felt more feminist than not,'' she said. ''I would call myself a Black Marxist feminist. But then when I was with white women, I was just like, 'I'm not a feminist like y'all are, so I don't want to use the word.'''\par \par \uc2 Concerns that Black women lacked a presence in the women's movement is what prompted Representative \uc2 Eleanor Holmes Norton\uc2 , the District of Columbia's nonvoting House delegate, to co-found the National Black Feminist Organization. Despite the group's forming in 1973, amid the backdrop of Roe, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not loom large in their conversations, she said.\par \par \uc2 Black women receive about one-third of the abortions in the United States, according to recent data from the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  research group that supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. But Ms. Ross, who was trying to help the \uc2 National Organization for Women\uc2  plan a women's rights march, said it was tough to get Black women's organizations involved because few wanted to engage with the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate.\par \par \uc2 For a second march in April 1989, which drew more than 600,000 people, Ms. Ross made a banner for women of color to gather around to make them visible.\par \par \uc2 Over the years, she held steadfast to one principle. ''I definitely was going to stand up for women's rights,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Catherine Starr, above right, marched with her mother and grandmother in 1973. Loretta J. Ross, speaking in 1979, left, was energized by the passage of the Hyde Amendment. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY BETTMAN COLLECTION/GETTY IMAGES; RICK REINHARD, VIA SOPHIA SMITH COLLECTION OF WOMEN'S HISTORY, SMITH COLLEGE SPECIAL COLLECTIONS);The Concerned Women's Clinic, in Cleveland, above, where Susan Bilyeu worked in 1978, was destroyed by a firebomb. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220628ei6s0004e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
