\par\par \uc2 MICHELLE GOLDBERG\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Lessons From the Triumph of the Anti- \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Movement\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michelle Goldberg \par \uc2 981 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 23\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In the grief-choked days since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, I've been haunted by a moment from the new documentary ''Battleground.'' Much of the film, which recently premiered at the Tribeca Film Festival, follows female leaders of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement, including Marjorie Dannenfelser, president of \uc2 Susan B. Anthony\uc2  Pro-Life America, and Kristan Hawkins, president of Students for Life of America. These aren't the people regularly standing outside of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics harassing patients. They are, rather, savvy lobbyists and organizers, and the documentary is in part a window into how they won.\par \par \uc2 The scene I keep revisiting features a Students for Life training session about ''how you can change minds about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  online,'' in which members of the group learned how to draw young pro-choice people into debate in comment threads. Hawkins said they'd had 105,000 conversations.\par \par \uc2 Cynthia Lowen, the director of ''Battleground,'' told me she was struck by the activists' ''strategy to get into environments and places, online and offline, where young, typically pro-choice people are,'' and to try to create ''doubts about their position.''\par \par \uc2 This is quite different from what I've seen in the pro-choice movement, where activists frequently act as if those who don't agree with them on everything aren't worth engaging with. (Last week, NARAL tweeted, ''If your feminism doesn't understand how anti-trans policies disproportionately impact BIPOC folks, particularly Black trans women and girls, it's not feminism.'') In the aftermath of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement's catastrophic victory, it's worth asking what we can learn from their tactics.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement hasn't convinced anywhere near a majority of Americans. Roe's death comes courtesy of three Supreme Court justices appointed by a president who lost the popular vote. According to a \uc2 CBS\uc2 /News \uc2 YouGov\uc2  poll taken after the ruling, 59 percent of Americans -- and 67 percent of women -- disapprove of it.\par \par \uc2 The Senate can't codify minimal \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  because of the filibuster, which gives a minority of conservatives veto power over much of national policymaking. In states like Wisconsin, legislatures are so gerrymandered that it will take more than a popular-vote majority to undo their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. The right pretends that ending Roe returns \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the democratic process, but Roe's demise was made possible by democracy's erosion.\par \par \uc2 That shouldn't blind us, however, to the success of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement, which has organized for almost 50 years to bring us to this moment. Those state-level gerrymanders didn't just happen. As The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  reported, they were made possible by the 2010 Republican wave, which reduced the number of state legislatures controlled by Democrats from 27 to 16. Republicans then used redistricting to cement their hold on power even as they passed a barrage of state laws meant to chip away at Roe.\par \par \uc2 The legal and political wings of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement were methodical, often biding their time until they had a friendly Supreme Court in place. As general counsel of the \uc2 National Right to Life Committee\uc2 , James Bopp opposed attempts to prohibit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  outright, fearing that their rejection would only strengthen Roe. Instead, as Irin Carmon reported in 2013, he focused on wedge issues like 20-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, grass-roots \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents have remained relentless, often drawing people in for reasons of sociability as much as ideology. In ''The Making of Pro-Life Activists,'' the sociologist Ziad W. Munson found that many activists had been ambivalent about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or even pro-choice, before being invited to a rally or meeting. The movement welcomed them, and the experience of activism converted them. Similarly, one of Lowen's interviewees said she wasn't opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until she tagged along to the March for Life with some college friends. She later went to work for Students for Life.\par \par \uc2 I fear that some \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights activists are learning the wrong lessons from their enemies' triumph, taking inspiration from the most confrontational anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  forces. A string of apparent arsons at anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis pregnancy centers mimics years of pro-life assaults on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics. (So far, mercifully, the arsons haven't caused any injuries.) Before Dr. Barnett Slepian, a Buffalo \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider, was assassinated in 1998, protesters followed his children to their grade school. Recently a shadowy pro-choice outfit called Ruth Sent Us hinted at doing something similar to the kids of Justice Amy Coney Barrett, tweeting about the school they attend.\par \par \uc2 Besides being immoral, these tactics suggest a misunderstanding of how the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement got to this point. Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  terrorism has been correlated with greater support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, harming the political campaign to reverse Roe. That campaign prevailed because of a movement that spent decades mastering the nuts and bolts of American politics, persisting despite years of failure and disappointment.\par \par \uc2 This doesn't just mean ''vote harder.'' It means contesting every level of power, all the time, including local elections, judicial selections and administrative rule-making. It means drawing people into a community that will make continuous struggle seem rewarding rather than depleting.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents have shoved us into a nightmare world of surveillance, coercion and medical desperation. They've also shown us the arduous path out of it.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTopinion) and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2  opponents celebrating at the Supreme Court on Friday. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Shuran Huang for The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220628ei6s00041\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
