\par\par \uc2 THE CONVERSATION\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 The Supreme Court's Fighting Words\b0\par\par\uc2 By Gail Collins and Bret Stephens \par \uc2 1,636 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 22\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Bret Stephens: Hi, Gail. We've had two monumental Supreme Court decisions over the last week, on guns and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Maybe it isn't a fair question, but which of them scares, dismays, enrages or makes you want to bang your head against the wall more?\par \par \uc2 Gail Collins: I feel totally traumatized by both of them -- even though, I admit, I was pretty much expecting everything that happened.\par \par \uc2 Bret: A line that's making the rounds: It's like knowing daylight saving time is coming and setting your clock back 50 years.\par \par \uc2 Gail: Maybe feeling most intense about the gun decision because I know how many terrific colleagues are rallying against the one on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Here we are in New York, which is, relatively speaking, a very safe city, thanks in good part to the local gun laws we've observed. But conservative attachment to law and order disappears whenever it gets in the way of right-wing attachment to their guns.\par \par \uc2 Sorry, I'm ranting. Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 Bret: You aren't ranting at all. Or rather, rant away.\par \par \uc2 Gail: OK, if I've got permission to rant, I'll get to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  decision after all. Just reading \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 's preening concurrence about how this is just the first step -- next targets: same-sex marriage and women's overall right to purchase contraceptives.\par \par \uc2 Bret: Give him a point for honesty, which is more than can be said for \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Gail: I wrote a while back wondering if Texas' anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law from last September would be the first step in a war against every form of birth control more efficient than the rhythm method, but did not expect the idea to pop up in a Supreme Court opinion.\par \par \uc2 I know that was just from Thomas's comments, but it's so important to see which way this kind of thinking is pointing. A woman's power to decide whether she wants to be pregnant was totally key to our liberation. Take it away and you're back to a time when women were expected to quit work after they got married because there was no effective way of knowing when they'd get pregnant. And to totally avoid sex if they were single because of the fear that they'd get pregnant and ruin their reputations and careers.\par \par \uc2 OK. Time for you to get in here.\par \par \uc2 Bret: Agree with everything you say. For me, the word that comes to mind is arrogance. Supreme arrogance.\par \par \uc2 In the gun decision, the court is denying New York State the normal democratic right to decide for itself how it should go about ensuring domestic tranquillity, which is the basic function of government. In the Mississippi \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case, the court is doing something closer to the opposite: giving a state government the unfettered ability to erase an individual right that, until last Friday, had been upheld by the court for nearly five decades.\par \par \uc2 That said, I'm probably not as gloomy about this as you are. There might even be some positives.\par \par \uc2 Gail: Oh, gosh, tell me what -- quick.\par \par \uc2 Bret: Maybe this is all lipstick on the proverbial pig, but three things.\par \par \uc2 Politically, last week was a very good one for Democrats. The national conversation suddenly turned from the price of goods to priceless goods: personal safety and choice. There's now a fighting chance that Democrats will hold the Senate in November. They might also start winning back some of the seats they lost in state legislatures, where the future of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights will be decided.\par \par \uc2 Gail: Republican Party, this is how far you've fallen: Bret Stephens is rooting for Democrats to take over all the legislatures.\par \par \uc2 Bret: So long as they are pro-business, pro-police, pro-Israel and pro-charter schools. Mike Bloomberg, in other words.\par \par \uc2 My second point of optimism is that, medically speaking, we're in a different world from 1972 or 1973. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills that work till the 10th week of pregnancy are now widely available and, as of December, can be obtained by mail. Far from an ideal solution, I know, but it goes some way toward mitigating the effects of the court's decision.\par \par \uc2 Gail: Waiting for the Texas legislature to take on the pharmacies next. I'm really not long-term confident, but you're right for now. And that's one of the reasons the gun decision made me even crazier.\par \par \uc2 Bret: Final glimmer of optimism: New York and the other states whose gun laws are affected by the court's ruling will find legal workarounds to blunt the effects of the decision, like adding training requirements.\par \par \uc2 Gail: Yeah, but it's not going to be nearly as easy for the police to be efficient in cracking down on illegal weaponry. Or to contain the flood of make-it-yourself gun kits that come through the mail.\par \par \uc2 One idea you've suggested in the past that looks really attractive today is to create skill standards. People who own guns have to be able to pass a test that demonstrates they have at least a minimal ability to hit the thing they're pointing at.\par \par \uc2 Bret: Back in the day, the \uc2 National Rifle Association\uc2  was primarily known as a group that taught marksmanship and the safe handling of firearms. Maybe it can get back to that instead of being the lobbying juggernaut that facilitates a massacre a month.\par \par \uc2 Gail: If I thought all the people who'd be packing heat in New York would shoot only their intended target, I'd still be opposed to this change, but not nearly as frightened as I am now. It's my strong impression that many people with guns have little or no training, and to tell you the truth, my experience with police suggests that while many of them have good intentions, a whole lot of them do not have good aim.\par \par \uc2 Bret: Remind me to tell you the story of the shootout my parents found themselves in, when a ''good guy with a gun'' started firing at the bad guys with guns who were robbing our home in Mexico. Good thing the coffee table under which my mom took cover was solid mahogany.\par \par \uc2 Gail: Wow, I want details before next week. And Bret, before we leave this topic, I have to admit -- sadly, sadly, sadly -- that we might not be living through this disastrous moment if \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  had not refused to retire before Barack Obama left office.\par \par \uc2 I'm a total fan of Justice Ginsburg -- treasure the memory of one day I spent with her while she was on the court. But she was in her 80s, people were begging her to step down, and she seemed to just hang on in the expectation that \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  would be the next president.\par \par \uc2 Bret: In fairness, she wasn't alone in the expectation.\par \par \uc2 Gail: If the court had split 5-4 instead of 6-3, we might still have gotten bad decisions on these cases, but Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  made it pretty clear he'd have pushed things closer to the middle if he had the numbers.\par \par \uc2 Bret: As you know, Gail, I supported the nominations of \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2  and Kavanaugh -- though I didn't support Amy Coney Barrett's. I thought they were closer in judicial temperament to \uc2 Sandra Day O'Connor\uc2  and \uc2 Anthony Kennedy\uc2 , both of whom upheld Roe in the 1992 Casey decision, than to the go-for-broke moral fervor of Justices Thomas and \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 . Now's a pretty good time for me to admit that I was wrong and you were right.\par \par \uc2 Gail: Love it when you do that.\par \par \uc2 Bret: My admissions of error are like Bill Clinton's goals for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 : safe, legal -- and rare.\par \par \uc2 Gail: Before we leave guns and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the day, I want to send a shout-out to the senators, particularly \uc2 Chris Murphy\uc2  of Connecticut, who passed an actual gun reform bill last week. Not the kind of assault-rifle-banning law many of us wanted, but it did push things forward -- expanding background checks for young gun purchasers, encouraging red-flag laws that make it easier to remove weapons from people whose record indicates they're potentially dangerous.\par \par \uc2 Bret: And congratulations go to some Republican senators, too, particularly \uc2 John Cornyn\uc2  of Texas, who showed political courage to lead a bipartisan effort. Cornyn is what the other Texas senator is not: a grown-up.\par \par \uc2 Gail: It's not a super-reform, but Congress at least did something -- for the first time in years. And then, alas, the Supreme Court managed to distract our attention with its sweeping terribleness.\par \par \uc2 Bret: One of the things I've learned from conversing with you, Gail, is that history is full of even more sweeping terribleness that puts ours into perspective. On Friday I got together with a close family friend, Luis Stillmann, a survivor of the Mauthausen concentration camp whose story I told in a column a couple of years ago. He turned 100 in December. He just returned from a trip to Europe. He and his wife, Buba, a survivor of Auschwitz, are busy planning another trip to Israel in November to visit their beloved \uc2 Weizmann Institute of Science\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 If Luis and Buba can stay positive and forward-looking, so can we.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTopinion) and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY MARK PETERSON/REDUX IMAGES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220628ei6s0003n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
