\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 A Legal Flurry From Coast to Coast on  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Shawn Hubler and Mitch Smith \par \uc2 1,506 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates filed lawsuits to halt or delay bans on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in some states, with judges in Louisiana and Utah temporarily blocking new laws.\par \par \uc2 SACRAMENTO -- The battle over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  shifted to the states on Monday as a weekend of furious protest and prayerful thanksgiving in the wake of the Supreme Court's Roe v. Wade reversal gave way to a coast-to-coast wave of lawsuits, legislation and pitched political fights.\par \par \uc2 With conservatives in roughly half of the states moving swiftly to end or dramatically restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and liberals in about 20 more scrambling to preserve them, the national debate suddenly fragmented into a contentious patchwork, with lawyers and lawmakers dissecting state constitutions and statutes after Friday's ruling in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization.\par \par \uc2 ''It's all about the states from here on out,'' said Jessie Hill, a law professor at \uc2 Case Western Reserve University\uc2  who has worked on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights cases. ''We can fantasize about federal solutions to this issue or nationwide settlements of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  question, but I think that after Dobbs, I don't see a lot of possibilities at the federal level.''\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates in Kentucky, Idaho, Louisiana, Mississippi and Texas sued on Monday to halt or delay bans on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after a similar court challenge was filed in Arizona over the weekend. Planned Parenthood South Atlantic moved to withdraw a federal court challenge to a ban in South Carolina, but apparently only so the organization could file a fresh challenge in state courts.\par \par \uc2 To understand why, one need only to look at Louisiana and Utah, where judges on Monday temporarily blocked enforcement of laws that would have banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates are coalescing around a strategy of asking courts for temporary injunctions that at the very least can allow abortions to proceed in the short term. One of Louisiana's three clinics already said on Monday that it would reopen.\par \par \uc2 While the actions with the most immediate potential impacts occurred in states with bans or restrictions, states that support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights moved on Monday to shore up their protections. In California, a supermajority of state lawmakers placed a constitutional amendment on the November ballot to explicitly protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights for the state's 40 million people. In Washington, Gov. \uc2 Jay Inslee\uc2  said he would pursue a change in that state's Constitution to make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights permanent.\par \par \uc2 In states trying to ban abortions, the legal battles are accelerating.\par \par \uc2 Professor Hill is part of a team of lawyers challenging in federal court an Ohio law that bans abortions after about six weeks of pregnancy. A judge allowed that law to take effect after the Supreme Court ruling. But Professor Hill said she believed that protections for individual rights in Ohio's Constitution could make for a compelling argument that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is protected in the state.\par \par \uc2 In Florida, providers on Monday deployed similar arguments in a court hearing, contending that privacy rights in the state's Constitution pre-empted a new state ban on the procedure after 15 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The Louisiana district court temporarily blocked so-called trigger laws that would have criminalized nearly all abortions after health providers argued that the bans were unenforceable and vague and violated the state's Constitution. In Utah, the judge said he would temporarily block enforcement of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in that state.\par \par \uc2 ''There is irreparable harm that has been shown,'' Judge Andrew Stone said while issuing a temporary restraining order that will be in effect for two weeks. Lawyers for a clinic that sought the order said they had 28 women awaiting an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  appointment on Monday.\par \par \uc2 But even as lawsuits were filed, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents moved to introduce restrictions. In Mississippi, the state attorney general officially recognized the Supreme Court's ruling, starting a 10-day clock after which almost all abortions will be prohibited. In South Carolina, a measure banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at about six weeks of pregnancy appeared likely to take effect after an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider asked a federal judge to withdraw a lawsuit that had blocked the law from taking effect. And in Indiana, the attorney general asked courts to allow the state to enforce several laws, including one banning abortions sought because of race, sex or disability.\par \par \uc2 ''I believe in building a culture of life in Indiana,'' Attorney General Todd Rokita said in a statement. ''That means protecting the lives of unborn babies and safeguarding the physical, mental and emotional well-being of their mothers.''\par \par \uc2 As states absorbed the end of a half-century-old set of \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  that had been guaranteed by the U.S. Constitution, the most striking impact was the pace with which it intensified already widening political fissures.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, attorneys general in 21 states and the District of Columbia issued a joint statement reassuring out-of-state patients that they would protect their access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The represented states included New Mexico, North Carolina and Minnesota, which could see more patients from nearby states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 That comes after attorneys general in 19 other states last week jointly asked the \uc2 U.S. Justice Department\uc2  to protect anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  organizations from violence. Among the states represented were Florida, Ohio and Texas.\par \par \uc2 In some places, state bans initiated by conservative lawmakers were challenged by more liberal cities. In Ohio, where courts permitted a 2019 law banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at about six weeks to take effect after the Supreme Court ruling, Cincinnati's mayor said on Monday that he was taking steps to change the city's health plan to reimburse city employees for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related travel and for reproductive services to the extent they are allowed under state law.\par \par \uc2 ''It is not my job to make it easier for the state legislature and governor to drag women back to the 50's and strip their rights,'' Mayor Aftab Pureval tweeted. ''It's my job to make that harder.''\par \par \uc2 In North Dakota, a fund-raising campaign raised more than a half-million dollars in three days to help move the state's sole \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic a few miles to a new location on the other side of the Minnesota state line.\par \par \uc2 Several court challenges to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans focused on state constitutions, particularly those in which a right to privacy is embedded. Alaska, Arizona, California, Florida, Hawaii, Illinois, Louisiana, Montana, New Hampshire, South Carolina and Washington all have specific provisions relating to a right to privacy.\par \par \uc2 Joanna Grisinger, an associate professor of instruction at Northwestern's Center for Legal Studies, said she also expected \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates to challenge the enforceability of trigger laws, which more than a dozen states enacted to quickly ban or restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the Supreme Court decision.\par \par \uc2 ''There's all sorts of ways that a motivated state could probably try to continue, if they have the votes, to limit or ban most \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access,'' she said. ''But there are certainly some strategies in the coming months we're going to see pushing back against some of these.''\par \par \uc2 Dr. Grisinger predicted that there could be another round of court review if doctors were charged with crimes for performing abortions, believing that the mother's health is at risk. Some \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans that provide exceptions for the life or health of the mother use relatively vague language that could put prosecutors and doctors at odds.\par \par \uc2 ''I would at least anticipate challenges from doctors and medical associations as they really strive to figure out what they can do,'' she said. But, she added, ''a lot of this is going to wait until someone is prosecuted.''\par \par \uc2 Brigitte Amiri, a deputy director of the Reproductive Freedom Project at \uc2 the American Civil Liberties Union\uc2 , warned that court challenges, while important, were not a panacea for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters. Even in places where they find relief in trial courts, they face a difficult long-term legal and political landscape.\par \par \uc2 The state supreme courts in many places with restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws are dominated by justices who are Republicans or were appointed by Republicans. And even if certain \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions are deemed unenforceable, legislatures can try to pass new laws, and voters can seek to amend their state constitutions.\par \par \uc2 ''Like we've often said, the courts are not going to be able to save us,'' Ms. Amiri said. ''That was true even when Roe was the law of the land -- that you needed all the tools in the toolbox then. But I think even more so now. We have to diversify what we're doing in terms of trying to ensure \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.''\par \par \uc2 Reporting was contributed by Richard Fausset, Alexandra Glorioso, Jesus Jim\u233\'00\'E9nez and Campbell Robertson.\par \par \uc2 Reporting was contributed by Richard Fausset, Alexandra Glorioso, Jesus Jim\u233\'00\'E9nez and Campbell Robertson.\par \par \uc2  rights advocates marched past a statue of Brigham Young in Salt Lake City on Friday in reaction to the Supreme Court's overturning of Roe v. Wade. (PHOTOGRAPH BY KIM RAFF FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A14) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220628ei6s0003j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
