\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Companies Form Policies For  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Emma Goldberg \par \uc2 1,220 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Some have offered to cover travel and other expenses for employees, and are working to ease worker fears about safety and confidentiality.\par \par \uc2 There is no clear blueprint for corporate engagement on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . After numerous companies came forward to announce that they would cover travel expenses for their employees to get abortions, executives have had to move swiftly to both sort out the mechanics of those policies and explain them to a work force concerned about confidentiality and safety.\par \par \uc2 Few companies have commented directly on the Supreme Court's ruling in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, which ended nearly 50 years of federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Far more have responded by expanding their health care policies to cover travel and other expenses for employees who can't get abortions close to home, now that the procedure is banned in at least eight states with other bans set to soon take effect. About half the country gets its health care coverage from employers, and the wave of new employer commitments has raised concerns from some workers about privacy.\par \par \uc2 ''It's a doomsday scenario if individuals have to bring their health care choices to their employers,'' said Dina Fierro, a global vice president at the cosmetics company Nars, echoing a concern that many workers have expressed on social media in recent days.\par \par \uc2 Employers are scrambling to prepare for possible legal challenges to their health care policies, as well as responding to scrutiny of their past political donations to politicians who supported \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. \uc2 Match Group\uc2 , for example, whose former chief executive \uc2 Shar Dubey\uc2  announced a fund in September supporting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in partnership with Planned Parenthood Los Angeles, donated over $100,000 to the Republican Attorneys General Association last year, as was reported in Popular Information. \uc2 Match Group\uc2  declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 Among the companies that said they would assist employees who have to travel for abortions are \uc2 Disney\uc2 , \uc2 Macy's\uc2 , \uc2 H&M\uc2 , \uc2 Nordstrom\uc2 , \uc2 Nike\uc2 , \uc2 Dick's Sporting Goods\uc2 , Goldman Sachs, Bank of America and Snap, which join a larger group including \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  and \uc2 Yelp\uc2  that had previously committed to doing so. \uc2 Salesforce\uc2  and \uc2 Google\uc2  both said they would move employees who want to leave states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is banned.\par \par \uc2 These employers cover health care for only a fraction of the millions of people living in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is or will soon be banned. And other major employers have not made public statements regarding employee assistance. The country's largest private employer, \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , declined to comment on the Supreme Court's ruling. Other large employers like Target, \uc2 Coca-Cola\uc2  and \uc2 Delta Air Lines\uc2  did not respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 Some marketing experts note that companies that do weigh in will likely face some backlash. ''Consumers and employees don't want companies to 'take a stand' -- unless companies take up their position and cause,'' Kimberly Whitler, who teaches marketing at the \uc2 University of Virginia Darden School of Business\uc2 , said in an email.\par \par \uc2 In an attempt to ease fears over potential confidentiality issues, many employers rolling out new benefits related to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are aiming to allow workers, and others on their health care plans, to get travel reimbursement without disclosing anything to their managers. In some cases, that means having people submit claims to their insurance companies as they would for other medical procedures. \uc2 Yelp\uc2 , for example, explained to its employees in April that its travel benefit is administered through its insurance provider.\par \par \uc2 ''No one at \uc2 Yelp\uc2  will ever receive any information on who incurred a claim or received reimbursement,'' a \uc2 Yelp\uc2  spokeswoman said.\par \par \uc2 Aetna\uc2 , one of the largest insurance companies, said it would ''ensure our data practices comply with all applicable laws protecting the privacy of our members.'' \uc2 UnitedHealth\uc2  declined to comment specifically on privacy issues. Anthem, \uc2 Cigna\uc2  and \uc2 Humana\uc2  did not respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 Expedia\uc2  said travel costs would be reimbursed through medical plan providers, and employees could use their time off without noting the reason. \uc2 BuzzFeed\uc2  said that instead of reimbursements for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related expenses, it would offer stipends that would be approved by the head of its human resources department -- someone, the company said, who was trained to handle confidential issues.\par \par \uc2 PayPal\uc2  said it had an employee advocacy team that provided confidential information to employees on sensitive issues, including on using their health care benefits. \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers have third-party point people, called advocates, whom employees can anonymously approach with questions about health care benefits, ensuring they don't have to disclose details about their medical needs to managers.\par \par \uc2 ''That can be anything from 'I've got knee surgery planned and want to make the right decision on a plan,' to getting advice on what they should do if they intend to use the fertility benefit and everything in between,'' said Reggie Borges, a spokesman for the company.\par \par \uc2 Some employers have laid out the details of their new health care policies in memos to staff. \uc2 Impossible Foods\uc2 , for example, said that in addition to travel for abortions it would also cover lodging, meals and child care. \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  said that as of July 1 its health care plans would include reimbursement for travel and lodging for ''legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related services.'' (Patagonia said it would also cover bail for employees who are arrested while peacefully protesting the Supreme Court's decision.)\par \par \uc2 Many other companies were still ironing out their plans. Culture Amp, for example, an employee survey firm, said in announcing up to $2,000 in reimbursements for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related travel that it was figuring out how to ''minimize the disclosure of information in the reimbursement process.''\par \par \uc2 The company said Monday that it was still getting final confirmation that flight or gas expenses could be routed for approval to the human resources team instead of through managers.\par \par \uc2 ''You shouldn't have to tell your manager you're getting an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' said Aubrey Blanche, a senior director at the company.\par \par \uc2 Currently, no states with bans try to prosecute women who travel out of state for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but some legal experts think that those laws could be possible in the future, as could attempts to use existing laws to prosecute \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  travel. Republican legislators in Texas have already said they plan to introduce legislation penalizing companies that pay for out-of-state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  travel.\par \par \uc2 ''We're going to see creative attempts by people who are deeply committed to stopping \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to use existing laws and pass new laws to stop as many abortions as possible, including those funded by companies,'' said \uc2 David Cohen\uc2 , a constitutional law professor at \uc2 Drexel University\uc2 . ''Companies are gearing up for a fight.''\par \par \uc2 And some executives seemed prepared for it. On Friday, \uc2 Marc Benioff\uc2 , chief executive of \uc2 Salesforce\uc2 , wrote in a tweet: ''I believe CEOs have a responsibility to take care of their employees -- no matter what.''\par \par \uc2 Lora Kelley contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Lora Kelley contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Bank of America, Starbucks and Dick's Sporting Goods are among a number of companies that have committed to assisting employees who can't get \uc2  care close to home. About half the country gets its health care coverage from employers, prompting privacy concerns from some. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY JEENAH MOON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES; AUDRA MELTON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES; SPENCER PLATT/GETTY IMAGES) (B3) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220628ei6s0003c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
