\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 California Looks to Enshrine  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Rights in State Constitution\b0\par\par\uc2 By Shawn Hubler \par \uc2 604 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The state constitutional amendment is the latest in a wave of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights legislation that has swept states led by Democrats.\par \par \uc2 SACRAMENTO -- California state lawmakers are expected as soon as Monday to put a state constitutional amendment on the ballot that would explicitly protect \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The amendment, which would go to California voters in November for approval, comes as states across the country react to the sweeping Supreme Court decision ending longstanding \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections.\par \par \uc2 At least 15 states and the District of Columbia affirmed or expanded \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights before Friday's court shift, while roughly two dozen other states signaled that they would end or dramatically restrict access to the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  of California has vowed to ''fight like hell'' to sustain \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the state. In the past year, Mr. Newsom has ramped up funding for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers; offered tax breaks for companies seeking to move from states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be outlawed; and signed bills to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients from privacy intrusions, insurance co-pays and threats, pressure or other attempts at ''reproductive coercion.''\par \par \uc2 On Friday, Mr. Newsom signed a bill to shield California \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers from liability or prosecution related to out-of-state bans on abortions. He also announced an agreement with Gov. \uc2 Kate Brown\uc2  of Oregon and Gov. \uc2 Jay Inslee\uc2  of Washington to establish a West Coast \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  firewall that would protect providers and patients from the legal reach of other states.\par \par \uc2 Pending bills would authorize experienced nurse practitioners to perform first-trimester abortions without a physician's supervision and create a state-administered fund to help underwrite travel expenses for the many women from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -ban states expected to come to California for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The legislative blitz reflects California's widespread support of a woman's right to choose when and whether to bear children. A poll last year by the \uc2 Public Policy Institute of California\uc2  found that roughly four out of five California voters oppose the repeal of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 The state's constitution already includes a right to privacy that has long underpinned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  statutes. But after the Supreme Court leak in May, the state's legislative leaders moved to make certain that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and contraception are explicitly protected. A similar constitutional amendment will go to Vermont voters in the fall, ensuring ''personal reproductive liberty.''\par \par \uc2 California's constitutional amendment ''would make it undeniably clear that in California, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and contraception are health care and are a private matter between a patient and their medical provider,'' said Toni G. Atkins, the president pro tempore of the State Senate, who directed a women's health clinic in San Diego before entering public service.\par \par \uc2 The Senate has already passed the amendment with supermajority approval, and it needs to receive at least two-thirds support in the Assembly by this week to be placed on the ballot in November. The amendment does not require Mr. Newsom's signature to reach the ballot, but he supports it.\par \par \uc2 Opponents include the California Catholic Conference, which criticized the proposal's wording as ''extreme'' and so loose that it could be interpreted as allowing late-term abortions.\par \par \uc2 ''The sad reality is that California already has some of the most accommodating \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws and services in the nation,'' the conference said in a statement, vowing to muster ''the state's 12 million Catholics'' in its fight against the amendment.\par \par \uc2 California Gov. Gavin Newsom signed a bill to shield \uc2  providers from prosecution related to out-of-state bans. (PHOTOGRAPH BY RICH PEDRONCELLI/ASSOCIATED PRESS) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220627ei6r00044\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
