\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Ban Is Huge Obstacle for Native Women\b0\par\par\uc2 By Tom Lawrence \par \uc2 351 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SIOUX FALLS, S.D. -- South Dakota's law banning abortions, which went into effect on Friday, will greatly affect Native American women, Sierra Wolcott, a member of the Sisseton Wahpeton Tribe, said.\par \par \uc2 ''Reservations already tend to be quite a drive from big cities, and now women will have to cross state lines to even attempt to receive this health care,'' Wolcott, 42, said.\par \par \uc2 South Dakota is home to around 70,000 Native Americans, who make up about 8.5 percent of the state's population. Anyone seeking an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in South Dakota would have to travel out of state. Minnesota, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal, is the nearest state to women in East River, as South Dakotans refer to the eastern side of the state, while Colorado would be the nearest state for women in West River.\par \par \uc2 But, Mrs. Wolcott said, there are other options for Indigenous women who, she said, are understood to be in charge of their own bodies.\par \par \uc2 ''Indigenous cultures have had medicinal ways of ending pregnancy for generations,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm worried for the stability of Planned Parenthood and clinics like them. When I was pregnant with my second child I had no insurance and I was able to go to Planned Parenthood for prenatal care,'' she said. ''These clinics save lives and help women and men every day, I cannot imagine how detrimental it would be to lose them.''\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been a flashpoint for Native Americans in South Dakota previously. In 2006, after the South Dakota Legislature passed a law outlawing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Oglala Sioux Tribe President Cecilia Fire Thunder, who is a nurse, said she would work with Planned Parenthood to open a clinic on the Pine Ridge Reservation.\par \par \uc2 The Oglala Sioux Tribal Council, which was primarily male, soon suspended her from office and later impeached her. The council passed a law more restrictive than the state ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which was overturned by voters in 2006. Another ban was put into effect in 2008, but South Dakota voters rejected it.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220627ei6r00041\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
