\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Factions Gear Up For New Clashes in  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Fight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate Zernike \par \uc2 1,796 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Lawyers, politicians and advocacy groups responded to Friday's Supreme Court ruling with fresh plans for the fight over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court ruling overturning Roe v. Wade has unleashed a frenzy of activity on both sides of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  fight, with anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  forces vowing to push for near-total bans in every state in the nation, and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups insisting they would harness rage over the decision to take to the streets, fight back in the courts and push the Biden administration to do more to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 The court said its ruling on Friday was needed because of what it called a half-century of bitter national controversy sparked by Roe, but its decision set off more immediate and widespread rancor and mobilizing than the original ruling -- and guaranteed pitched battles and extraordinary division ahead.\par \par \uc2 The maneuvering was already underway.\par \par \uc2 In Florida, where the Legislature recently passed a ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 15 weeks, lawmakers pushed Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  to call a special session to consider a ban after six weeks.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 National Right to Life Committee\uc2  promoted model legislation for state bans and renewed calls toward its original, bigger goal of a constitutional amendment banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide. It and other anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups also pledged to punish prosecutors who have said they would not enforce \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 They promised other steps to limit access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including pushing for legislation prohibiting people from crossing state lines to get abortions or obtaining \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights groups were heading back to court with a hearing Monday where they are seeking an injunction to stop Florida's 15-week ban from taking effect. They promised court fights over the so-called trigger bans that took effect on Friday upon the Supreme Court's ruling.\par \par \uc2 In Ohio, Freda J. Levenson, legal director for the Ohio American Civil Liberties Union, said Sunday that her organization and Planned Parenthood of Ohio would file suit early this week to block the implementation of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in the state, arguing that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a protected right under the Ohio constitution.\par \par \uc2 The Women's March, which rallied hundreds of thousands to demonstrations after Donald J. Trump became president in 2017, promised street protests in a ''Summer of Rage'' and said it would back primary challenges to Democrats it considered complicit in the appointment of the conservative Supreme Court majority.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, California state lawmakers are expected to put a state constitutional amendment on the ballot that would explicitly protect \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In Michigan, where Gov. \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  has filed suit to stop a nearly century-old ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from taking effect, activists were collecting signatures on a ballot initiative that would enshrine the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state Constitution.\par \par \uc2 ''We're going at it, we're pulling out all the stops,'' Ms. Whitmer, a Democrat, said on ''Face the Nation With Margaret Brennan.'' ''This is a fight-like-hell moment.''\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights supporters could take heart over what appeared to be broad public disapproval of Friday's ruling. A CBS News/\uc2 YouGov\uc2  poll conducted immediately after the court handed down its decision shows that Americans considered it a ''step backward'' for the nation by more than a 20 percentage-point margin.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 60 percent of Americans and two-thirds of women disapproved of the ruling, the poll said. Fifty-eight percent said they would approve of a federal law making \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legal.\par \par \uc2 And 56 percent of women said the ruling would make women's lives worse, according to the poll, far greater than the 16 percent who said it would improve women's lives\par \par \uc2 But, opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , celebrating their biggest victory in the nearly 50 years since Roe, felt as if they had the wind at their back.\par \par \uc2 Kristan Hawkins, president of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group Students for Life of America, said its primary focus would now be on preventing pregnant women from getting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills as a workaround to bans. It had also discussed proposed legislation, modeled along the lines of a Texas law that since September has banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after six weeks, that would allow ordinary citizens to sue anyone who provided \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services across state lines.\par \par \uc2 ''Ultimately our mission in the pro-life movement is to make the act of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  unthinkable and unavailable in our nation,'' Ms. Hawkins said.\par \par \uc2 The waves of joy and anger set off immediately after the decision Friday continued all weekend, on the airwaves and in pulpits and at protests in the sweltering summer heat.\par \par \uc2 A thousand protesters waving signs and chanting objected to the court's decision outside the State Capitol in Oklahoma City, where Gov. Kevin Stitt, a Republican, signed one of the strictest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in the nation last month, in anticipation that the court would overturn Roe.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds turned out to support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights at rallies in Birmingham, Huntsville and Montgomery in deeply conservative Alabama. Leaders of the rally outside the Madison County Courthouse in Huntsville urged the crowd not to pay attention to or interact with a group of chanting anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protesters who attempted to interrupt the gathering.\par \par \uc2 Denunciations of the court ruling rang out at events wrapping up Gay Pride month across the country. A contingent of Planned Parenthood supporters led off the boisterous Pride Parade in downtown Manhattan, chanting, ''Rise up for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights!'' At the Pride event in San Francisco a city supervisor, Rafael Mandelman, told the crowd that while they could party for the day, ''tomorrow we have work to do!'' Even those in California, he said, could campaign for congressional candidates in other states.\par \par \uc2 ''If we're going to change what happened on Friday, we all need to do work,'' he said. ''We can knock on doors and we can elect Democrats and we can protect Democrats.''\par \par \uc2 For many conservatives, Sunday was a day of celebration\par \par \uc2 At the Austin Baptist Church in Texas, the Rev. Jonathan Spencer devoted his two morning sermons to celebrating the court's decision.\par \par \uc2 ''I rejoice with the Lord in his mercy and grace in helping remove what I believe is one of the greatest tragedies of our generation,'' he told his congregants, asserting that more than 63 million children have been killed because of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since Roe.\par \par \uc2 ''This does not end the battle,'' he said. ''\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  still stands and people will still undergo these procedures.''\par \par \uc2 His message was well-received among the congregation. ''I thought he was perfectly on point,'' said Dawn Church, 49. Of the court decision, she said, ''I'm ecstatic.''\par \par \uc2 But in other congregations there were other messages.\par \par \uc2 At Mount Zion Baptist Church in Nashville, Bishop Joseph Walker III blessed several babies in a baby dedication ceremony, before calling on women in the large and mostly Black congregation to stand and be applauded.\par \par \uc2 He recognized the women for the role they have long played ''on the front lines of so many battles and fights'' nationally and globally and committed to more prayers for them.\par \par \uc2 ''Look at these beautiful babies, life is a blessing,'' he said. ''At the end of the day, no one has a right to tell you what to do with you. That's between you and God.''\par \par \uc2 Tameka Gibson, 45, welcomed his support. ''I believe in pro-choice,'' she said. ''I believe that is a decision between people and God.'' She did not agree with the direction Tennessee was taking; its trigger ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  took effect on Friday.\par \par \uc2 ''Everything is going backwards,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Protests were mainly peaceful, though some sporadic violence was reported. A grocery store worker on Staten Island was arrested on Sunday after hitting \uc2 Rudolph W. Giuliani\uc2  on the back while the former mayor campaigned on behalf of his son, a Republican candidate for governor. Mr. Giuliani said he was walking through a \uc2 ShopRite\uc2  grocery store when the employee slapped his back and said, ''You're going to kill women.''\par \par \uc2 But as the nation absorbed the gravity of the moment -- the rare occasion when the court has taken away a constitutional right -- there were scenes of doubt, nuance and sometimes a desire to find middle ground, or at least understand those with different and deeply held views.\par \par \uc2 At the service at the Cathedral of the Incarnation in Nashville on Sunday morning with her husband and 5-month-old daughter, Katie Fullan said she supported the court's decision. ''But I had mixed feelings too,'' she said. ''I have a lot of friends, co-workers, neighbors who feel very distressed by it, and I feel like I really sympathize with them and understand where they are coming from.''\par \par \uc2 And while she supported the state's decision to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , she thought it also needed to pass laws for paid maternity leave, subsidized child care and financial support for food and housing for those who need it.\par \par \uc2 ''Many of the reasons women feel like they need \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is because of the lack of support for raising children,'' Ms. Fullan said. ''The hardships that come with pregnancy and recovery, that's going to be hard even with paid health care and paid child care.''\par \par \uc2 At the Brethren Church on a rural highway in Jefferson Township, Ohio, the congregation is split roughly half between Black and white members, with a handful of Latino congregants. Part of the service is delivered in Spanish. And while the church's stances have historically been progressive, the membership prides itself on nurturing a diversity of views.\par \par \uc2 ''I am totally for the Supreme Court verdict, I don't believe in harming innocent children,'' said Sharon Sampson.\par \par \uc2 Terri Griffith said: ''I am very disillusioned. This Supreme Court is dangerous.''\par \par \uc2 Yet those on opposite sides had worshiped together and shared in the potluck after the service. Jan Putrell, 68, was also there. While she described herself as a ''radical progressive'' on many social issues, she said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't fit the easy categories that some do.\par \par \uc2 ''We need a time of discernment,'' she said, ''to reflect on the verdict.''\par \par \uc2 Reporting was contributed by Jamie McGee, David Montgomery, Kevin Williams, Holly Secon, Luke Vander Ploeg, Sydney Cromwell and Ben Fenwick\par \par \uc2 Reporting was contributed by Jamie McGee, David Montgomery, Kevin Williams, Holly Secon, Luke Vander Ploeg, Sydney Cromwell and Ben Fenwick\par \par \uc2  rights advocates demonstrated outside the Supreme Court in Washington on Sunday. The Women's March promised street protests in a ''Summer of Rage.''; The waves of joy and anger set off when Roe v. Wade was overturned on Friday continued all weekend, on the airwaves, in pulpits and at protests in the sweltering summer heat. At left, demonstrators for \uc2  rights. At right, anti-\uc2  protesters. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY SHURAN HUANG FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A16) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220627ei6r0003h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
