\par\par \uc2 DEALBOOK NEWSLETTER\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Businesses Scramble to Navigate the Choppy Post-Roe Waters\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ephrat Livni, Lauren Hirsch and Jenny Gross \par \uc2 1,422 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Reeling from competing demands from stakeholders -- including activists, clients, consumers, shareholders and elected officials -- businesses are increasingly caught in the middle of the country's bruising culture wars.\par \par \uc2 The DealBook newsletter delves into a single topic or theme every weekend, providing reporting and analysis that offers a better understanding of an important issue in business. If you don't already receive the daily newsletter, sign up here .\par \par \uc2 Yesterday's Supreme Court decision overruling Roe v. Wade sent business leaders and employees across the nation scrambling for answers about how to react, what to say -- or what not to say -- and the immediate practical implications of the ruling.\par \par \uc2 'This is the time of reckoning'\par \par \uc2 For U.S. businesses, there are pressing issues that demand fast answers, starting with: What will this mean for employees' health care coverage? ''I guarantee you that there are teams of lawyers trying to figure this out right now,'' said Tom Baker, an insurance law expert at the \uc2 University of Pennsylvania\uc2 's law school.\par \par \uc2 Businesses have already been battered in political and social fights in recent years and are reeling from competing demands from stakeholders -- including activists, clients, consumers, shareholders and elected officials -- about how to respond to the country's bruising culture wars.\par \par \uc2 Some executives are already starting to focus on the potential for other shoes to drop: Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 's concurring opinion raised questions about what other rights could soon disappear. The rationale that the Supreme Court used to declare that there is no right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he argued, should also be used to overturn cases establishing rights to contraception, consensual same-sex relations and same-sex marriage.\par \par \uc2 Local officials in states that restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are already threatening to punish businesses that help employees gain access to it elsewhere; activists who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are calling on businesses to cut campaign donations to officials who oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''This is the time of reckoning,'' said Sonja Spoo, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaigns at the gender justice organization UltraViolet, in a statement calling on corporations to act. She named \uc2 AT&T\uc2 , \uc2 Comcast\uc2  and \uc2 Disney\uc2  in particular for their political donations, and demanded that social media platforms like \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , \uc2 YouTube\uc2 , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and TikTok ensure that people have access to accurate information about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 There is also this backdrop: Many businesses are moving to lower-tax states, and many of those same states are now poised to enact near-total bans on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In the past year, \uc2 Tesla\uc2  moved from the San Francisco Bay Area to Austin, Texas; just this past week, Citadel said it was moving from Chicago to Miami. That has been the playbook for corporate America for decades: Find a blue enclave in a red state -- Austin in Texas, Nashville in Tennessee -- and move there for low taxes and a more affordable work force.\par \par \uc2 That may be much more complicated in the aftershock of yesterday's ruling.\par \par \uc2 ''Texas, obviously, the economic argument was the primary argument -- at least for me it was one of the primary arguments,'' said \uc2 Vivek Bhaskaran\uc2 , chief executive of QuestionPro, a technology services company that moved its headquarters to Austin from San Francisco just before the pandemic. ''It definitely puts a pause on the economic argument.''\par \par \uc2 Executives have tried to stay out of the fray -- or have waded in only after intense employee pressure, with tumultuous results. In April, Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  of Florida, angry with \uc2 Disney\uc2 's chief executive, \uc2 Bob Chapek\uc2 , for speaking out against the state's new Parental Rights in Education act, had the Legislature strip the company of its special tax status.\par \par \uc2 Reaffirming commitments\par \par \uc2 Companies began to come out with policies on covering travel expenses for employees who need abortions when a draft opinion that was leaked in May previewed the justices' decision in the case. This small group includes \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , \uc2 Yelp\uc2 , \uc2 Airbnb\uc2 , \uc2 Netflix\uc2 , Patagonia, \uc2 DoorDash\uc2 , \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2 , \uc2 Levi Strauss & Company\uc2 , \uc2 PayPal\uc2  and Reddit.\par \par \uc2 Others, including \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , \uc2 Disney\uc2 , \uc2 Meta\uc2 , \uc2 Dick's Sporting Goods\uc2  and Cond\u233\'00\'E9 Nast, joined them on Friday when the decision became final, though most of them avoided making public statements directly referring to the ruling. Instead, the statements often obtusely signaled that the companies offered out-of-state medical services, seemingly hoping to appeal to employees without drawing the ire of politicians.\par \par \uc2 Facebook\uc2  told employees not to discuss the decision on internal chat systems.\par \par \uc2 A spokeswoman for \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 , the country's largest bank, with about 170,000 U.S. employees, said it was focused on equal access to health care for all its employees. She highlighted a June 1 memo alerting them that their travel costs would be covered if they needed to go more than 50 miles to receive certain medical procedures, including abortions.\par \par \uc2 But the ruling raises new questions over whether state authorities may try to prosecute businesses that offer to pay for employees to travel out of state for abortions, as lawmakers in Texas have been threatening to do.\par \par \uc2 ''This kind of decision forces politics into the workplace, and employers hate that,'' Austin Kaplan, an employment lawyer in Texas, said.\par \par \uc2 A signal to the rest of the world\par \par \uc2 Outside the United States, the Supreme Court decision is being watched closely.\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  of Britain called the ruling ''a big step backwards.''\par \par \uc2 In Belgium, Prime Minister \uc2 Alexander De Croo\uc2  said on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  on Friday that he was concerned about the implications of overturning Roe v. Wade. ''Banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  never leads to fewer abortions, only to more unsafe abortions,'' he tweeted, adding that Belgium would continue working with other countries to advance \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  and rights.\par \par \uc2 Nicola Sturgeon\uc2 , Scotland's first minister, said the decision was one of the darkest days for women's rights in her lifetime. ''Obviously the immediate consequences will be suffered by women in the US -- but this will embolden anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  & anti-women forces in other countries too,'' she tweeted.\par \par \uc2 Most of the European companies contacted by DealBook declined to comment or said it was too early to say how the Supreme Court's decisions on gun regulation and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  affected their approach to posting employees in the United States. But in general, most European countries have, in recent years, moved to relax \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions.\par \par \uc2 In the United States, some female executives have already made their stances known. Julie Jones, chairwoman of the law firm \uc2 Ropes & Gray\uc2 , wrote to employees yesterday in a memo that was obtained by DealBook: ''As a woman, I have a profound feeling of vulnerability caused by the elimination of a longstanding right of women -- a right that affects their bodies and their agency.\par \par \uc2 ''As a person with privilege,'' she continued, ''I recognize and worry about the decision's disproportionate impact on women with limited resources. As an American, I fear the divisive nature of this topic will further fracture an already angry and divided citizenry. As a leader of \uc2 Ropes & Gray\uc2 , I am concerned about the effect of this decision on our community.''\par \par \uc2 The economic impact\par \par \uc2 In a Senate hearing last month, after a leaked draft decision indicated the Supreme Court would overturn Roe v. Wade, Treasury Secretary \uc2 Janet L. Yellen\uc2  said an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban would damage both the U.S. economy and economic prospects for women.\par \par \uc2 She added that legalization of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped lead to increased labor force participation among women and that ''denying women access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  increases their odds of living in poverty or need for public assistance.''\par \par \uc2 A 2020 working paper published in the \uc2 National Bureau of Economic Research\uc2  comparing economic outcomes for women who were able to access abortions with those who weren't, found those without access experienced ''a large increase in financial distress that is sustained for several years.'' It also concluded that women who were denied abortions had substantially more public records, which include events like eviction and bankruptcies.\par \par \uc2 For companies that don't want to weigh in on political or social issues, Sarah Miller, an assistant professor at the Ross School of Business at the \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2  and a lead author of the paper, said being reticent could impact their labor cost since ''at some point in the pandemic, women crossed the 50 percent threshold for the work force.''\par \par \uc2 What do you think? Can companies stay silent on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? Let us know: dealbook@nytimes.com.\par \par \uc2 -rights demonstrators outside the Supreme Court on Friday after Roe v. Wade was overturned. (PHOTOGRAPH BY SHURAN HUANG FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220627ei6r00038\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
