\par\par \uc2 GUEST ESSAY\par \uc2 Sunday Review Desk; SECTSR\par \b \uc2 The Fight Continues\b0\par\par\uc2 By Elizabeth Warren and Tina Smith \par \uc2 1,189 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court has spoken: Roe is gone. But the Supreme Court doesn't get the final say on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The American people will have the last word through their representatives in Congress and the White House.\par \par \uc2 With its decision in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, an extremist Supreme Court has overturned nearly 50 years of precedent, stripping away the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and ruling that the government -- not the person who is pregnant -- will make the critical decision about whether to continue a pregnancy. At least nine states have already banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; over a dozen more could soon follow suit by severely restricting or outright outlawing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , putting the lives, health and futures of girls and women at risk.\par \par \uc2 If we sound angry and alarmed, that's because we are. This decision is devastating -- and we have seen what happens next. We both lived in an America where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was illegal. A nation in which infections and other complications destroyed lives. A nation in which unplanned pregnancies derailed careers and livelihoods. A nation in which some women took their own lives rather than continue pregnancies they could not bear.\par \par \uc2 But we must hold on to hope. Each of us can and should act -- both elected officials and everyday Americans. We can start by helping those who need access to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Support Planned Parenthood and other organizations that are expanding their services in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is available. Contribute to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds. Encourage state legislators to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  in states like New Mexico and Minnesota that border places where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services will most likely be severely restricted and even criminalized. Encourage employers in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans to give their employees adequate time off and money for travel to find the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care they need. Do all you can -- and demand the same all-you-can approach from all of our elected leaders.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, along with Senator \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2  and half the Senate Democratic Caucus, we sent a letter to President Biden outlining executive actions he could take to defend reproductive freedom. These actions include increasing access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication, providing federal resources for individuals seeking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care in other states and using federal property and resources to protect people seeking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services locally. We need action, and we need it now.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, with the release of the Dobbs decision, we entered a perilous time that threatens millions of women across this nation. We urge the president to declare a public health emergency to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access for all Americans, unlocking critical resources and authority that states and the federal government can use to meet the surge in demand for \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  services. The danger is real, and Democrats must meet it with the urgency it deserves.\par \par \uc2 We're in this dark moment because right-wing politicians and their allies have spent decades scheming to overrule a right many Americans considered sacrosanct. Passing state laws to restrict access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care. Giving personhood rights to fertilized eggs. Threatening to criminalize in vitro fertilization. Offering bounties for reporting doctors who provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services. Abusing the filibuster and turning Congress into a broken institution. Advancing judicial nominees who claimed to be committed to protecting ''settled law'' while they winked at their Republican sponsors in the Senate. Stealing two seats on the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 For nearly 50 years, right-wing extremists rejected the beliefs held by an overwhelming majority of Americans. They doubled and redoubled their efforts to create a future in which women and their doctors could face a prison sentence for seeking or providing basic health care. When these extremists couldn't impose their radical views through the legislative process, they stacked the courts. And now that the Supreme Court has opened the door by overturning Roe, Republicans will continue their assault on our civil rights and liberties.\par \par \uc2 Former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  called for a national ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in all 50 states; \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , the Senate minority leader, flat out stated that it's a possibility. And the logic laid out by the majority in Dobbs seems to undercut other precedents, raising the alarming possibility that we could soon see an assault on privacy and marriage equality.\par \par \uc2 In order to fix the damage Republicans have done to our system in their efforts to control women's lives, we need broad democracy reform: changing the composition of the courts, reforming Senate rules like the filibuster, and even fixing the outdated Electoral College that allowed presidential candidates who lost the popular vote to take office and nominate five of the justices who agreed to end the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We can't undo in five months the damage it took Republicans five decades to accomplish, but we can immediately start repairing our democracy. The public is overwhelmingly on our side. A vast majority of Americans oppose the decision the Supreme Court just made. Nearly two-thirds of Americans believe that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all or most cases. And more Americans describe themselves as pro-choice today than at any other point in the last 25 years.\par \par \uc2 Let's be clear: Roe may be gone, but the protections it once guaranteed are on the ballot. States like Kansas and Kentucky have initiatives to strip away state constitutional protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while Michigan and Vermont are working toward statewide votes to create constitutional protections for reproductive freedom. But make no mistake, this radical decision affects all Americans, not just those in states where the right to a safe, legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will soon fall.\par \par \uc2 Now is the time to demand that every single candidate for every single office voice a firm position on \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Ask every Senate candidate to commit to reforming the filibuster rules, so that the chamber can pass federal legislation protecting the right to reproductive freedom. If voters help us maintain our control of the House and expand our majority in the Senate by at least two votes this November, we can make Roe the law all across the country as soon as January.\par \par \uc2 Simply put: We must restore our democracy so that a radical minority can no longer drown out the will of the people. This will be a long, hard fight, and the path to victory is not yet certain. But it's a righteous fight that we must win -- no matter how long it takes. The two of us lived in an America without Roe, and we are not going back. Not now. Not ever.\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth Warren (@SenWarren) has represented Massachusetts in the Senate since 2013. \uc2 Tina Smith\uc2  (@SenTinaSmith) has represented Minnesota since 2018.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTopinion) and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY Michael McCoy/Reuters FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220626ei6q00063\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
