\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Laws Considered Relics May Now Be Enforced\b0\par\par\uc2 By Julie Bosman \par \uc2 1,414 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 23\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In Wisconsin, Michigan and other states, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans that were long considered dormant could determine if access to the procedure survives the overturning of Roe.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has become or will soon become illegal in more than a dozen states whose legislatures had passed so-called trigger laws, allowing for bans shortly after the Supreme Court decision overturning Roe v. Wade on Friday.\par \par \uc2 But \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are also in jeopardy in other states because of older bans criminalizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , some of which were written before the Civil War. Though the bans were considered dormant after the Roe decision in 1973, they were never repealed by state legislatures -- and could now be enforced. Two of the states, Michigan and Wisconsin, have Democratic governors who favor \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access and polling that shows a majority of residents do, too. But their Republican-controlled legislatures have shown no interest in repealing the old laws.\par \par \uc2 ''Every district attorney in the state is going to be empowered to potentially investigate miscarriages to test the limits of the law and see if they can put doctors in prison,'' said State Senator Kelda Roys, a Democrat in Wisconsin. ''It makes things very difficult for health care providers. It unleashes a whole host of terrible circumstances.''\par \par \uc2 The sudden importance of laws that were written before women had the right to vote has sent legislators, activists and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers scrambling to understand the implications. In Wisconsin, clinics in Milwaukee and Madison had already paused scheduling appointments for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedures next week in anticipation of the Supreme Court ruling; after its decision came on Friday morning, all of the state's clinics stopped providing abortions entirely.\par \par \uc2 Ismael Ozanne, the Dane County district attorney, signaled on Friday that he would not enforce the Wisconsin law that criminalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a suggestion that a patchwork situation could develop in which \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is prosecuted differently from county to county.\par \par \uc2 According to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , which supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, eight states still have \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans on the books that predate Roe v. Wade, but some have more recent bans that would most likely take precedence. In recent years, states including New Mexico, Vermont and Massachusetts have removed old bans.\par \par \uc2 In Michigan, where a law from 1931 bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the battle is already playing out in the courts. Gov. \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , a Democrat, filed a lawsuit in April asking the Michigan Supreme Court to resolve whether the State Constitution protects the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A Michigan judge issued an injunction in May that stops the ban from being enforced, at least temporarily, until a separate lawsuit is resolved.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, Ms. Whitmer called the 1931 law ''antiquated,'' noting that it does not provide exceptions for rape or incest. ''The 1931 law would punish women and strip away their right to make decisions about their own bodies,'' she said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Whitmer has vowed to veto legislation that would restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Michigan Legislature has a Republican majority but not one large enough to be likely to override a veto.\par \par \uc2 There is also a pre-Roe ban in West Virginia, but experts said it was unclear whether that or newer state laws that put fewer restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would take effect. The state's attorney general, Patrick Morrisey, said in a statement on Friday that he would soon ''be providing a legal opinion to the Legislature about how it should proceed to save as many babies' lives as humanly and legally possible.''\par \par \uc2 Arizona, Alabama and North Carolina also have older \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws on the books, but more recent restrictions passed in those states could take precedence, such as a total ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that became law in Alabama in 2019 but was superseded by Roe until now.\par \par \uc2 In Wisconsin, both sides are preparing for lawsuits and political battles over whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, which has been unenforceable since Roe v. Wade made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legal in 1973, will result in prosecutions.\par \par \uc2 ''The future of this old law will be determined in our state courts and our state political system,'' said Mike Murray, the vice president of government and external affairs for Planned Parenthood of Wisconsin. ''On a practical level, there is going to be litigation requesting clarification from our state courts about whether or not the 1849 law is enforceable.''\par \par \uc2 Gracie Skogman, the legislative director for Wisconsin Right to Life, said she hoped the 1849 law ''is enforceable and saves lives here in Wisconsin, but we also do expect that there will be legal challenges.'' On Friday, the organization said ''Wisconsin is in powerful position to defend preborn life due to our pre-Roe statute.''\par \par \uc2 Under the ban in Wisconsin, doctors who perform abortions can be found guilty of a felony. It includes exceptions for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that is necessary to save the mother's life, but does not make exceptions for cases of rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 Laws banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the 19th century were typically the result of an effort to regulate how medicine was practiced, which medicines could be distributed and who was providing drugs that could cause \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , historians said. The laws tended to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  only after ''quickening'' -- a point about midway through pregnancy when a woman can feel a fetus move in the womb.\par \par \uc2 James Mohr, a professor at the \uc2 University of Oregon\uc2  whose book ''\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in America'' details the history of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the United States, said 19th-century laws banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  were passed not for political reasons, but because of pressure from elite physicians, who were concerned that people who called themselves doctors were performing abortions without training.\par \par \uc2 ''It's very hard for Americans to wrap their mind around the fact that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was simply not a public issue in the 19th century,'' he said. ''It was not discussed in public, it was not political, it was not politicized.''\par \par \uc2 After states passed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, he said, ''It would appear that the practice of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued just about the way it always had.''\par \par \uc2 ''The same number of pregnancies as a percentage continued to be terminated,'' he continued. ''Prosecutors almost never brought prosecutions under these laws because juries wouldn't convict.''\par \par \uc2 Lauren MacIvor Thompson, an assistant professor of history and interdisciplinary studies at \uc2 Kennesaw State University\uc2  in Georgia who studies \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  history, said that recent laws banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  were far more restrictive than those passed well over a century ago.\par \par \uc2 ''By and large, many of the laws passed in the 19th century were more lenient and often did not punish the woman,'' she said. ''That is shifting rapidly.''\par \par \uc2 Past efforts to repeal the 1849 law in Wisconsin have fizzled, even when the Democratic Party controlled both the governor's office and the Legislature, and there was little push from the public to overturn it.\par \par \uc2 ''I hadn't heard much about the ban until quite recently,'' said Jenny Higgins, a professor of gender and women's studies and obstetrics and gynecology at the University of Wisconsin-Madison School of Medicine and Public Health. ''Folks didn't really believe that overturning Roe was possible, or palatable, until recently.''\par \par \uc2 Wisconsinites have indicated in recent polls that they favor keeping \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legal. In a recent poll conducted by Marquette Law School, 58 percent of state residents said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all or most cases.\par \par \uc2 This past week, Gov. \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2  convened a special session in the Legislature to pressure lawmakers to repeal the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. A ring of protesters in pink shirts gathered at the Statehouse in Madison, their chants ricocheting under the dome of the Capitol building.\par \par \uc2 But Republicans, who hold a majority in the State Senate, ended the session almost as quickly as it began, without a vote or discussion. \uc2 Robin Vos\uc2 , the speaker of the Assembly, posted on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  on Friday that ''safeguarding the lives of unborn children shouldn't be controversial.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Evers, who is running for re-election in November, condemned the Republican lawmakers after the session, saying they had jeopardized access to health care.\par \par \uc2 ''Republicans' refusal to act will have real and severe consequences for all of us and the people we care most about who could see their ability to make their own \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care decisions stripped away from them,'' Mr. Evers said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Gov. Tony Evers of Wisconsin called for the repeal of the state's \uc2  ban, which dates to 1849 and had been believed to be dormant. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ANDY MANIS/ASSOCIATED PRESS) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220626ei6q0005t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
