\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Roe's Fall Started in 2010 With G.O.P. Wave\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate Zernike \par \uc2 3,933 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The downfall of the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  began 12 years ago, after Republicans swept state house elections and passed hundreds of restrictions.\par \par \uc2 The beginning of the end of Roe v. Wade arrived on election night in November 2010.\par \par \uc2 That night, control of state houses across the country flipped from Democrat to Republican, almost to the number: Democrats had controlled 27 state legislatures going in and ended up with 16; Republicans started with 14 and ended up controlling 25. Republicans swept not only the South but Democratic strongholds in the Midwest, picking up more seats nationwide than either party had in four decades. By the time the votes had been counted, they held their biggest margin since the Great Depression.\par \par \uc2 There had been a time, in the 15 years after Roe, when Republicans were as likely as Democrats to support an absolute right to legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and sometimes even more so. But 2010 swept in a different breed of Republican, powered by Tea Party supporters, that locked in a new conservatism. While Tea Party-backed candidates had campaigned on fiscal discipline and promised indifference to social issues, once in office they found it difficult to cut state budgets. And a well-established network was waiting with model anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws.\par \par \uc2 In legislative sessions starting the following January, Republican-led states passed a record number of restrictions: 92, or nearly three times as many as the previous high, set in 2005.\par \par \uc2 The three years following the 2010 elections would result in 205 anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws across the country, more than in the entire previous decade.\par \par \uc2 ''A watershed year in the defense of life,'' Charmaine Yoest, at the time president of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group \uc2 Americans United for Life\uc2 , proclaimed when the sessions were over, noting that 70 of the laws -- restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills and hurdles for women getting abortions and clinics providing them -- had adopted the group's model legislation. ''And that is just the beginning.''\par \par \uc2 ''It was a massacre,'' said Beth Shipp, then the political director of \uc2 NARAL Pro-Choice America\uc2 , which had been founded before Roe to push for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 In the fraught and much disputed language of pregnancy, the elections of 2010 were the quickening of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement. The movement started out weak but gained power in the new red wave. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups, meanwhile, were weakened in the states. The ensuing debates in state legislatures pointed toward the polarization that would divide the country over the coronavirus pandemic and the presidential election 10 years later: gerrymandered control and party-line votes, and the two sides increasingly operating under a different definition of the facts. And as legislatures continued to layer restrictions upon restrictions, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups could argue to the court what Jeanne Mancini, the president of the March for Life, proclaimed to crowds in Washington this year: ''Roe is not settled law.''\par \par \uc2 The momentum that started in 2010 led to the Supreme Court overturning Roe on Friday, even though polls show that a vast majority of Americans supported it, and that most now believe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is morally acceptable. The court's decision lamented that Roe had ''sparked a national controversy that has embittered our popular culture for a half century.'' In fact, that controversy started not so much with Roe but in state houses, and raged hottest over the last decade.\par \par \uc2 ''Women are asking me all the time -- what happened? They have no understanding of how this could be,'' said Cecile Richards, who was president of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  from 2005 to 2018. ''What's happened is not about religion, or morality or unborn babies. It's about politics. Women can't wrap their brains around it. Republicans want to pretend it's about something else. But it's about control -- that's what politics is about.''\par \par \uc2 Living With Compromise\par \par \uc2 Even some of the most ardent anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists did not see the shift coming.\par \par \uc2 In 2009, the country seemed settled around the idea that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be ''safe, legal and rare,'' in the phrase coined by Bill Clinton. States had passed restrictions that lined up with public opinion, requiring parental consent and waiting periods for abortions. Ballot initiatives to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in South Dakota and Colorado had failed in 2008, and another failed in Colorado in 2010.\par \par \uc2 ''At either extreme people were unhappy, but essentially everybody could live with that compromise,'' said Neal Devins, a law professor at William and Mary and the author of a 2009 article in The Yale Law Journal headlined, ''How Planned Parenthood v. Casey (Pretty Much) Settled the \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wars.'' The contentious Casey decision, in 1992, had reaffirmed the central holding of Roe v. Wade -- that states could not ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before a fetus was viable outside the womb, or about 23 weeks -- but allowed states to impose restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  so long as they did not impose an ''undue burden'' on women.\par \par \uc2 For the most part, Professor Devins's article argued, state lawmakers hated talking about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and liked that the court had given them cover to pass some restrictions, but not ones that might contradict public opinion.\par \par \uc2 In May 2009, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  extremist fatally shot Dr. George Tiller, one of the nation's only providers of late-term \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in his church, damaging the image of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups were also riding a string of victories. ''In 2006 and 2008 we made great gains with pro-choice Dems and some pro-choice Republicans,'' Ms. Shipp, at NARAL, recalled. ''We had the largest majorities in the House, we had the majority in the Senate.''\par \par \uc2 They had secured health insurance coverage for birth control in Obamacare, and fended off congressional Republicans' attempts to defund \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''We were dead in the water,'' said \uc2 Chuck Donovan\uc2 , a longtime legislative director for the \uc2 National Right to Life Committee\uc2 . Except to fight over state funding on tax credits for health insurance, ''pro-life didn't have a movement.'' Demographics were moving the country toward a more diverse majority that was presumed to lean left. Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  forces feared the re-election of Barack Obama and six more years of potential appointments to the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 Republican strategists, however, had an eye on the states in the 2010 midterms. In The Wall Street Journal, Karl Rove, the architect of \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 's victories, wrote that a group called the Republican State Leadership Committee was aiming to flip 18 legislative chambers where Democrats were holding the majority by four or fewer seats. Because it was a census year, taking control of state legislatures would give Republicans power over redistricting.\par \par \uc2 The group spent just $30 million -- less than the cost of some Senate races. Republicans won 680 seats, more than the Democrats had won in the post-Watergate election of 1974.\par \par \uc2 In another year, the swing in party control might not have given much hope to anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  forces. They had failed to pass a constitutional amendment banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  even with Republicans controlling Congress and the White House in the 1980s, and failed to get a favorable Casey decision even with Republican appointees dominating the court. A decade later, the party had set up a ''big tent'' to include \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters, with Roger Stone, the future Trump operative, advising Republicans for Choice, a group led by his wife.\par \par \uc2 The Republicans of 2010 had been backed by the Tea Party, which had swelled in protest over Obamacare in the summer of 2009. Polls found that about 60 percent of Tea Party supporters thought that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be illegal, and about half identified as part of the religious right, a coalition that had belatedly come together after Roe, in 1980, to oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Over the next year, Republican-led chambers passed more restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  than in any year since the Roe decision in 1973.\par \par \uc2 Many of the earliest bills reflected the bitterness of the Obamacare debate. They prohibited insurance coverage of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and eliminated public funding for \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Others added delays for women seeking abortions: ultrasounds, counseling from anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis pregnancy centers and waiting periods. South Dakota's 24-hour waiting period became 72 hours. Six states mandated that parental notification for abortions be notarized. North Carolina required women to receive counseling that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  could damage their ability to get pregnant and cause lasting mental health consequences -- claims unsupported by medical studies.\par \par \uc2 Many of the bills copied model legislation from anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups such as \uc2 Americans United for Life\uc2  or the \uc2 National Right to Life Committee\uc2  that had stalled in committee in previous years. Now they were gliding through two Republican-controlled chambers to the governor's pen. ''It wasn't that they were coming up with new ideas,'' said Donna Crane, then the policy director at NARAL. ''It's just that the goalie got pulled.''\par \par \uc2 The Red Wave\par \par \uc2 The new laws accelerated the strategy that anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups had embraced since their failure to secure the constitutional amendment against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the 1980s: If they could not topple Roe in one nationwide swoop, they would go state by state passing laws that chipped away at what it allowed. By destabilizing it, they would undermine its legitimacy.\par \par \uc2 A young Justice Department lawyer named \uc2 Samuel A. Alito Jr\uc2 . had articulated this strategy in a 1985 memo advising the Reagan administration to enter an amicus brief seeking to uphold \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions in Pennsylvania. The department ultimately had to push to overturn Roe v. Wade, he wrote, but a ''frontal assault'' on the decision's holding on viability -- that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was protected until the fetus could survive outside the womb -- risked the Supreme Court ''summarily rejecting'' an outright ban. Instead, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents had to make the case that more incremental laws were ''eminently reasonable and legitimate.''\par \par \uc2 But there was a divide in the movement between those who argued for incrementalism and those who wanted the frontal assault. James Bopp Jr., a conservative lawyer who had fought \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since Roe and in 1980 wrote the first anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  plank into the Republican platform, tried to reinforce the message for discipline in 2007, as restive anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups pushed for outright bans on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  starting at conception. These efforts were only ''doomed to expensive failure,'' Mr. Bopp wrote in a memo that year, and would only end up enriching \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  and other groups that would win legal fees in court challenges.\par \par \uc2 More damaging, he wrote, ''there is the potential danger that the court might actually make things worse than they presently are,'' by reinforcing Roe's declaration of a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 By the end of 2011, five states -- Alabama, Idaho, Indiana, Kansas and Oklahoma -- had followed Nebraska's lead to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before viability. They prohibited \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 20 weeks of pregnancy, asserting that the fetus could then feel pain.\par \par \uc2 ''One of the dynamics that drove this was a kind of frustration,'' Mr. Bopp said. '''Look how long it's been, we need to force the court.' There was a natural frustration which makes people willing to take more risks, or do things differently.''\par \par \uc2 In March 2011, the new Republican supermajority in the Arizona Legislature passed the nation's first bill banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the woman's decision was based on the sex or race of the fetus, one of many that aimed to portray \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers as sexist, racist and supporters of eugenics. The bill had been pushed by the Center for Arizona Policy, which had been founded in 1995 largely to support anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislation.\par \par \uc2 The bill's sponsors named it the \uc2 Susan B. Anthony\uc2  and Frederick Douglass Prenatal Nondiscrimination Act. On the floor, they cited a few studies -- one including just 65 women -- that suggested some immigrants from countries such as India and China, where there has been a traditional preference for sons, faced pressure to abort female fetuses.\par \par \uc2 ''This was an issue that didn't exist,'' said \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , a Democrat elected to the Arizona House that year. ''But how do you fight against that without sounding like you actually support people aborting babies because they are a race or gender that they don't want?'' If the bill was ''ludicrous,'' she said, ''it kept the issue at the forefront, made it look like the Democrats were supporting these extreme measures, and why wouldn't you want to stop them?''\par \par \uc2 The law punished providers, making violations a felony. Within two years, five other states had copied it, and North Dakota started a trend of bans on abortions based on diagnoses of Down syndrome and other genetic abnormalities.\par \par \uc2 Sue Liebel, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activist in Indiana at that time who became state director of what is now Susan B. Anthony Pro-Life America, founded in 1992 to support anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  candidates, called it ''pent-up demand after years of pro-choice control.''\par \par \uc2 ''This was a red wave -- it kind of rolled across the country,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 By early 2012, a report from the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , which supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, declared a ''seismic shift'' and the end of consensus on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . More than half of women of childbearing age lived in states the report deemed hostile to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights -- 15 million more than a decade earlier. A chunk of states in the Midwest, and the entire South, had moved to hostile ground.\par \par \uc2 Americans United for Life\uc2  noted the Guttmacher ratings as an accomplishment in its own annual report that year, giving Kansas and Arizona, two newly ''hostile'' states, awards for ''most improved.''\par \par \uc2 Growing Political Divide\par \par \uc2 Many of the new laws were bucking public opinion. Polls at the time showed that more than half of Americans thought that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal if a child would be born with a life-threatening illness or ''mentally disabled,'' or when the mother's mental health was endangered. A majority did not support blocking federal funding for abortions. And three-quarters thought there should be an exception to save the life of the mother even in the last three months of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 In 2011, even as their State Legislature was tightening restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Mississippi voters rejected a ballot initiative on ''fetal personhood'' that would have declared that life begins at conception.\par \par \uc2 But polls also showed the issue becoming more partisan. Increasingly, Republicans were identifying as ''pro-life'' and Democrats as ''pro-choice.'' Support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights had once depended largely on education and economic status, but now depended mostly on party affiliation, with Democrats moving sharply toward supporting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 And while Republicans and anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  forces were increasingly working in concert to turn the states to their advantage, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters accused Democrats of all but giving up on local elections.\par \par \uc2 ''On the far right, they realized that the most lasting impact of 2010 would be in the states,'' said Daniel Squadron, a former New York state senator and the executive director of the States Project, which was founded by Democrats in 2017 to try to win back control of legislatures. ''On our side, state power was a footnote. The lesson we took was 'Focus more on midterms'; the lesson they took was 'Wield power in states.' And today, both sides are reaping what we sowed.''\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups lacked the infrastructure their opponents had in the states. NARAL had cut its number of state affiliates nearly in half between 1991 and 2011. And with Democrats in the glow of winning Congress in 2006 and electing the nation's first Black president two years later, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups were having trouble convincing big donors and grass-roots supporters alike that Roe was in trouble.\par \par \uc2 Donors liked to support congressional and presidential elections, and tended to go away when they perceived that the threat had disappeared. ''When you were trying to convince them they had to put money into Kansas or Nebraska, they were like, 'That's futile,''' said Nancy Keenan, the president of NARAL at the time.\par \par \uc2 Opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights had always proved easier to mobilize than supporters. In polls and focus groups, NARAL asked women who were sympathetic to its cause what it would take to get them to be more active in protecting Roe v. Wade. ''Consistently,'' Ms. Keenan said, ''We received answers saying, 'If they overturn it.'''\par \par \uc2 Some younger activists were pushing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups to stop apologizing for or seeking compromise on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . To the new generation, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was health care, and bodily autonomy was not something to be compromised. The Democratic Party platform in 2012 left in ''safe'' and ''legal'' but took out ''rare.''\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups exploited this, portraying the Democrats' position on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as anytime, anywhere, under any circumstance, and paid for with government funds. By contrast, said Marjorie Dannenfelser, the president of \uc2 Susan B. Anthony\uc2  Pro-Life America, a 20-week ban looked reasonable, in keeping with what polls showed Americans wanted.\par \par \uc2 ''Two thousand ten was the year that the light came on about the reality of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law in the nation,'' she said. ''It was the year that the polarization between the extreme \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  absolutism and the Republican Party position was a winning contrast. It's the first year that sitting officeholders could see that this issue really helps us. And it just got stronger every election cycle because we would not relent with that contrast.''\par \par \uc2 Republicans running in right-leaning districts backed increasingly strict laws to appeal to reliable anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  voters and avoid primary challenges. By 2016, one analysis found not a single Republican state legislator willing to identify as ''pro-choice.''\par \par \uc2 In the nearly 50 years between the Roe decision and its reversal on Friday, states enacted 1,380 restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Almost half -- 46 percent -- were enacted since 2011.\par \par \uc2 A Quest for Voters\par \par \uc2 Professor Devins, of William and Mary, revisited the question of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politics in 2016, this time in a paper for The Vanderbilt Law Review. He stuck by his 2009 assessment, but the middle ground he had written of approvingly then had disappeared. ''Today, red state political actors are not interested in compromise,'' Professor Devins wrote in 2016. With the rightward shift in the Republican Party, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights had become about partisan advantage, along with voter identification laws, tax reform and elimination of public-sector unions.\par \par \uc2 In 2015, the Florida Senate had taken just one hour to approve a 24-hour waiting period on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with Republicans rejecting all eight amendments offered by Democrats. Wisconsin had passed its ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 20 weeks without a single Democrat. And a bill in Texas to prohibit abortions based on fetal abnormality was brought up for a committee vote after the House had recessed and Democrats were absent.\par \par \uc2 ''It's conventional wisdom to say that the court's decision in Roe caused the polarization over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' said Reva Siegel, a law professor at Yale. ''But the court did not cause that polarization. It was the Republican Party's quest for voters -- political party competition -- that savaged Roe. Once the attack on Roe was underway, the defense needed to be full tilt in politics as well as in the courts -- and in all political arenas, state, local and federal. Because over time the attack on Roe has become more than an attack on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; it has become an attack on democracy.''\par \par \uc2 By 2019, proponents of the incremental strategy for undoing Roe were losing to those who wanted the frontal attack. With two new conservative justices, the Supreme Court was tilted toward the latter. Momentum was on their side, and states began passing legislation designed to force the court to act. Twenty-week bans had led to 18-week bans, eight-week bans, and now six-week bans.\par \par \uc2 Still, many anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  leaders predicted that the Supreme Court would continue to chip away rather than strike down the decision. Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , the court's only Black justice, had criticized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as eugenics. So the next big case, many reasoned, would concern one of the bans on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in cases of genetic abnormalities.\par \par \uc2 Instead, the court took the case regarding a Mississippi law passed in 2018 that banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Republicans had taken control of the Mississippi Legislature in 2011 -- the first time since Reconstruction that Democrats had not controlled the House.\par \par \uc2 ''It became much easier to navigate these waters and get restrictions passed,'' said Senator Joey Fillingane, a Republican who sponsored numerous anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bills after being elected in 2000. ''Even though these state Democrats were social conservatives, they still had to answer to the national Democratic Party and received pressure to limit the number of restrictions.''\par \par \uc2 Republicans got smarter about strategy, he said, having women appear as the chief sponsors of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislation to avoid criticism that ''this is just a bunch of white guys trying to tell women how to do their life.''\par \par \uc2 During the two decades when Democrats controlled the chambers, the two parties had often worked together. As the 2011 elections approached, David Baria, then a state representative, recalled discussing strategy with a fellow Democrat who had served for 35 years and was poised to become House speaker. To hold the House, the colleague argued, they would have to make it safe for Democrats to vote for anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws and against gun control. Democrats had to be able to go back home and appeal to voters as moderates.\par \par \uc2 But the moderates had disappeared. His fellow Democrat lost. Mr. Baria took over as the leader of the new Democratic minority, and party lines were harder than ever. The Republicans were using a text messaging service to convey how members should vote. Soon, he adopted the same for the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Still, Mr. Baria thought the vote on the ban in 2018 might be different. Proponents offered no scientific justification for cutting off \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at that gestational age. The state had already restricted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  so much that there was only one clinic remaining, and it performed so few abortions after 15 weeks that the provider had declined to file a legal challenge to a 20-week ban. And the proposed 15-week ban included no exceptions for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 Proponents of the bill had mobilized callers, and by 10 to one those who called urged a yes vote on the ban. ''We're a conservative state, but what I would argue is that the majority of callers didn't understand that there were no exceptions for rape or incest,'' Mr. Baria said. ''The message is, are you for or are you against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Baria expected that at least a few Republicans would vote against the bill because it did not have rape or incest exceptions. Instead, even amendments to add those exceptions failed.\par \par \uc2 Four Democrats in the House and four in the Senate crossed the aisle to vote for the bill. Not a single Republican voted against it.\par \par \uc2 Demonstrators at the Supreme Court on Friday. In the new and shifting post-Roe world, \uc2  was banned in at least nine states. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ANNA ROSE LAYDEN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A1); A procedure room inside a San Antonio clinic. The three years following the 2010 elections would result in 205 anti-\uc2  laws across the country, more than in the entire previous decade. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ILANA PANICH-LINSMAN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A20); An \uc2  clinic in Wichita. Kansas was among five states in 2011 -- Alabama, Idaho, Indiana and Oklahoma were the others -- that followed Nebraska's lead to ban \uc2  before viability. (PHOTOGRAPH BY CRAIG HACKER FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A21) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220626ei6q0005h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
