\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Patients and States Turn Focus to Pills\b0\par\par\uc2 By Pam Belluck \par \uc2 1,809 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Demand for medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is surging, setting the stage for new legal battles.\par \par \uc2 In the hours after the Supreme Court released its decision overturning the legal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the United States, nearly 100 requests for appointments flowed into Just the Pill, a nonprofit organization that arranges for patients to obtain \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills in several states.\par \par \uc2 That was about four times the usual daily number of appointment requests for the organization, and many came from patients in Texas and other states that quickly halted abortions after the court ruling.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, already used in more than half of recent abortions in the U.S., are becoming even more sought-after in the aftermath of Roe v. Wade being overturned, and they will likely be at the center of the legal battles that are expected to unfold as about half the states ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and others take steps to increase access.\par \par \uc2 The method, known as medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is authorized by the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  for use in the first 10 weeks of pregnancy. It involves taking two different drugs, 24 to 48 hours apart, to stop the development of a pregnancy and then to cause contractions similar to a miscarriage to expel the fetus, a process that usually causes bleeding similar to a heavy period.\par \par \uc2 Many patients choose medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  because it is less expensive, less invasive and affords more privacy than surgical abortions -- the pills can be received by mail and taken at home, or anywhere, after an initial consultation with a doctor by video, phone, in person or even just by filling out an online form.\par \par \uc2 The patient must participate in the consultation from a state that allows \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even if it simply involves being on the phone in a car just over the border. The IP address of the computer or phone they use allows the clinic to identify where they are.\par \par \uc2 For states that ban all forms of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to provide significant enforcement challenges. It is one thing to shut down a clinic; it is much harder to police activities like sending or receiving pills through the mail or traveling to a state where pills are legal to have a consultation and pick them up, legal experts say.\par \par \uc2 ''When people say we're going back to the days before Roe, there's no such thing as a time machine -- we have a very different pharmaceutical landscape,'' said Katie Watson, a constitutional scholar and medical ethicist at the Feinberg School of Medicine at \uc2 Northwestern University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws beginning to take effect in numerous conservative states ban all forms of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In addition, 19 states already had laws barring using telemedicine for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Texas recently enacted a law prohibiting sending \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills through the mail. So groups and some state governments that support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are mobilizing to help patients obtain the pills in states where they are legal.\par \par \uc2 Since October 2020, Just the Pill has provided more than 2,500 telemedicine consultations with doctors to supply \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills by mail to patients in Colorado, Minnesota, Montana and Wyoming. Within a few days, it plans to deploy in Colorado the first of what will become ''a fleet of mobile clinics'' to park along state borders, providing consultations for medication abortions and dispensing pills, said Dr. Julie Amaon, the organization's medical director.\par \par \uc2 Called ''\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Delivered,'' the clinic-on-wheels program, which will also provide surgical abortions for patients who prefer it or are too far along in pregnancy for a medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is designed to reach patients from nearby states like Texas, Oklahoma and South Dakota that quickly outlawed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the court decision, as well as other states like Utah that are expected to ban or sharply restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''By operating on state borders, we will reduce travel burdens for patients in states with bans or severe limits,'' Dr. Amaon said. ''And by moving beyond a traditional brick-and-mortar clinic, our mobile clinics can quickly adapt to the courts, state legislatures, and the markets, going wherever the need is.''\par \par \uc2 Similar medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers are also planning for an influx. Hey Jane, an organization that has served nearly 10,000 patients in California, Colorado, Illinois, New Mexico, New York and Washington, plans to expand to more states. ''We've ramped up our team to accommodate this significant increase in demand,'' said its chief executive, Kiki Freedman.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups are trying to counter the rise in interest in medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  by claiming it is unsafe, calling it ''chemical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .'' James Studnicki, vice president of data analytics at Charlotte Lozier Institute, an arm of Susan B. Anthony Pro-Life America, said on Friday that ''the safety of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill is greatly exaggerated,'' and called the rise in medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ''a serious public health threat.''\par \par \uc2 Much remains unknown about how states that ban all or most abortions will try to enforce their laws in cases of medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But as the Biden administration scrambled to react to the court ruling, two cabinet members swiftly released statements vowing to protect the right to take medicines that had been approved by the federal government.\par \par \uc2 ''We stand unwavering in our commitment to ensure every American has access to health care and the ability to make decisions about health care -- including the right to safe and legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that has been approved by the F.D.A. for over 20 years,'' \uc2 Xavier Becerra\uc2 , the secretary of Health and Human Services, said in his statement.\par \par \uc2 In another statement, Merrick B. Garland, the attorney general, referred specifically to the first drug in the medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimen, mifepristone. In December, the F.D.A. made access to it significantly easier by permanently lifting the requirement that patients obtain mifepristone by visiting an authorized clinic or doctor in person.\par \par \uc2 ''We stand ready to work with other arms of the federal government that seek to use their lawful authorities to protect and preserve access to reproductive care,'' Mr. Garland said. ''In particular, the F.D.A. has approved the use of the medication mifepristone. States may not ban mifepristone based on disagreement with the F.D.A.'s expert judgment about its safety and efficacy.''\par \par \uc2 But it is unclear what the Justice Department can do. Some legal scholars have argued that federal drug approval pre-empts state actions to restrict a drug's use. Others say that has only applied to cases where a state claims that safety or efficacy is an issue.\par \par \uc2 ''Today, the Supreme Court said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it can only be regulated in sort of a health and safety way when it's permitted, but it can be completely banned,'' Professor Watson said.\par \par \uc2 As a result, she said, the ability of the federal government to assert that the F.D.A.'s approval takes precedence over state laws ''is limited, given, traditionally, states get to regulate the practice of medicine.''\par \par \uc2 Legal experts said there might be other ways for the Justice Department to become involved in fighting medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, such as contesting laws that bar mailing pills, since the mail is under federal oversight.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the F.D.A. took a cautious stance, saying in a statement, ''We have not had an opportunity to review the opinion, but we do note that F.D.A.'s independent and regulatory decisions are based on science and facts.''\par \par \uc2 The agency added that ''patients should have access to medications that are safe and effective for their F.D.A.-approved use.''\par \par \uc2 Medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  became legal in the United States in 2000, when mifepristone was approved by the F.D.A. The agency imposed tight restrictions on the drug, many of which remain in place. But access to the method increased in 2016, when the F.D.A. expanded the time frame within which the drug could be taken -- from seven weeks to 10 weeks into a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Major medical groups cite years of data showing that medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is safe. For example, a research program that the F.D.A. allowed to provide telemedicine consultations and send pills by mail reported that 95 percent of the 1,157 abortions that occurred through the program between May 2016 and September 2020 were completed without requiring any follow-up procedure. Patients made 70 visits to emergency rooms or urgent care centers, with 10 instances of serious complications, the study reported.\par \par \uc2 As conservative states began passing more laws restricting access to surgical abortions, more patients opted for pills, especially because they could be taken in the privacy of one's home.\par \par \uc2 The Covid pandemic fueled that trend. The \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a research organization that supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, reported that in 2020, medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  accounted for 54 percent of all abortions.\par \par \uc2 As patients look for ways to obtain the pills, some are expected to turn to international websites like Aid Access, a European organization that the F.D.A. has tried -- so far unsuccessfully -- to stop from mailing pills to the United States, further complicating enforcement efforts.\par \par \uc2 Mary Ziegler, a law professor at the \uc2 University of California, Davis\uc2 , who has written widely on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said in an interview last month that there might be attempts by states that ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prosecute doctors and other health providers in other states who provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services like consultations and pills to their residents, or to try to block organizations or funds that give financial help to patients to travel to other states.\par \par \uc2 States where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains legal are mobilizing to increase access stifle legal assaults from other states. Connecticut passed a bill that would prevent \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers from being extradited to other states, bar Connecticut authorities from cooperating with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  investigations from a patient's home state and allow Connecticut residents who are sued under another state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provision to countersue. Legislation in California would provide financial assistance to patients traveling from other states to obtain abortions and increase the number of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 , in an opinion concurring with the Supreme Court decision, suggested that patients who traveled to other states to receive an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be protected by the constitutional right to interstate travel.\par \par \uc2 So far, most states that restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  have long adhered to a principle of targeting providers and others who help patients, but not the patients themselves. Professor Ziegler said it was possible that could also change because, in circumstances where the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  takes place outside state boundaries, ''there may be absolutely no one else in that state to go after but the patient.''\par \par \uc2 Katie Benner contributed reporting from Washington.\par \par \uc2 Medication \uc2  is authorized by the Food and Drug Administration for patients to use in the first 10 weeks of pregnancy. (PHOTOGRAPH BY REUTERS/EVELYN HOCKSTEIN) (A19) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220626ei6q00055\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
