\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Nation Faces Changed  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Landscape\b0\par\par\uc2 By Julie Bosman \par \uc2 1,572 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Around the country, a patchwork of laws was taking hold amid protest and celebration.\par \par \uc2 A new and rapidly shifting reality took hold across America on Saturday as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a basic legal right for nearly a half century, was outlawed in some states, and the initial bursts of elation and shock from the overturning of Roe v. Wade gave way to action.\par \par \uc2 At \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics across the country, providers hastily canceled appointments out of fear of prosecution, and stunned women abruptly made plans to cross state lines into places where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was still allowed -- traveling from Missouri to Illinois, from Wisconsin to Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 In Arkansas, where a trigger law banning abortions went into effect on Friday, 17 patients had been scheduled for abortions on Friday at Little Rock Family Planning Services, but none were performed before the Supreme Court's decision shut down operations. About 30 more patients had been scheduled for an ultrasound and consultation that was required under Arkansas' previous law before women could get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Yellowhammer Fund, which is based in Alabama and provides financial support to women seeking abortions, has received an influx of calls in the last day from people confused about the changing laws and seeking guidance and money to travel elsewhere for abortions.\par \par \uc2 ''People who had appointments for next week no longer have appointments,'' said Laurie Bertram Roberts, the executive director of the fund. ''The person who runs the call line is very overwhelmed.''\par \par \uc2 Legal experts confronted a quickly changing landscape of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws. In the newly redrawn map of the United States that was taking shape on Saturday, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was banned in at least nine states, prompting vows of swift enforcement from officials in conservative states. Prosecutors in liberal states and counties responded with defiance, saying they would not violate their own values by pursuing criminal cases against doctors who had performed abortions.\par \par \uc2 Demonstrations continued to roil cities across the country. Americans said they were steeling themselves for a fight in the wake of the court's decision, whether that meant pushing for still more restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or working to elect politicians in the midterm elections who favor \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 ''I fear for my child. I worry that she isn't going to have choice,'' said Abbye Putterman, 36, who stood outside an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic in Overland Park, Kan., on Saturday and spoke of the impact the decision could have on her 12-year-old daughter. ''I feel like a whole bunch of white men are trying to decide what my daughter should do. Those men don't know anything about what it's like to carry a child -- what pregnancy does to your body.''\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still legal in Kansas but was banned in neighboring Missouri on Friday. In August, a ballot initiative will ask voters in Kansas to decide whether the State Constitution should continue to protect the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ms. Putterman was at the clinic to show support to the women receiving services there, while anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protesters gathered outside.\par \par \uc2 ''We don't believe in moral compromise, and we don't want them to be guilty of murder,'' said Valley Scharping, 26, who stood on the sidewalk. He held a sign that read ''Love your preborn neighbor as yourself.''\par \par \uc2 On Saturday, President Biden spoke of the Roe decision. ''Jill and I know how painful and devastating the decision is for so many Americans,'' he said, adding that the administration would focus on states and ''how they administer it and whether or not they violate other laws.''\par \par \uc2 Some states imposed new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions on Saturday, and others tried to accelerate timelines for the bans to take place.\par \par \uc2 After the Supreme Court handed control over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions back to the states, at least nine states that are home to roughly 40 million people quickly put bans in place. Other \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  prohibitions that had been passed in anticipation of a post-Roe legal landscape were working their way through the courts.\par \par \uc2 In Idaho, North Dakota and Texas, officials said they would wait the 30 days stipulated in their laws for their so-called trigger laws to take effect, banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In Ohio, a law outlawing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after about six weeks of pregnancy took effect after a federal judge lifted an injunction that had blocked the law for the past three years. Gov. \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2  reiterated his opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday, saying he believed ''that the life of a human being is at stake and we have an obligation to protect that innocent life.''\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood Association of Utah and the American Civil Liberties Union of Utah filed a lawsuit in state court on Saturday seeking to block the state's ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which went into effect on Friday. The lawsuit argues that the ban violates several protections in the state's constitution, including the right to determine family composition. Planned Parenthood in the state said that it had to stop performing abortions immediately after the ban went into effect and that it would have to cancel 55 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  appointments scheduled for next week unless temporary relief was granted.\par \par \uc2 In many states, residents were left to grapple with a confusing array of pronouncements as local and state officials clashed over the legalities of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions and how they would be enforced.\par \par \uc2 In Tennessee, Herbert Slatery, the attorney general, filed an emergency motion on Friday asking a court to lift an injunction and allow a ban on abortions after six weeks to be made law.\par \par \uc2 ''After nearly 50 years, today's decision gives the people of Tennessee a say on what the Court called 'a profound moral issue,''' he said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 But Glenn Funk, the district attorney in Nashville, said in a statement that he would not prosecute doctors performing abortions or women who choose such a procedure.\par \par \uc2 ''I will use my constitutional powers to protect women, health providers and those making personal health decisions,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Officials of Mississippi's only \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic, which was the center of the case decided on Friday by the Supreme Court, predicted that conservative activists would soon seek limits to rights related to birth control and same-sex marriage.\par \par \uc2 Diane Derzis, who owns the clinic, the Jackson Women's Health Organization, said it would most likely remain open for 10 days after the Supreme Court's decision, before shutting its doors when a new law is expected to take effect in that state.\par \par \uc2 ''It has begun,'' she said. ''In the next few days, weeks and year, you will see half of the states have no \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services. We are continuing to do services. We are not laying down.''\par \par \uc2 In states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains legal, leaders promised to reinforce protections.\par \par \uc2 Governors in California, Oregon and Washington issued a joint ''commitment to reproductive freedom,'' saying they would welcome people who sought abortions in their states and push back on efforts by other state governments to prosecute people who did so.\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 J.B. Pritzker\uc2  of Illinois, a Democrat, called for a special session for lawmakers to strengthen \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, anticipating that women from other states would be flocking to Illinois for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services.\par \par \uc2 At a Planned Parenthood clinic in Waukegan, Ill., just miles from the Wisconsin border, a group of about 20 anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protesters stood with signs and prayed on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 The clinic was opened in 2020 in anticipation that Roe would be overturned and Wisconsin would ban abortions, said Mary Jane Maharry, a spokeswoman for Planned Parenthood of Illinois. ''We do have enough staff to meet the needs today and we are working at increasing our staff to meet the anticipated surge of 20,000 to 30,000 additional out-of-state patients per year,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 In Charleston, W. Va., the state's lone \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic ended all appointments, fearing that an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban from the 19th century was suddenly enforceable again after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 One of the appointments had just been made on Thursday by a 21-year-old pregnant woman in West Virginia who had weighed whether she was ready to have a child and decided that she was not.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, a clinic employee called to tell the woman, who spoke on condition of anonymity because she feared her parents would disown her if they knew she was planning to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , that her appointment would be canceled.\par \par \uc2 ''When I went to bed, I had my appointment and everything was set,'' she said, ''and then today it's like pre-1973.''\par \par \uc2 Reporting was contributed by Nicholas Bogel-Burroughs, Aurelien Breeden, Robert Chiarito, Emily Cochrane, Jimmie E. Gates, Carey Gillam, Victoria Kim and Erica Sweeney.\par \par \uc2 Reporting was contributed by Nicholas Bogel-Burroughs, Aurelien Breeden, Robert Chiarito, Emily Cochrane, Jimmie E. Gates, Carey Gillam, Victoria Kim and Erica Sweeney.\par \par \uc2 Demonstrations erupted in New York, above, and in dozens of other cities nationwide on Friday. The police in Arizona used tear gas on one rally that turned chaotic. (PHOTOGRAPH BY JEENAH MOON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Anti-\uc2  protesters in Salt Lake City, above left, and \uc2  rights advocates outside the Supreme Court, right, on Friday. In some states, doctors immediately halted \uc2  procedures and canceled appointments, forcing some women to travel out of state. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY KIM RAFF FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES; SHURAN HUANG FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A19) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220626ei6q00054\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
