\par\par \uc2 Sunday Review Desk; SECTSR\par \b \uc2 11 Moderate Voters On Culture War Issues\b0\par\par\uc2 By Patrick Healy and Adrian J. Rivera \par \uc2 2,268 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 7\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 F OR our latest Times Opinion focus group, we wanted to explore how moderate voters who helped elect \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in 2020 were feeling about the state of America. In particular, we wanted to dig into their views about Democratic positions on sensitive issues like \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , guns, trans rights and race. Republicans are hoping to appeal to some moderates in the coming elections by portraying Democrats as exceedingly liberal and judgmental. As we prepared our questions, we thought there might be some tense exchanges and even hostility toward Democratic elites and cultural progressivism. But what we found were some encouraging signs for democracy: The focus group participants didn't dwell on their differences from other Americans, and they sought to be positive about the country and empathetic toward people different from them. On the issue of trans rights, for example, most were accepting and felt that the government shouldn't get involved in personal matters.\par \par \uc2  Even as a transfeminine participant and others wrestled with the issue of trans women competing in women's sports, she said that though she expected ''to hear such horrible things,'' she was happy with the group's openness. As concerned as they were about the economy, inflation and crime, our participants were not forlorn or cynical and wanted to find ways to overcome divisions on issues like guns. One such way might come in the form of an independent candidate in 2024. The voters described feeling trapped by the choice of presidential candidates in 2020 and put forth people like \uc2 Andrew Yang\uc2  and Dwayne Johnson as potential third-party candidates. Trapped though they felt, none of them said they wished they had voted for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Kim 34, Ohio, independent, Asian, sales manager Rasheed 44, California, independent, Asian, sales engineer Steven 51, Arizona, independent, white, maintenance coordinator Angel 39, Florida, independent, Latino, communications Donna 64, Kansas, independent, white, paraprofessional Conrad 55, Missouri, independent, white, construction Caleb 27, Utah, Republican, white, doctoral student Robert 29, Maryland, independent, white, human resources Yana 44, Georgia, independent, Black, teacher Michael 45, Wisconsin, independent, white, floral designer Natalie 44, Illinois, independent, white, librarian P\par \par \uc2 What's the most important issue facing the country today? Michael: Rising gas costs. Steven: Corporate control of our government. Donna: The violence, the hatred, the killing on the street, outside your house, everywhere. Natalie: Gun control, inflation. There's just a lot. Everything is out of control in the United States. Yana: The cost of housing, the cost of groceries. As a teacher, almost everyone I know has a side hustle right now. Kim: At least half my paycheck is dedicated to paying for my daughter's day care. And then the other thing that I think is a major issue is the lack of tolerance that is just rampant with everyone. Within my own family, there are people who won't even talk to each other anymore or go to certain family gatherings. There's no such thing as gray anymore. Everything has to be extremes. Show of hands: How many people here think America's best days are behind us? [One person raises a hand.] Steven: I don't see any way out of this mess that we're in. With social media and all the misinformation and conspiracy theorists running the country, I don't think anything's going to get better. I feel like every problem we have, if the solution isn't going to work perfectly, then it's not worth doing, you know what I mean? So nothing ever gets done. Margie Omero, moderator: Raise your hand if you agree with this: America's best days are ahead of us. [Nine people raise their hands.] Angel:I like to believe in humanity. This is America. People risk their lives, bring their families across borders, illegally or legally, just to be here to have a chance. Conrad: America has gone through worse than what we're going through right now. There was the Great Depression. There were world wars. So I think I'm optimistic, too. Margie Omero: Let me move on to a specific issue that has been coming up recently: \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Where do folks come down on this? Here are three options, and then we can talk about them. Raise your hand if you agree. How many people agree with Option 1? ''I personally support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and think it should be legal.'' [Nine people raise their hands.] How about Option 2? ''I oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  personally but don't think the government should get involved in women's decisions.'' [Four people raise their hands.] What about Option 3? ''I oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and think it should be illegal.'' [Nobody raises a hand.] Yana, tell me why you voted twice. Let's start there. Yana: Because of my faith, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not something that I agree with, but I also don't feel like the government should be able to tell me whether or not I should have a child. Margie Omero: How important is this issue when you're thinking about a candidate and how you're going to vote? Angel: Scale of one to 10, I'd say a seven. You're taking a right away from somebody. Kim:Yeah, it used to not be something that I actually focused on when I was younger. Now that I'm older and have a child, what am I preserving for her when she comes of age? It's not that I don't believe the government should have some rules and regulations in parts of our lives. They should. But if they're willing to limit that much of my own self-autonomy, how much are they going to limit other people's? How do you feel about the gun laws in this country? Give me one word or two. Caleb: My word is ''ugh.'' Margie Omero: Tell me about that. Caleb: I'm a gun owner, and historically I have been pretty against gun control. But I am getting overwhelmed and frustrated with what's been going on in our country, and I want to see a change, and I want to see a drastic change with gun violence. Kim:I think the issue isn't so much that it's the right to have a gun. It's how you are able to obtain said gun. Margie Omero:In the aftermath of a mass shooting, people sometimes say we should bring back the assault weapons ban. Other folks say this should be up to individual states. Where do folks come down on this? Donna: I don't know why you need an assault weapon in general, age or no age. Caleb: I go to the range out here to shoot my gun, and there are a lot of people who have assault rifles. A lot of times, the guys out here treat it as a hobby -- building a gun, making it awesome. It's cool that it's a hobby. But people don't have hobbies of building bombs. That would be upsetting. That would be absurd. And it feels like we're getting to the same territory. Some Americans have said they're worried the Supreme Court could eventually reverse its decision to allow gay marriage. Is this something that concerns you? Angel: What's next? Are we going to go backward and take away women's right to vote? Like, we've already tackled these hurdles and made it right for people to do what they want to do in this country. Why would we want to go back and start taking things away? Michael:Well, I am a part of the L.G.B.T.Q. community, so it would be devastating to me and all my friends' and associates' lives. And it would be throwing us back to the ice age. Patrick Healy: Do you think transgender people should be treated as the gender they say they are? Robert: Absolutely. I mean, why not? Just help me out in understanding what that is, so that I can properly work with you on what you need from me. Should trans girls in high school be allowed to compete on girls' sports teams? How do you see that? Caleb: I think that they deserve compassion and respect and understanding, but I don't know how to offer it in that instance. Michael: I do identify as a transgender person, and I would not ever enter a competition as a woman. I was born biologically male, and that would give me an unfair advantage. I don't want to down-talk or talk against ones that are competing in women's sports. I just -- they are dominating them, and I think it is very unfair to the women that have been in these sports for all of their lives. Patrick Healy: Michael, can I ask, is there anything you think we should keep in mind or that Americans should keep in mind, just based on your perspective and your kind of lived experience here, as we talk about these issues as if they are issues? You also live them. This is your life. Michael: I am transfeminine. I live my life as a female. But I don't call myself a woman, because I was not born biologically female or a woman. I just, I want to be treated as a woman. My pronouns are ''she, her and hers.'' And just the hatred that comes with it is just very unnerving. But this group seems to be very accepting, and I'm over here with a big smile on my face because everybody's so nice. I was expecting to hear such horrible things, you know? Patrick Healy: Thank you, Michael. For everyone: What about teenagers who say they're trans? Should they be allowed to get puberty blockers or hormone therapy and counseling on transition? There is a difference of opinion among some political leaders on this. Natalie: Absolutely, absolutely. It is up to each individual. Politicians should just stay out of it. I mean, as long as I'm not hurting anybody, it is up to me as an individual. Rasheed: I think the only thing I would mention is that if there are situations where those decisions cannot be reversed, that's a complicated aspect for someone who's not yet 18. So I think that that's a tough challenge because all of us, our thinking about what we want to do can change over a period of time. Margie Omero: Let's talk about race and the way we talk about race. How many people say that the country is paying too much attention to the issue of race? [Nobody raises a hand.] How many people say we're not paying enough attention to the issue of race? [Seven people raise their hands.] How many people say we're paying the right amount of attention? [One person raises a hand.] Donna: I don't think we're paying too much attention. I think it's always been pushed under the rug. Caleb: I think that we are getting more attention than ever, and I think more is better. Yana: I think that it's the wrong type of attention sometimes, when you're talking about \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2 . I think all lives matter. I think it's misguided attention and not necessarily for the right things. Patrick Healy: Let's turn to President Biden for a bit. What does he do well? Angel: Helping out the student loan situation, trying to help that is probably one of his bigger gains. Donna: For those who are trying to get a more unified United States, his calmness, his empathy -- it's all a lot of words, but I think he's trying to bring back a little more hope. Patrick Healy: What about the flip side? Anything President Biden has not done well? Rasheed: I think he's just not pacing himself fast enough to respond to issues. I think he takes the long approach. But with social media, you have to move much faster. We're used to that, particularly from Trump, where you get a response right away, good or bad. So things like student loans, whatever he's going to do, why wait? Why hold off? If you could do any one thing to make our country less divided, what would you change? Rasheed: I'd give everybody a passport. Kim: Before they become confirmed in their home religion, whatever they were raised with, everybody should really make an effort to go to other churches, go to other religious organizations or meetings and just see how other people view the world. Robert: Take money out of politics. Donna: In the perfect world, I think we need to make sure that everybody has a good education. Teachers need a lot more pay. Steven: I don't know why every civilized country in this world does ranked-choice voting and we do either/or. It's ridiculous. Angel: Finance classes to bring that wealth gap a little smaller between races. Yana: I'd like to take you and Patrick on the road, and if you do one of these in every community all over the nation, I think we'd really find out that we're not as divided as people think we are. Everybody wants the same things, and only after you sit down and talk to people do you realize that.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220626ei6q0004k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
