\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 How  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Bans Will Ripple Across America\b0\par\par\uc2 By Quoctrung Bui, Claire Cain Miller and Margot Sanger-Katz \par \uc2 1,959 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 22\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 At the start of the month, nearly all women in America lived within a few hours' drive of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic. But with Roe v. Wade overturned Friday, and the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended, clinics are closing quickly close in huge swaths of the country.\par \par \uc2 Now a new set of political fights will begin, playing out in state legislatures and courthouses across America. By the time they are done, a quarter of U.S. women of reproductive age could have to travel more than 200 miles to obtain a legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Under the farthest-reaching scenarios, that number could rise to nearly half.\par \par \uc2 The longer the distance to the nearest clinic, the fewer women make the trip, research has shown.\par \par \uc2 The new analysis of clinic closures -- performed by Caitlin Myers, an economist at \uc2 Middlebury College\uc2  -- underscores how the effects of each state's decisions will spill over borders.\par \par \uc2 In the post-Roe world, a woman's ability to get a legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will depend not only on what is happening in her state, but on what happens in surrounding states, too.\par \par \uc2 Jim Olsen, a Republican legislator in Oklahoma who has helped pass several \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions this year, said that even if some women would still leave the state to seek abortions, many would not. ''Our laws will still save lives,'' he said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  may also become harder to obtain even in states where it remains legal, because clinics may be overwhelmed with out-of-state patients. And states like Colorado, Kansas and Illinois -- which will be surrounded by states with bans -- could become major destinations, seeing a surge of patients. ''We're going to see enormous numbers of women funneling into these states,'' Professor Myers said.\par \par \uc2 In interviews with clinics that expect to remain open across the country, doctors worried about how they would manage.\par \par \uc2 ''I think we will end up over the next months with waiting lists and more patients than we can schedule,'' said Dr. Erin King, executive director of Hope Clinic for Women in Granite City, Ill.\par \par \uc2 Declining Access\par \par \uc2 Currently, 716,000 women of reproductive age -- or about 1 percent -- live more than 200 miles from the nearest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic. But in the coming months, that number will be multiplied many times.\par \par \uc2 Thirteen states already have laws on the books, known as trigger bans, to outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  now that Roe is overturned.\par \par \uc2 Once all those trigger bans are in effect, the number of women who are that far from a clinic will rise to 9 million.\par \par \uc2 Five of those bans had taken effect by the end of the day on Friday. Others are designed to start 30 days later, or require a government official to sign off. Texas' trigger law won't activate for 30 days, but officials there said they had already begun enforcing another law banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from before Roe was decided.\par \par \uc2 Even under Roe, some states had restrictions that made it hard for clinics to operate. But until recently, each state had at least one clinic. That changed after a draft Supreme Court opinion leaked in May indicating that the court intended to overturn Roe. Clinics in Oklahoma, South Dakota and elsewhere stopped offering \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in anticipation of the decision.\par \par \uc2 Clinics have closed in two additional states because those states still had laws on the books from before Roe. An \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban is seen as probable in another nine states, either because of similar laws or because they have legislatures that have passed stringent \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions more recently.\par \par \uc2 Bans in these states would raise the number of women more than 200 miles away from a clinic to 17 million.\par \par \uc2 Some of these states will ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  immediately. Wisconsin's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics have said they will close after the decision because of an 1849 law that's still on the books. Alabama's more recent ban was paused by a court because of its now-irrelevant conflict with Roe.\par \par \uc2 In other states in this group, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  may remain legal for some time, but their legal and political histories suggest that bans are more likely than not to pass eventually. In Iowa and Arizona, the legal status of their older anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws may take months of court battles to resolve. In Nebraska, a recent effort to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  failed by two votes, but legislators there are poised to try again after one of the legislators who voted no was replaced after his death.\par \par \uc2 And then there are three states -- Florida, Virginia and Kansas -- that could have an outsize impact, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 's future is uncertain. If these states banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the number of women far from a clinic would rise to 24 million.\par \par \uc2 In this new post-Roe world, each of these three states could become linchpins of access, making their legislatures' choices particularly consequential. The legal uncertainty could also make it harder for clinics to expand capacity for out-of-state patients; providers may be wary of investing in larger clinics or new staff if they don't know for how long they'll be allowed to operate.\par \par \uc2 Florida recently passed a law outlawing most abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy, but some legislators want to go further. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Virginia seems safe for now with Democrats in control of the State Senate, but it might not be if Republicans retake control in the next election. The Kansas Supreme Court has found that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is protected under the state Constitution, but a constitutional amendment to change that is up for a vote this summer.\par \par \uc2 Tory Marie Blew, a Republican legislator in Kansas who has led efforts to pass a constitutional amendment to end \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections, said she was partly driven by the fact that women around the Midwest may turn to Kansas if their states ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . During the early months of the coronavirus pandemic, when several nearby states closed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics for public health reasons, out-of-state women flooded into Kansas, increasing the number of abortions provided in the state in 2020 by nearly 10 percent.\par \par \uc2 ''I personally don't want Kansas to be an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  destination state, as it currently is,'' she said, adding that her district was peppered with purple signs saying ''Value Them Both'' -- referring to mothers and children -- in support of the amendment.\par \par \uc2 Measuring Impact\par \par \uc2 As clinics start to close, having just one nearby may not always be enough to ensure that a woman can get an appointment, because \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is time-sensitive and surges in demand are expected.\par \par \uc2 Even before the decision, the closest clinic for many women in the South had been American Family Planning, in Pensacola, Fla. It was the only \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider in the Florida Panhandle, and regularly served patients from six states. But at the end of May, the state closed the clinic after three women in a year were hospitalized for complications from second-trimester abortions, with the state saying the clinic did not file the requisite paperwork. For now, the next nearest clinic is around 180 miles away, in Tallahassee.\par \par \uc2 ''If it doesn't reopen, all those women are going to have to travel that much further,'' said Julie Gallagher, the clinic's lawyer.\par \par \uc2 Even in very large cities, there may be only a few clinics within a six-hour drive once bans go into effect.\par \par \uc2 Research has shown that when clinics close and driving distances increase, there are corresponding drops in the number of women obtaining abortions. Professor Myers estimated that an increase of 100 miles of driving distance reduced \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates by 20 percent.\par \par \uc2 Most of the reductions would be among poor women who are unable to travel long distances, because they are less able to overcome the obstacles of cost, transportation, child care and other logistics, research shows.\par \par \uc2 The number of legal abortions may fall further if the remaining clinics cannot meet the demand of women traveling from states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is banned. There is no precedent for closings in more than a dozen states at once.\par \par \uc2 Still, abortions may not decline as much as it might appear. Some women will find other ways to end their pregnancies, including illegal ones, and those abortions will be harder to count. One main alternative is pills that can safely end a pregnancy, which can be illicitly purchased online from another country or sent in the mail from a state where they remain legal. After Texas banned all but the earliest abortions last year, around 1,100 women a month began ordering pills from overseas, three times as many as before.\par \par \uc2 But in the end, there will almost certainly be fewer abortions, the long-term goal of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement.\par \par \uc2 ''We've always been very hopeful,'' said Kristan Hawkins, the president of Students for Life of America, adding that it would result in ''thousands of lives saved.''\par \par \uc2 Surging Demand\par \par \uc2 Even before Roe was overturned, the consequences of declining \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  capacity were apparent. When the restrictions in Texas went into effect in the fall, patients left for neighboring states, mostly Oklahoma. Oklahomans then had trouble getting appointments, so some went to other states. In late May, Oklahoma banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''Think of a pond when you throw a rock in it and you see those ripples -- that's what's happening,'' said Dr. Kristina Tocce, the medical director for Planned Parenthood of the Rocky Mountains, which operates clinics in Colorado, New Mexico and Nevada, and is already treating a growing number of patients from out of state. ''You have so many patients competing for a finite number of appointments.''\par \par \uc2 Some clinics in Illinois already had a majority of their patients coming from Missouri, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been nearly inaccessible since 2019. Now those Illinois clinics are seeing patients daily from as far as Texas.\par \par \uc2 ''We have been preparing for this for a long time,'' said Dr. Colleen McNicholas of Planned Parenthood in Fairview Heights, Ill., just across the border from St. Louis. ''We opened it with the need to serve Missouri, but we really did so with an eye toward this moment.''\par \par \uc2 The clinic plans to operate 10 hours a day instead of eight, and open one or two Sundays a month. Nearby, Hope Clinic for Women in Granite City, Ill., hired twice as many doctors and reworked the clinic's space to build more exam rooms. Together, the two clinics created an informational center staffed with people who can help patients with the logistics of getting to Illinois, including hotels and child care. Neither clinic will turn away women who can't pay.\par \par \uc2 ''Do I think that's going to be enough to see all the patients who need to come?'' said Dr. King, the director at Hope. ''No, but we had to start somewhere.''\par \par \uc2 In parts of the country where opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  runs strong, doctors have begun considering this new future. Dr. John Lee, who has been practicing obstetrics and gynecology at the Santa Rosa Women's Center in Milton, Fla., for 33 years, says when patients ask about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he first suggests counseling and talks to them about other options. But if they choose to proceed, he directs them to the clinic. Now, he said, he's not sure where to send them.\par \par \uc2 He's most concerned about those who cannot travel, for economic or logistical reasons. ''Will you have these backwoods deals people used to do?'' he said. ''I hope not, because people would get hurt. But you have the old adage: Desperate people will do desperate things. What I would want to do is if they're still adamant about going the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  route, counsel them to do it in a safe way.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220626ei6q0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
