\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 In an Instant, Midterm Contests Are Scrambled\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katie Glueck \par \uc2 1,689 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Democrats hope the fall of Roe v. Wade will jolt \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters into action in midterm elections. But is it enough to turn voters' attention away from inflation?\par \par \uc2 GRAND RAPIDS, Mich. -- The Supreme Court's decision overturning Roe v. Wade on Friday catapulted the explosive battle over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights into the center of several marquee midterm races, turning the fight over key governor's contests and coveted Senate seats into heated debates about personal freedom and public health.\par \par \uc2 Devastated Democrats, facing staggering political challenges amid high inflation and President Biden's low approval ratings, hoped the decision might reinvigorate disaffected base voters. They also saw the moment as a fresh chance to hold on to the moderate, suburban swing voters who have helped them win recent elections.\par \par \uc2 Republicans, for their part, publicly celebrated the ruling as the realization of a decades-long effort, even as some strategists -- and former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  -- privately acknowledged that the issue created at least some risk for a party that has enjoyed months of political momentum. Many argued that competitive races would ultimately be decided by other issues.\par \par \uc2 ''From the grass-roots perspective, there's a lot of joy,'' said Scott Jennings, a Republican who is a former top campaign aide to Senator \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , the minority leader. ''This is why we fight. And at the same time, this election is going to be decided on a couple of issues: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's approval rating, inflation, the economy, crime, quality of life.''\par \par \uc2 For years, the prospect of overturning Roe v. Wade was an abstract concept for many Americans -- a distressing but distant worry for some and a long-term goal rather than an imminent possibility to others. The Supreme Court's opinion eliminating the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended that era of disbelief, opening a new chapter of concrete consequences, in which races for governor, state legislature and attorney general, and even state courts might determine whether millions of Americans have access to the procedure.\par \par \uc2 ''This fall, Roe is on the ballot,'' Mr. Biden said on Friday. ''Personal freedoms are on the ballot.''\par \par \uc2 Both parties agree that the high stakes will be galvanizing, to some degree, to their respective bases. But the critical question remains whether swing voters -- in particular, independent women from the diverse suburbs, who are currently focused on economic uncertainty -- will turn their attention to the fight over access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''There are a lot of independent women, I think there are a lot of women who haven't been participating in elections, and are going to engage,'' Gov. \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  of Michigan said in an interview earlier this week, after hosting an emotional round table focused on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights at a brewery in Grand Rapids. ''But I'm not going to assume it. We're going to have to make sure that we're doing the work of education and persuasion and activation.''\par \par \uc2 Already this year, Democratic campaigns and supportive outside groups have spent nearly $18 million in advertising on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues, while Republicans and affiliated outside groups have spent nearly $21 million, according to the media tracking firm AdImpact. Both figures may balloon.\par \par \uc2 Activists and party strategists, who have been preparing for months to mobilize around this issue, are focusing in particular on governor's races in Michigan, Wisconsin and Pennsylvania, three states currently led by Democratic governors, and places where the outcomes this fall could directly impact the future of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights after the Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization decision handed control over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections back to the states.\par \par \uc2 Democrats also are planning to use the issue to play offense in other governor's races, while making the case that Senate and House candidates across the country, too, have embraced positions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that are far outside the mainstream.\par \par \uc2 An early test of energy around this issue will come in August, as Kansans vote on whether to remove the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the state constitution.\par \par \uc2 In a fund-raising email on Friday, Gov. \uc2 Laura Kelly\uc2  of Kansas, a Democrat, declared that ''I could be the only Kansas leader standing in the way'' of new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions. Her likely opponent, the state attorney general, Derek Schmidt, said that he would support the ballot initiative.\par \par \uc2 Democrats had been preparing to try to direct the expected outpouring of shock and anger into electoral action once the opinion was handed down, with party committees and state parties conferring on national messaging and mobilization plans, as well as launching a website on Friday to direct organizing efforts.\par \par \uc2 Candidates and organizations have employed focus groups and polling to assess the issue; there are sprawling fund-raising efforts; and the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups \uc2 Planned Parenthood Action Fund\uc2 , \uc2 NARAL Pro-Choice America\uc2  and Emily's List have said they intend to spend $150 million on the midterm elections. American Bridge 21st Century, a Democratic-aligned super PAC, says it has tapped social media influencers to communicate about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and Republican records on that issue to Americans who may be only casually political.\par \par \uc2 ''We will see, state by state by state, pre-existing bans go into effect, state legislatures rush to pass \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans,'' said Cecile Richards, the former president of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  who is now a chair of American Bridge. ''It's a different conversation now because it's become real.''\par \par \uc2 Despite all the mobilization, many party strategists do not anticipate that even Friday's seismic decision will fundamentally change voters' focus on cost-of-living worries. But some see it as reinforcing their core argument against Republicans: that the right wing of the party is in control, out of step with public opinion, and focused above all else on cultural battles. Senate Democrats and strategists are particularly focused on highlighting the Republican candidates who support near-total bans on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''Economic issues are always going to outweigh \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a lot of voters,'' said Celinda Lake, a veteran Democratic strategist. ''But it's very, very important for Democrats -- to win these swing voters -- to make this a choice, not a referendum.'' \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , she said, ''is going to be a major factor in that, because it is a very clear distinction.''\par \par \uc2 Polling shows that Americans strongly oppose completely overturning Roe v. Wade -- in a Washington Post-ABC poll conducted in late April, 54 percent of Americans thought the Roe decision should be upheld, while 28 percent believed it should be overturned. But views on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  vary depending on a state's political tilt.\par \par \uc2 That is one reason Republicans' messaging on the issue has been less unified. On Friday, as some candidates, lawmakers and the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  rushed to celebrate the ruling, others sought to quickly return their focus to pocketbook issues.\par \par \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 , the Republican Senate candidate in Nevada -- a state with a history of supporting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights -- on Friday cheered the ''historic victory for the sanctity of life,'' but stressed that access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was already ''settled law'' in Nevada.\par \par \uc2 ''It won't distract voters from unaffordable prices, rising crime or the border crisis,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 When asked for comment, Jesse Hunt, a spokesman for the \uc2 Republican Governors Association\uc2 , responded in a statement that ''the persuadable voters that will determine the outcome in competitive races are deeply concerned with the damage being done to their financial security'' by Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Even Mr. Trump, the former president who put conservatives on the court, has privately told people that he believes the court's decision will be ''bad for Republicans.'' In a public statement on Friday, Mr. Trump called the decision ''the biggest \uc2 WIN\uc2  for LIFE in a generation.''\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights opponents are working to capitalize on conservatives' enthusiasm.\par \par \uc2 The anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights group Susan B. Anthony Pro-Life America launched a field program last year, with plans to engage eight million voters in critical battleground states. The group is focusing on ''those people that are in play, that could go either way based on this particular issue,'' said Marjorie Dannenfelser, the president of the organization.\par \par \uc2 ''It's not just some theoretical vote about somebody who says they're pro-life,'' she said. ''It's now an opportunity to actually do something about it.''\par \par \uc2 Penny Nance, the president of Concerned Women for America, an organization that opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, said the group was planning a summit that would focus on the role of state activism in a post-Roe nation.\par \par \uc2 Some state officials have ''basically said, 'We don't really have the ability to change the law because of the Supreme Court decision,''' she said.\par \par \uc2 ''Now,'' she continued, ''it changes everything.''\par \par \uc2 That new focus on state laws has already intensified the debate in statehouses and governor's races in politically divided states. In Pennsylvania, the next governor and a Republican-led statehouse will likely determine access.\par \par \uc2 ''Roe v. Wade is rightly relegated to the ash heap of history,'' said \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 , the far-right Republican nominee for governor in Pennsylvania. \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2 , the state attorney general and the Democratic nominee for governor, wrote on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  on Friday that ''without Roe, the only thing stopping them is the veto pen of our next Governor.''\par \par \uc2 In Michigan and Wisconsin, old laws on the books call for near-total bans on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Democratic governors up for re-election have vowed to fight to protect access.\par \par \uc2 In Michigan, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters are working to secure a constitutional amendment protecting the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Ms. Whitmer has also filed a lawsuit asking ''the Michigan Supreme Court to immediately resolve'' whether the State Constitution protects the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 At her roundtable discussion this week, Ms. Whitmer spoke with women about whether they thought voters had yet grasped the significance of what overturning Roe v. Wade would mean.\par \par \uc2 ''So many people,'' one attendee told her, ''didn't realize it was this serious.''\par \par \uc2 ''We're going to have to make sure that we're doing the work of education and persuasion and activation,'' Gov. Gretchen Whitmer of Michigan said. (PHOTOGRAPH BY EMILY ELCONIN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A11) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220625ei6p00045\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
