\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Legal Precedents Will Other Rights 'Come Crumbling' Down?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sheryl Gay Stolberg \par \uc2 576 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The same rationale could also be used to overturn cases establishing rights to contraception, same-sex consensual relations and same-sex marriage.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , in his concurring opinion overturning Roe v. Wade, laid out a vision that prompted concerns about what other rights could disappear: The same rationale that the Supreme Court used to declare there was no right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said, should also be used to overturn cases establishing rights to contraception, same-sex consensual relations and same-sex marriage.\par \par \uc2 In the majority opinion written by Justice \uc2 Samuel A. Alito\uc2 , the court said that nothing in its decision ''should be understood to cast doubt on precedents that do not concern \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .'' Justice Thomas said he agreed with that.\par \par \uc2 However, he noted that in its rationale, the court's majority found that a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not a form of ''liberty'' protected by the due process clause of the 14th Amendment to the Constitution -- as the court had said in Roe.\par \par \uc2 Then, he took aim at three other landmark cases that relied on that same legal reasoning: Griswold v. Connecticut, a 1965 decision that declared married couples had a right to contraception; Lawrence v. Texas, a 2003 case invalidating sodomy laws and making same-sex sexual activity legal across the country; and Obergefell v. Hodges, the 2015 case establishing the right of gay couples to marry.\par \par \uc2 Justice Thomas wrote that the court ''should reconsider'' all three decisions, saying it had a duty to ''correct the error'' established in those precedents. Then, he said, after ''overruling these demonstrably erroneous decisions, the question would remain whether other constitutional provisions'' protected the rights they established.\par \par \uc2 This kind of language is just what advocates for \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  and for L.G.B.T.Q. rights have been fearing. Defenders of the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  have repeatedly warned that if Roe fell, the right to contraception and same-sex marriage would be next.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents, who fought hard to overturn Roe, have insisted they have no interest in trying to undo the right to contraception.\par \par \uc2 But already, states like Missouri are trying to restrict access to contraception by banning public funding for certain methods: intrauterine devices and the so-called morning after pill. And some Republicans, notably Senator \uc2 Marsha Blackburn\uc2  of Tennessee, have said that the Griswold case was wrongly decided. Earlier this year, Ms. Blackburn called Griswold ''constitutionally unsound.''\par \par \uc2 Justice Thomas alone signed the concurring opinion.\par \par \uc2 Lawrence O. Gostin, a professor at Georgetown University Law School who specializes in public health law, said it would be ''easy to dismiss Thomas as a lone wolf'' and to do so would be a mistake. Now that the majority on the court has destroyed the foundation on which Roe was built, he said, ''the other rights may well come crumbling after.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Gostin cited another contentious legal area -- gun rights -- as an example. In ruling that the Second Amendment applies to individuals, he said, the court overturned a longstanding precedent. At the time, he said, Justice \uc2 Antonin Scalia\uc2  said the decision would not affect other gun safety regulations. But ''that promise didn't last,'' he said, noting that on Thursday, the court issued a major decision expanding gun rights.\par \par \uc2 Justice Clarence Thomas speaking at an event in Dallas last month. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Allison V. Smith for The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220625ei6p0004i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
