\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 From the Archives: Landmark Decision Establishes a Constitutional Right\b0\par\par\uc2 By Derrick Bryson Taylor \par \uc2 217 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Nearly 50 years ago, the Supreme Court legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the United States with its decision in Roe v. Wade, reshaping the nation's social and political landscape. The 7-2 ruling was announced on Jan. 22, 1973 -- the same day that Lyndon B. Johnson died. That news led \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  the next day, with the Roe decision underneath in Columns 1 and 2. Justice Harry A. Blackmun, a modest Midwestern Republican and a defender of the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , wrote the majority opinion. In short, the decision established a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It struck down laws in many states that had barred \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , declaring that they could not ban the procedure before the point at which a fetus can survive outside the womb. With its ruling, the court created a framework to govern \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulation based on the trimesters of pregnancy. In the first trimester, it allowed almost no regulations. In the second, it allowed regulations to protect women's health.\par \par \uc2  In the third, it allowed states to ban abortions so long as exceptions were made to protect the life and health of the mother.\par \par \uc2 The Times called it a ''historic resolution of a fiercely controversial issue.'' \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220625ei6p00048\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
