\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Ending One Fight and Starting Another in a Polarized America\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate Zernike \par \uc2 1,749 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court sent the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  back to the states in an even more polarized era than when Roe v. Wade was introduced.\par \par \uc2 Within minutes of the Supreme Court's overturning of Roe v. Wade on Friday, the attorney general in Missouri issued an opinion banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in his state. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics in several cities, including Montgomery, Ala., and Sioux Falls, S.D., shut down. But others in Illinois and Ohio kept seeing patients.\par \par \uc2 At a Phoenix clinic, 40 women were waiting to schedule appointments, setting the staff scrambling for answers about whether it was still allowed to perform abortions. ''We sent a bunch of people home, and they were hysterical,'' said Dr. Gabrielle Goodrick, the clinic's owner.\par \par \uc2 In Ohio, Candice Keller, a former state representative who sponsored a law banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after six weeks of pregnancy, broke down in tears of joy. ''I just started to cry,'' Ms. Keller said. ''It has been a real battle. It felt like you are never going to win. But we did win.''\par \par \uc2 The overturning of Roe on Friday, stunning even as it was long predicted, set off waves of triumph and of despair, from the protesters on either side massing in front of the Supreme Court, to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics and crisis pregnancy centers, and in texts with friends and bursting social media feeds.\par \par \uc2 The split-screen reaction reflected a polarized nation: jubilation and relief on one side, outrage and grief on the other.\par \par \uc2 ''If I had confetti I would be tossing it high,'' said Dale Bartscher, the director of South Dakota Right to Life. ''Today, we're celebrating a day that we've long dreamed of, advocated for and worked for: the overcoming of Roe v. Wade.''\par \par \uc2 David Ripley, the director of Idaho Chooses Life, said he did not think he would be alive to see the day when Idaho's trigger ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- making it illegal upon Roe's fall -- would actually take effect.\par \par \uc2 ''The court has finally admitted that its ruling and the rulings of the federal courts for the last 50 years have been egregiously wrong,'' Mr. Ripley said. ''I am ecstatic.''\par \par \uc2 On the other side, supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights worried for the millions of women living in the wide swath of the country where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be illegal or essentially unavailable because of layers of restrictions that have added expense and delays for women seeking the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Some women hoarded \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills. A group called Shout Your \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  began a campaign proclaiming #AbortionPillsForever, vowing to help get them to women in need.\par \par \uc2 ''I knew this was coming but I didn't expect to feel such anger,'' Amalie Hahn, 49, said in Jackson, Miss. ''You want to ban abortions in the state of Mississippi, but you don't want to take into account that Mississippi is one of, if not the worst state to give birth in. We are in the midst of a formula shortage and poverty is at an all-time high and they are forcing women to have babies. This is insane.''\par \par \uc2 Jackson is the home of the clinic, known locally as the Pink House, at the center of the Supreme Court's decision. On Friday, volunteers there continued to escort patients inside, and lawyers said the clinic would continue to offer \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the next 10 days, until Mississippi's trigger ban takes effect.\par \par \uc2 The court's ruling, which had been foreshadowed in oral arguments in December and again when a draft opinion was leaked in May, means that within a month \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be banned except for rare exception in 13 states. Opponents and supporters alike say it will quite likely become illegal or inaccessible in about half the states, with 33.6 million women of childbearing age living in states likely to lose access.\par \par \uc2 Millions of Americans have never known a world without the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In Kansas City, Mo., one of them, Mallorie McBride, said she was ''shocked and horrified'' at the Supreme Court decision.\par \par \uc2 ''We're taking so many steps back,'' Ms. McBride, 24, said. ''I've always been a believer that older men should not be making decisions about women's bodies. As a single woman in my 20s, I haven't felt very represented by my government in a while, but this takes it one step further.''\par \par \uc2 ''It's also like, what else will happen after this?'' said Briana Perry, 30, a board member of Healthy and Free Tennessee, a \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  network in Nashville. ''Not only when it comes to \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but other rights that we have that we thought we were secured through Supreme Court rulings that are now in question.''\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court's decision calls \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ''a profound moral issue on which Americans hold sharply conflicting views.'' But while Americans have become more likely to say that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is morally acceptable, the issue is very much a political one. Friday's ruling made it even more so, sending the question of how to regulate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  back to the states -- and into a new and even more polarized era.\par \par \uc2 Both sides quickly pivoted to the fights ahead.\par \par \uc2 James Bopp Jr., general counsel to the \uc2 National Right to Life Committee\uc2 , who has crusaded against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the Roe decision in 1973, called Friday's ruling ''a total victory for the pro-life movement and for America.'' Still, he said, the job for anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  forces was ''half-done.'' The group was assembled for its convention in Atlanta when the decision was announced, and had already drafted model legislation to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in every state, with exceptions only for risks to the life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 ''That's going to be an enormous task -- there will be an array of forces against us,'' Mr. Bopp said. ''This the end of the beginning, as Churchill said once. A huge obstacle has been removed, and now we're going to make sure that the law is used to protect the unborn.''\par \par \uc2 Troy Newman, the president of Kansas-based Operation Rescue, which staged a long campaign of blockades outside \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics, said the decision still left too much latitude for states like his, largely led by Democrats, to allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''It is now time for the pro-life movement to pull up our big-boy boots and win the rest of the states,'' he said. ''We will be mopping up, putting the remaining dirty, disgusting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  mills out of business.''\par \par \uc2 NARAL, \uc2 Planned Parenthood Action Fund\uc2  and other groups pledged to spend $150 million in the 2022 midterms to elect supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights to state houses and Congress. The Women's March, which rallied demonstrators after the election of Donald J. Trump, called for protests in a ''Summer of Rage.''\par \par \uc2 In Conway, Ark., Stacey Margaret Jones, 52, said she kept thinking about the women she encountered when she volunteered at Planned Parenthood.\par \par \uc2 ''I feel really hopeless because I feel like there's nothing I could have personally done differently,'' Ms. Jones said. She has donated to candidates who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, attended marches and written to her legislators. But in a conservative state like Arkansas, she does not feel like her voice is heard. Her state senator is Jason Rapert, a lead sponsor of Arkansas's trigger law that outlawed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm looking for guidance from somebody or some organization to say, 'OK, we knew this could happen and this is what we're going to do,''' Ms. Jones said.\par \par \uc2 As protests swelled outside the Supreme Court, with supporters and opponents shouting slogans back and forth, \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2  sent additional officers to line the barriers blocking the court and the Capitol building across the street. They were preparing for larger crowds as people finished work. By the afternoon, protests had shut down the nearby Frederick Douglass Memorial Bridge.\par \par \uc2 But the divided reaction played out far from Washington, too.\par \par \uc2 In Leawood, Kan., a protester shouted through an amplifier, ''You're murdering your child!'' as Daniel Morrison and his girlfriend arrived in the rain at a Planned Parenthood clinic so she could have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ''You've come to a death camp. Babies are murdered here.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Morrison responded, ''I am helping my girlfriend, I am helping her choose,'' emphasizing the word ''choose.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Morrison said that he worked in a restaurant in Oklahoma and his girlfriend volunteered in a homeless shelter for youth, and that they were not prepared financially or emotionally for a child.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm not here because I just want to go have fun and party more,'' Mr. Morrison said. ''I want to be able to plan a life for a child and be able to support a child in ways more than money -- be able to give it time and everything a child would need to be able to develop. Having the choice to do that is very important. I don't consider it murder.''\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court decision is only going to cause pain and difficulty for people, he said.\par \par \uc2 Across the parking lot, the Advice & Aid pregnancy center had extra security on site Friday morning because of what its executive director, Ruth Tisdale, said were calls for attacks on facilities such as hers. Ms. Tisdale said that the Supreme Court's decision was ''an exciting time,'' but that her work had to continue.\par \par \uc2 Reporting was contributed by Austyn Gaffney, Jimmie E. Gates, Carey Gillam, Jack Healy, Carolyn Komatsoulis, Tom Lawrence, Erica Sweeney and Kevin Williams.\par \par \uc2 Reporting was contributed by Austyn Gaffney, Jimmie E. Gates, Carey Gillam, Jack Healy, Carolyn Komatsoulis, Tom Lawrence, Erica Sweeney and Kevin Williams.\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY SARAHBETH MANEY/THE NEW YORK TIMES); WASHINGTON, D.C.: Anti-\uc2  advocates outside the Supreme Court. Friday's ruling will trigger near-total bans in 13 states. (PHOTOGRAPH BY SHURAN HUANG FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); SALT LAKE CITY: Supporters of \uc2  rights lined up Friday outside the State Capitol in Utah, where \uc2  is now illegal. (PHOTOGRAPH BY KIM RAFF FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A14); NEW ORLEANS: A protester at Women's Health Care Center, the city's only \uc2  clinic. Louisiana's trigger law made \uc2  illegal. (PHOTOGRAPH BY KATHLEEN FLYNN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); LOS ANGELES: Pulling up for a demonstration for \uc2  rights at the city's federal courthouse. \uc2  remains legal in California. (PHOTOGRAPH BY MORGAN LIEBERMAN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A15) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220625ei6p0004e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
