\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Other Significant Cases Chipping Away at Roe\b0\par\par\uc2 By Victoria Kim \par \uc2 577 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 13\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The legal case over a law that makes most abortions illegal after 15 weeks of pregnancy is by no means the first to challenge or chip away at Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court's decision in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, the case before the nation's highest court on a restrictive Mississippi law, is the most consequential on women's access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the court's 1973 decision in Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 But the legal case, over a law that makes most abortions illegal after 15 weeks of pregnancy, is by no means the first to challenge or chip away at Roe v. Wade. Here is a look at some of the most significant past rulings.\par \par \uc2 Webster v. \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Health\b0 \highlight\uc2  Services, 1989\par \par \uc2 The narrow 5-4 ruling gave states the right to impose new restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , upholding parts of a 1986 Missouri law relating to public resources being used to assist women in abortions not necessary to save their lives. The laws barred public employees from being involved and banned the use of public buildings, and separately required doctors to perform tests to determine whether the fetus can live outside the womb if the woman is at least 20 weeks pregnant.\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood of Southeastern Pennsylvania v. Casey, 1992\par \par \uc2 In the draft opinion leaked in May, Justice \uc2 Samuel A. Alito Jr\uc2 . wrote that Casey should be overruled along with Roe v. Wade. In this decision, the Supreme Court reaffirmed the ''essence'' of the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  outlined in Roe v. Wade while giving states new leeway to impose restrictions on the procedure. The 5-4 ruling upheld part of a Pennsylvania law regulating access to abortions while finding that laws prohibiting all or most abortions are unconstitutional, with the majority writing that Roe v. Wade established a ''rule of law and a component of liberty we cannot renounce.'' The decision also set the standard that it was unconstitutional for states to ban abortions before ''fetal viability,'' estimated to be about 24 weeks into pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Gonzales v. Carhart, 2007\par \par \uc2 The 5-4 decision upheld a federal law banning a method of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  known by opponents of the procedure as ''partial birth,'' reversing course from a decision to strike down a similar state law seven years earlier in Stenberg v. Carhart. It was the first time the court backed a ban on a specific method of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , opening up doctors to criminal prosecution for performing the procedure. The federal law, enacted in 2003, prohibited a procedure known medically as ''intact dilation and extraction'' used to terminate pregnancies beginning at about 12 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Whole Woman's Health v. Hellerstedt, 2016\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court struck down parts of a restrictive Texas law that required doctors performing abortions to have admitting privileges at nearby hospitals and clinics, which could have drastically reduced the number of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics in the state. The 5-to-3 decision was the court's most sweeping since Planned Parenthood v. Casey. It found that Texas' restrictions would place an ''undue burden'' on the ability to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The decision curbed states' ability to restrict and regulate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Phillis Schlafly, a political activist, spoke outside the Supreme Court after the justices released their decision in Planned Parenthood of Southeastern Pennsylvania v. Casey, in 1992. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Ron Sachs/CNP, via Getty Images FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220625ei6p0004h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
