\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Though Trump Paved Way, He Fears Effect on G.O.P.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Maggie Haberman and Michael C. Bender \par \uc2 1,719 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  has privately told friends and advisers that a ruling overturning Roe v. Wade will be ''bad for Republicans.''\par \par \uc2 The end of the landmark Roe v. Wade ruling was the culmination of decades of work by Republicans and social conservatives -- one that came to pass only after a former Democrat from New York who had once supported \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights helped muscle through three Supreme Court justices.\par \par \uc2 Publicly, former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  heralded the Supreme Court's ruling on Friday ending federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections as a victory. Yet, as he faces possible prosecution over his efforts to subvert the 2020 election and prepares for a likely 2024 presidential campaign, Mr. Trump has privately told friends and advisers the ruling will be ''bad for Republicans.''\par \par \uc2 When a draft copy of the decision leaked in May, Mr. Trump began telling friends and advisers that it would anger suburban women, a group who helped tilt the 2020 race to President Biden, and would lead to a backlash against Republicans in the November midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 In other conversations, Mr. Trump has told people that measures like the Texas state law banning most abortions after six weeks and allowing citizens to file lawsuits against people who enable abortions are ''so stupid,'' according to a person with direct knowledge of the discussions. The Supreme Court let the measure stand in December 2021.\par \par \uc2 For the first hours after the decision was made public on Friday, Mr. Trump was muted in response, a striking contrast to the conservatives who worked in his administration, including former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 . Mr. Pence issued a statement saying, ''Life won,'' as he called for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents to keep fighting ''in every state in the land.''\par \par \uc2 For weeks in advance of the ruling, Mr. Trump had been just as muted. In an interview with \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  in May, Mr. Trump uttered an eyebrow-raising demurral in response to a question about the central role he had played in paving the way for the reversal of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 ''I never like to take credit for anything,'' said Mr. Trump, who has spent his career affixing his name to almost anything he could.\par \par \uc2 Pressed to describe his feelings about having helped assemble a court that was on the verge of erasing the 1973 ruling, Mr. Trump refused to engage the question and instead focused on the leak of the draft opinion.\par \par \uc2 ''I don't know what the decision is,'' he said. ''We've been reading about something that was drawn months ago. Nobody knows what that decision is. A draft is a draft.''\par \par \uc2 By early afternoon on Friday, Mr. Trump put out a statement taking a victory lap, including applauding himself for sticking by his choice of nominees. All three of Mr. Trump's appointees to the court -- whom he pushed through with help from \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , the Senate Republican leader -- were in the majority in the 6-to-3 ruling. He left unspoken the fact that he repeatedly attacked the court for not interceding on his behalf after he lost the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 ''Today's decision, which is the biggest \uc2 WIN\uc2  for LIFE in a generation, along with other decisions that have been announced recently, were only made possible because I delivered everything as promised, including nominating and getting three highly respected and strong Constitutionalists confirmed to the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 ,'' Mr. Trump said.\par \par \uc2 The former president also told Fox News, in an interview published after the decision on Friday, that the court was ''following the Constitution, and giving rights back when they should have been given long ago.'' He added, ''I think, in the end, this is something that will work out for everybody.''\par \par \uc2 Republicans are bracing for a fight: A memo in May from the National Republican Senate Committee, first reported by Axios, suggested that G.O.P. candidates deal with criticism from Democrats by highlighting ''extreme and radical views'' in support of late-term abortions and government funding for abortions, and suggesting that their own views are based ''in compassion and reason.''\par \par \uc2 While Mr. Trump had stayed quiet on the issue in recent weeks, people close to him anticipate he will become more vocal as he watches how clearly his right-wing base responds and how easily he can point to it as something that he made happen. His advisers believe he can highlight the issue as he faces potential Republican challengers and sees signs that his own political base has moved further to the right on vaccines and other issues.\par \par \uc2 Other potential candidates have been far more vocal. Mr. Pence has spent months talking about his desire to see Roe v. Wade end and visiting pregnancy centers. Former Secretary of State \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 , another evangelical Christian considering a presidential campaign, wrote on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  after the draft opinion emerged: ''I pray for the reversal of Roe v. Wade. Every human being, born and unborn, has a fundamental right to life, and it is our calling to guard and secure it.''\par \par \uc2 Most significantly from Mr. Trump's perspective, Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  of Florida, the Republican whom a number of Mr. Trump's former supporters have expressed interest in seeing as a 2024 candidate, signed a bill this spring banning abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Bob Vander Plaats, president of the Family Leader, a socially conservative political group based in Iowa, praised Mr. Trump before the ruling came down. ''What he did as president is, he followed through on what he said he was going to do and appoint Supreme Court justices that were faithful to the Constitution,'' Mr. Vander Plaats said.\par \par \uc2 Asked about Mr. Trump's private remarks that the ruling would hurt Republicans, Mr. Vander Plaats responded, ''I would just vehemently disagree with that.''\par \par \uc2 Indeed, while Republicans in competitive states and congressional districts have expressed some anxiousness about the sort of blowback Mr. Trump has told people he fears, many pollsters say it is too soon to tell how the issue will play out in the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 A Gallup survey this month found that the share of Americans identifying as ''pro-choice'' had jumped to 55 percent after hovering between 45 percent and 50 percent for a decade. That sentiment was ''the highest Gallup has measured since 1995,'' while the 39 percent who identified as ''pro-life'' was ''the lowest since 1996,'' the polling firm said.\par \par \uc2 A May survey conducted for CNN found that 66 percent of the people questioned said they believed Roe v. Wade should not be overturned.\par \par \uc2 But anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists who supported Mr. Trump as president insist the ruling will be a political boon to Republicans, and maintained that surveys in which voters are asked specific questions about the measure indicate that.\par \par \uc2 ''When pro-life Republicans go on offense to expose the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  extremism of their opponents, life is a proven winning issue for the G.O.P.,'' said Marjorie Dannenfelser, president of the Susan B. Anthony List, which supports anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  candidates.\par \par \uc2 Voluble as he is, Mr. Trump has long seemed to have a special difficulty in grappling with the subject of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which he supported for years as a right but said he personally abhorred. In 2011, as he considered a presidential campaign as a Republican, he announced he did not support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, but struggled to discuss the issue as a candidate four years later.\par \par \uc2 ''I know you're opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' CNN's Jake Tapper said to him in a June 2015 interview.\par \par \uc2 ''Right,'' Trump replied. ''I'm pro-choice.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Tapper furrowed a brow. ''You're pro-choice or pro-life?''\par \par \uc2 ''I'm pro-life,'' Mr. Trump quickly corrected himself. ''I'm sorry.''\par \par \uc2 In March 2016, Mr. Trump said in an MSNBC town hall event that if the nation outlawed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- a change he supported -- there would have to be ''some form of punishment'' for a woman seeking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The remark set off a firestorm, which Mr. Trump tried to quell by issuing two statements that only added to the confusion.\par \par \uc2 Two days later, on \uc2 CBS\uc2 , Mr. Trump said that he wished \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  were left up to the states, but that the federal laws were ''set, and I think we have to leave it that way.''\par \par \uc2 Officials with the Susan B. Anthony List said at the time that Mr. Trump had disqualified himself for the presidency. His campaign again issued a cleanup statement, saying he only meant that the laws must remain in place ''until he is president.''\par \par \uc2 Yet in his third and final debate against \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  in the 2016 general election, Mr. Trump laid out his belief that he would have two and as many as three Supreme Court seats to fill. And he explicitly promised, in a way other candidates never had, that when he chose jurists who shared his stated beliefs, Roe v. Wade would be overturned.\par \par \uc2 As president, however, Mr. Trump often wanted little to do with the issue.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump seemed to swing between fascination with and repulsion from the subject, remarking upon the thorniness of it and how divided the country was on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and wringing his hands when it came time to make decisions.\par \par \uc2 And he often preferred to defer to Mr. Pence, even at one point expressing hope that Mr. Pence would cancel a trip to Rome, including an audience with the pope, and instead represent the administration at the March for Life in Washington.\par \par \uc2 One of Mr. Trump's supporters, \uc2 Robert Jeffress\uc2 , a Texas pastor, recalled having discussions with the former president about the ''political complexities'' of the issue, describing Mr. Trump as an opponent of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  but also a ''realist.''\par \par \uc2 ''I've heard him point out in the Oval Office that 60-plus percent of Americans are against a repeal of Roe, and that makes this a politically complex issue,'' Mr. Jeffress said.\par \par \uc2 Donald J. Trump with Amy Coney Barrett, his third conservative pick for the Supreme Court. (PHOTOGRAPH BY OLIVER CONTRERAS FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Former Vice President Mike Pence has spent months talking about his desire to see Roe end, and lauded Friday's news. (PHOTOGRAPH BY JAMIE KELTER DAVIS FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220625ei6p00047\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
