\par\par \uc2 Foreign Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Germany Ends Prohibition Of  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2 -Services Ads\b0\par\par\uc2 By Christopher F. Schuetze \par \uc2 921 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The vote to scrap the law came on the same day that Roe v. Wade was overturned by the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BERLIN -- Lawmakers voted on Friday to end a Nazi-era ban on the advertisement of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services, a measure that had effectively criminalized doctors who provided information about the procedure.\par \par \uc2 The law, though ignored for years, had recently prompted several high-profile legal cases against doctors. That, in turn, led to a sustained campaign by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights activists for repeal of the advertising ban, which Germany's relatively new government had made a goal.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Germany is legal until the 12th week of pregnancy, after a mandatory consulting session.\par \par \uc2 ''This is our day,'' wrote Nora Sz\u225\'00\'E1sz, a gynecologist in the central city of Kassel, in a Tweet. Referring to the law by its criminal code number, she added: ''The #219a falls and with it a piece of the worst patronization of women.''\par \par \uc2 With the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  on Friday overturning Roe v. Wade, the 1973 Supreme Court decision that had legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the German lawmakers' decision reflected a broad counterpoint on the issue.\par \par \uc2 ''By abolishing Section 219a of the Criminal Code, we are strengthening the self-determination and safety of all women,'' said Nancy Faeser, the country's interior minister.\par \par \uc2 The vote, which was cast by a show of hands in German Parliament, was carried by the three parties of the coalition government and the far-left party.\par \par \uc2 Germany's conservative Christian Democratic Union, which ran the previous four governments under \uc2 Angela Merkel\uc2 , had blocked previous efforts to purge the law from the books and voted against the repeal on Friday.\par \par \uc2 ''We don't want public advertising of abortions on the internet or social media,'' Elisabeth Winkelmeier-Becker, a Christian Democratic member of Parliament, said before the vote.\par \par \uc2 Other lawmakers also opposed the repeal. ''A woman's right to sexual self-determination also includes taking responsibility for ensuring that she does not become pregnant if she does not want to,'' Thomas Seitz of the far-right Alternative for Germany party, said in Parliament on Friday.\par \par \uc2 But the new government, led by Chancellor \uc2 Olaf Scholz\uc2 's Social Democrats, had promised a repeal of the ban in its coalition agreement, a sort of political road map that it had presented last November.\par \par \uc2 ''Today is a tremendous day,'' Lisa Paus, the federal minister for families, seniors women and youth, told lawmakers before the vote. ''For the physicians and above all for all women in this country.''\par \par \uc2 Germany's action also highlights the ideological split on the issue within the Europe Union. In neighboring Poland, whose border is just an hour's drive from Berlin, all abortions are banned.\par \par \uc2 But while neighboring Belgium and the Netherlands both have obligatory waiting periods, and France requires women seeking abortions to go through two consultations, no other European country had banned the transmission of information about the procedure the way Germany had.\par \par \uc2 ''The fact that we prohibit highly qualified doctors who perform such procedures from providing factual information under criminal penalty is absurd, it is out of touch with the times, it is unjust and that is why we are ending this state of affairs,'' Marco Buschmann, the justice minister, told lawmakers during the session.\par \par \uc2 The law, which dates to the Nazi era and was quietly ignored by doctors for decades, became relevant again with the rising popularity of the internet as a medical resource -- raising the question of what is advertising and what is simply information -- and an increase in criminal complaints from anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists.\par \par \uc2 In 2017, Dr. Kristina H\u228\'00\'E4nel, a general practitioner who offers abortions in the central town of Giessen, posted information on the procedure on her website. Because it was deemed to be advertisement, a judge fined her 6,000 euros. Instead of removing the content, she took the decision to a higher court and eventually all the way to the country's highest court, the Constitutional Court in Karlsruhe.\par \par \uc2 And while the court upheld the law then, and she was forced to pay a reduced fine, her case brought the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advertising into public consciousness, and Dr. H\u228\'00\'E4nel became one of the country's best known \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights activists.\par \par \uc2 Three years ago the law was altered slightly, allowing doctors to say whether they offered abortions, but the ban on advertising remained in force. Judges continued to hand down penalties, including in a 2019 ruling against a two gynecologists in Berlin who were fined 2,000 euros for writing on their site that they offered the procedure in a ''protected atmosphere'' and that it was ''anesthesia-free'' and ''drug-induced.''\par \par \uc2 The ban on advertising had made finding an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider very difficult, Dr. H\u228\'00\'E4nel said. ''Women sometimes have to call 10 doctors -- where they are often treated badly -- before they stumble onto someone who can provide them information.''\par \par \uc2 ''Its such a relief after five years of grueling battles,'' Dr. H\u228\'00\'E4nel said about Friday's vote, which she traveled the 300 miles to Berlin to witness. ''I can now simply put my information back on the website so that the women who come to us can be simply be informed.''\par \par \uc2 Dr. Kristina H\u228\'00\'E4nel brought the issue of \uc2  advertising into public consciousness by challenging the ban on it in court. ''It's such a relief,'' she said of Parliament scrapping the Nazi-era law. (PHOTOGRAPH BY BORIS ROESSLER/PICTURE ALLIANCE, VIA GETTY IMAGES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220625ei6p0003t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
