\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 What Did Roe Say? No Ban Before Viability\b0\par\par\uc2 By Adam Liptak \par \uc2 511 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The 1973 decision said states could not ban abortions before fetal viability, the point at which the fetus can survive outside the womb.\par \par \uc2 By a 7-to-2 vote, the Supreme Court in Roe v. Wade in 1973 established a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , striking down laws in many states that had banned the procedure. The court said states could not ban abortions before fetal viability, the point at which the fetus can survive outside the womb. That was around 28 weeks at the time; because of improvements in medical technology, it is around 23 weeks now.\par \par \uc2 The decision was widely criticized, including by people who supported access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a policy matter.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2 , a strong supporter of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights who died in 2020, expressed qualms about Roe over the years.\par \par \uc2 ''The court bit off more than it could chew,'' she said in 2009, for instance, in remarks after a speech at \uc2 Princeton University\uc2 . It would have been enough, she said, to strike down the extremely restrictive Texas law at issue in Roe and leave further questions for later cases.\par \par \uc2 ''The legislatures all over the United States were moving on this question,'' she added. ''The law was in a state of flux.''\par \par \uc2 Roe shut those developments down, she said, creating a backlash.\par \par \uc2 ''The Supreme Court's decision was a perfect rallying point for people who disagreed with the notion that it should be a woman's choice,'' Justice Ginsburg said. ''They could, instead of fighting in the trenches legislature by legislature, go after this decision by unelected judges.''\par \par \uc2 The decision also came under withering criticism from some liberal law professors.\par \par \uc2 ''What is frightening about Roe is that this super-protected right is not inferable from the language of the Constitution, the framers' thinking respecting the specific problem in issue, any general value derivable from the provisions they included, or the nation's governmental structure,'' John Hart Ely, a prominent constitutional scholar, wrote in 1973 in The Yale Law Journal.\par \par \uc2 Professor Ely, who died in 2003, wrote that the Roe decision was untenable as a matter of intellectually honest jurisprudence.\par \par \uc2 ''It is not constitutional law,'' he said of the decision, ''and gives almost no sense of an obligation to try to be.''\par \par \uc2 Roe established a framework for governing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulation based on the trimesters of pregnancy. In the first trimester, it allowed almost no regulations. In the second, it allowed regulations to protect women's health. In the third, it allowed states to ban abortions so long as exceptions were made to protect the life and health of the mother.\par \par \uc2 The court discarded the trimester framework in 1992 in Planned Parenthood v. Casey. But Casey retained what it called Roe's ''essential holding'' -- that women have a constitutional right to terminate their pregnancies until fetal viability.\par \par \uc2 Roe established a framework to govern \uc2  regulation based on the trimesters of pregnancy. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Kenny Holston for The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220625ei6p0003m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
