\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Thinking Ahead to Midterms, Biden Tells Voters, 'This Is Not Over'\b0\par\par\uc2 By Zolan Kanno-Youngs \par \uc2 1,111 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ''This decision must not be the final word,'' President Biden said after the Supreme Court eliminated the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- Calling the Supreme Court's reversal of Roe v. Wade a ''tragic error,'' President Biden on Friday tried to galvanize voters ahead of the midterm elections and called on Americans to ''make their voices heard.''\par \par \uc2 Speaking from the White House, Mr. Biden said the Justice Department would defend any woman who travels to a different state to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and said the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  would make sure \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medications were available.\par \par \uc2 But to truly protect a ''constitutional right that is so fundamental,'' Mr. Biden said voters must elect candidates who will codify Roe v. Wade into federal law.\par \par \uc2 ''This decision must not be the final word,'' Mr. Biden said. ''My administration will use all of its appropriate lawful powers. Congress must act. And your vote? You can have the final word. This is not over.''\par \par \uc2 The president's speech, just hours after the Supreme Court overturned the nearly 50-year-old decision, signaled that Mr. Biden's party will likely make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a central issue in the midterms in an attempt to energize voters at a time when Democrats are expected to face losses.\par \par \uc2 With nearly a dozen senior aides, all women, crowded to the right of his lectern, Mr. Biden said the decision ''will have real and immediate consequences'' and that ''the health and life of women in this nation are now at risk.''\par \par \uc2 The forceful defense of \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  was striking from a president who has not always been comfortable speaking about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Mr. Biden, a practicing Roman Catholic, has rarely been the full-throated backer of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights that activists have sought, evolving from an outright critic of Roe early in his career to a largely quiet supporter.\par \par \uc2 In the weeks since a draft opinion of the overturning of Roe leaked, Mr. Biden has cast the possibility of the Supreme Court's decision as one that would threaten not just \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, but also the right to privacy more broadly.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, Mr. Biden warned that the Supreme Court decision could have implications for other rights that Americans have come to expect -- citing Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 's concurring opinion that said the same rationale the Supreme Court used to eliminate the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be used to reconsider decisions about contraception and same-sex marriage.\par \par \uc2 Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , who was in Illinois to speak about maternal health but recast her address to focus on Roe, said the ruling would affect ''decisions about the right to start a family,'' including the right to use contraception.\par \par \uc2 ''Today's decision on that theory, then, calls into question other rights that we thought were settled,'' including interracial and same-sex marriage.\par \par \uc2 She repeated the president's plea to get to the polls. ''You have the power to elect leaders who will defend and protect your rights,'' Ms. Harris said.\par \par \uc2 Stefanie Brown James, a co-founder of the Collective PAC, an organization dedicated to electing Black officials, said the president's blunt defense of Roe could further energize his base.\par \par \uc2 ''I appreciate that Biden is not being timid and is not measuring his words when it comes to this issue, which he has done before,'' Ms. James said. ''For him to say Roe is on the ballot, which it is, is a big thing.''\par \par \uc2 But Senator \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , Republican of Kentucky and the minority leader, who played a major role in shaping the court, described the ruling as ''courageous and correct.''\par \par \uc2 ''The justices applied the Constitution,'' he said in a statement. ''They carefully weighed the complex factors regarding precedent.''\par \par \uc2 After his speech on Friday, Mr. Biden left the lectern without answering shouted questions about whether he supported ending the filibuster to codify Roe. The White House also canceled its routine press briefing.\par \par \uc2 His attorney general, \uc2 Merrick B. Garland\uc2 , said in a statement on Friday that states should not ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, such as mifepristone. Mr. Biden also directed Health and Human Services to ensure the pill could be prescribed through remote telehealth systems, as well as pharmacies.\par \par \uc2 Kevin Brunell, a Washington resident who went to protest outside of the Supreme Court after Mr. Biden's speech, said he wanted to hear more from the president than a call to get to the polls.\par \par \uc2 ''I think they're going to use it as a leveraging tool to help get people to vote, when it seems, the way that things are going, there might be some more assertive action needed,'' Mr. Brunell said. ''I don't know what the answer is, but I don't think 'let's wait and see what happens in November' is going to cut it.''\par \par \uc2 Since the leaked draft of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opinion was published by Politico in early May, protesters have gathered outside the homes of justices and police have erected a fence around the court building.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden alluded to the tense atmosphere Friday when he asked ''everyone, no matter how deeply they care about this decision, to keep all protests peaceful.''\par \par \uc2 ''We must stand against violence in any form regardless of your rationale,'' Mr. Biden said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden already had a draft of his speech prepared before the decision that was largely written by his domestic policy adviser, Susan Rice, and Jennifer Klein, co-chair of the gender policy council. After the Supreme Court ruling was issued, Mr. Biden was briefed by his chief of staff, \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2 , in the Oval Office, according to a senior White House official, and quickly made revisions with this team after reviewing the decision.\par \par \uc2 A number of his senior aides, including his deputy chief of staff, \uc2 Jennifer O'Malley Dillon\uc2 , and the White House counsel \uc2 Dana Remus\uc2 , then held several calls on Friday with human rights organizations, state attorneys general and religious leaders.\par \par \uc2 And when Mr. Biden delivered the remarks, he pointed ahead to November.\par \par \uc2 ''Today the \uc2 Supreme Court of the United States\uc2  expressly took away a constitutional right for the American people,'' Mr. Biden said. ''They didn't limit it; they simply took it away.''\par \par \uc2 ''Roe is on the ballot,'' Mr. Biden added. ''Personal freedoms are on the ballot. The right to privacy, liberty, equality -- they're all on the ballot.''\par \par \uc2 Zach Montague contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Zach Montague contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 President Biden used his speech on Friday to urge voters to choose candidates who will pass laws to protect the right to an \uc2 . (PHOTOGRAPH BY T.J. KIRKPATRICK FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220625ei6p0003e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
