\par\par \uc2 NEWS ANALYSIS\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 A Conservative Supermajority May Be Just Getting Started\b0\par\par\uc2 By Charlie Savage \par \uc2 1,671 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The decision overruling Roe v. Wade exposed internal divisions among conservative justices about reconsidering other rights.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- The Supreme Court's decision on Friday to end the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  concluded one battle for now but immediately posed another far-reaching question: whether the judicial ground under rights in other personal matters, including contraception and same-sex marriage, is now also shaky.\par \par \uc2 The lack of a clear and consistent answer among the supermajority of conservative, Republican-appointed justices who control the Supreme Court prompted fear on the left, and anticipation among some on the other side of the ideological divide, that the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  decision could be just the beginning of a sharp rightward shift on issues that directly touch intimate personal choices.\par \par \uc2 Those reactions were stoked by Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 's concurring opinion, in which he explicitly said that precedents establishing those rights -- which relied on the same legal reasoning as the now-overturned Roe v. Wade -- should be reconsidered.\par \par \uc2 The majority opinion by Justice \uc2 Samuel A. Alito Jr\uc2 . sought to be more reassuring to those who see a judicial assault coming on same-sex marriage and contraception. He declared that a ruling that the 14th Amendment -- which forbids the government to take away people's freedom unfairly -- does not protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights should not be seen as imperiling precedents unrelated to ending fetal life. Yet his legal rationale implicitly called a series of such precedents into doubt.\par \par \uc2 The three dissenting liberals on the court said, in essence, don't be fooled. ''No one,'' they said, ''should be confident that this majority is done with its work.''\par \par \uc2 They wrote that precedents being cast aside by the court -- Roe v. Wade and Planned Parenthood v. Casey, a 1992 case that reaffirmed core parts of Roe -- were part of the same ''constitutional fabric'' behind ''settled freedoms involving bodily integrity, familial relationships and procreation.''\par \par \uc2 Then there was Justice \uc2 Brett M. Kavanaugh\uc2 , who sought to calm fears among supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights of even harsher and more wrenching changes to come. In his view, he said, states could not constitutionally bar women from traveling to another state to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Nor could they prosecute people for abortions before Friday's ruling took effect.\par \par \uc2 Friday's opinion had the immediate effect of allowing laws banning or severely curbing access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to snap into place in at least 20 states. But its implications for potential future disputes over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and for many other rights proclaimed by the Supreme Court since the second half of the 20th century could also be profound.\par \par \uc2 Over several generations, the modern court gradually ruled that a series of unwritten constitutional rights existed as part of the 14th Amendment. In addition to declaring a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the court struck down involuntary sterilization and laws interfering with who people could choose to live with or marry, along with decriminalizing contraception and same-sex intercourse.\par \par \uc2 The heart of Justice Alito's majority opinion is that the 14th Amendment protects only unwritten rights that were already understood to exist in 1868, when it was adopted. Many states then banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so it was wrong for the Supreme Court, in 1973's Roe v. Wade, to interpret the 14th Amendment as encompassing a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he reasoned.\par \par \uc2 The majority bloc in the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case -- Justices Alito, Thomas, Kavanaugh, \uc2 Neil M. Gorsuch\uc2  and Amy Coney Barrett -- modestly portrayed itself as getting the Supreme Court out of the business of drawing lines about which regulations go too far on the contentious subject. Under Justice Alito's opinion, so long as a state legislature has a ''rational basis'' for imposing a limit or ban on the procedure, the courts will not intervene.\par \par \uc2 But in a blistering but impotent joint dissent, the court's three remaining Democratic appointees -- Justices \uc2 Stephen G. Breyer\uc2 , \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  and Elena Kagan -- said the ruling would instead force the Supreme Court to wade further into hotly contested moral and philosophical issues, listing a dozen examples of new questions.\par \par \uc2 Those included whether and when a state must allow exceptions for a woman's life and health, what the ruling would mean for in vitro fertilization and miscarriage management, whether a state could bar advertising for out-of-state abortions or helping women get to out-of-state clinics, and whether it could bar women from traveling out of state or receiving \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication mailed by out-of-state pharmacies.\par \par \uc2 ''The majority does not save judges from unwieldy tests or extricate them from the sphere of controversy,'' they wrote. ''To the contrary, it discards a known, workable and predictable standard in favor of something novel and probably far more complicated.''\par \par \uc2 Against that backdrop, the concurring opinion by Justice Kavanaugh was particularly important because he appears to be the median judge on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues -- meaning he controls the fifth vote that decides which side to make into a majority in a closely divided case.\par \par \uc2 In addition to declaring that he thinks states cannot bar residents from traveling to another state to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Justice Kavanaugh strongly suggested that he thinks the Constitution requires \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans to include an exception when necessary to save the life of a mother.\par \par \uc2 The dissenting justices portrayed the prospect that the ruling will not prevent states that want to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legal from doing so as ''cold comfort'' for poor women in states that criminalize the procedures and who lack the money to travel to another state.\par \par \uc2 And, noting that ''no language in today's decision stops the federal government from prohibiting abortions nationwide, once again from the moment of conception and without exceptions for rape or incest,'' they wrote that if that happened, women seeking abortions would need to finance travel not to New York or California, but to Canada.\par \par \uc2 Friday's ruling also had implications that stretched far beyond potential future legal fights over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , calling into question the entire sweep of court precedents that established unwritten rights as deriving from the 14th Amendment's protections for liberty.\par \par \uc2 Political fights over judicial nominations often dwell in abstractions: Conservatives and Republicans press the view that the laws should be interpreted according to what their text was originally understood to mean. Liberals and Democrats tend to argue that the framers defined rights in general terms to permit future evolution in their scope and meaning by applying them in new ways in response to new societal understandings and conditions.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights ruling offered a concrete illustration: The three liberals in dissent acknowledged that no one thought there was a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1868, but also noted that women played no role in ratifying the 14th Amendment because they would not gain the right to vote for another half century. Locking down the meaning of freedoms from the vantage point of an antiquated society, they said, consigns women to the status of second-class citizens.\par \par \uc2 Against the backdrop of that debate, Justice Alito denied that the decision imperiled other precedents in which the Supreme Court proclaimed modern-era rights based on an evolving understanding of individual freedoms protected by the 14th Amendment -- including to contraception, sexual conduct with a member of the same sex or same-sex marriage.\par \par \uc2 He said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was different because it involved the destruction of fetal life, which the state had an interest in protecting.\par \par \uc2 ''To ensure that our decision is not misunderstood or mischaracterized, we emphasize that our decision concerns the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and no other right,'' he also wrote. ''Nothing in this opinion should be understood to cast doubt on precedents that do not concern \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 Even the conservative justices signaled disagreement among themselves over how much stock to put in that statement, however. At one end of the spectrum, Justice Thomas made no secret of his eagerness to press further and overturn those precedents, too.\par \par \uc2 He said he agreed with Justice Alito's line insofar as it meant that only \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was specifically ''at issue'' in the case decided on Friday. But he went on to call for the court to purge, ''at the earliest opportunity,'' all other cases that similarly reasoned that various unwritten rights are protected by the due process clause of the 14th Amendment.\par \par \uc2 At the other end of the spectrum, Chief Justice \uc2 John G. Roberts Jr\uc2 . agreed with the majority that a Mississippi law banning abortions after 15 weeks -- with no exceptions for rape or incest, including for minors -- should be upheld. But the chief justice, who has long favored narrow opinions and incremental change, declared that his five fellow conservatives had already gone too far in overturning Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 ''The court's opinion is thoughtful and thorough, but those virtues cannot compensate for the fact that its dramatic and consequential ruling is unnecessary to decide the case before us,'' he wrote.\par \par \uc2 For his part, Justice Kavanaugh echoed and emphasized Justice Alito's claim that the court's decision to overrule precedents about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not amount to overruling precedents about contraception and interracial or same-sex marriage, ''and does not threaten or cast doubt on those precedents.''\par \par \uc2 The dissenting justices expressed disbelief at Justices Alito's and Kavanaugh's attempts to distinguish \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from precedents about matters like contraception and same-sex intimacy and marriage. The bottom line, they wrote, was that the reasoning about the 14th Amendment and 1868 was the same for that entire constellation of rulings.\par \par \uc2 ''One of two things must be true,'' they wrote. ''Either the majority does not really believe in its own reasoning. Or if it does, all rights that have no history stretching back to the mid-19th century are insecure. Either the mass of the majority's opinion is hypocrisy, or additional constitutional rights are under threat. It is one or the other.''\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court has six justices appointed by Republican presidents and three appointed by Democrats. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Erin Schaff/The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220625ei6p0003d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
