\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Firms Are Largely Silent As Justices Overturn Roe\b0\par\par\uc2 By Emma Goldberg and Lora Kelley \par \uc2 1,588 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The corporate response to the Supreme Court's Friday decision was more muted, and the companies that did speak out mostly talked about their health care policies.\par \par \uc2 Companies had more than a month to formulate a response to the end of federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the United States, if they didn't weigh in immediately after a draft opinion was leaked in May.\par \par \uc2 But when the final decision arrived in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization on Friday, relatively few had anything to say about the outcome.\par \par \uc2 Most stayed silent, including some companies that are known for speaking out on social issues such as \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2  and L.G.B.T.Q. rights. Some of the corporations that blacked out their \uc2 Instagram\uc2  pages in 2020 or featured rainbow flags on their websites for Pride Month have so far been hesitant to comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''Executives are feeling some trepidation around this,'' said Dave Fleet, the head of global digital crisis at Edelman, a consulting firm. ''They're concerned about backlash because they know there's no way to please everyone.''\par \par \uc2 Many of the businesses that did make public statements on Friday opted to address the way the Supreme Court's decision would affect their workers' access to health care. In some cases they avoided the word ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 '' altogether, perhaps aiming for a more palatable response.\par \par \uc2 ''We have processes in place so that an employee who may be unable to access care in one location has affordable coverage for receiving similar levels of care in another location,'' \uc2 Disney\uc2  executives wrote in a memo to staff, adding that this included ''family planning (including pregnancy-related decisions).''\par \par \uc2 Other companies that came forward Friday to say they would cover employee travel expenses for abortions include \uc2 Warner Bros\uc2 ., Cond\u233\'00\'E9 Nast, \uc2 BuzzFeed\uc2 , \uc2 Vox Media\uc2 , \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , Snap, \uc2 Macy's\uc2 , \uc2 Intuit\uc2  and \uc2 Dick's Sporting Goods\uc2 . They joined a group including \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , \uc2 Yelp\uc2 , \uc2 Airbnb\uc2 , \uc2 Netflix\uc2 , Patagonia, \uc2 DoorDash\uc2 , \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2 , \uc2 Levi Strauss & Co\uc2 ., \uc2 PayPal\uc2 , OKCupid, \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 , \uc2 Kroger\uc2 , \uc2 Google\uc2 , \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , \uc2 Paramount\uc2 , \uc2 Nike\uc2 , Chobani, \uc2 Lyft\uc2  and Reddit that had previously implemented similar policies.\par \par \uc2 ''The employer is the way a lot of people access the health care system,'' Mr. Fleet added. ''You're seeing companies look inwardly first.''\par \par \uc2 A few companies accompanied those policy changes with statements. Roger Lynch, the head of Cond\u233\'00\'E9 Nast, called the decision ''a crushing blow to \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 .'' \uc2 Lyft\uc2  said the ruling ''will hurt millions of women.'' \uc2 BuzzFeed\uc2 's chief executive, \uc2 Jonah Peretti\uc2 , called it ''regressive and horrific.'' Some business leaders spoke out too, with \uc2 Bill Gates\uc2 , the co-founder and former head of \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , calling the ruling ''an unjust and unacceptable setback,'' and \uc2 Sheryl Sandberg\uc2 , the former chief operating officer of \uc2 Meta\uc2 , writing that it ''threatens to undo the progress women have made in the workplace.''\par \par \uc2 But many companies that have spoken out on social issues like racism did not respond to requests for comment or declined to comment after the Supreme Court's decision, including Target, \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , \uc2 Coca-Cola\uc2 , Delta and Wendy's. \uc2 Hobby Lobby\uc2 , which in 2014 brought a successful suit to the Supreme Court challenging whether employer-provided health care had to include contraception, declined to comment on the Dobbs decision.\par \par \uc2 In recent years there has been a growing expectation that companies weigh in on political and social issues. The share of online American adults who believe that companies have a responsibility to participate in debates about current issues has risen in the past year, according to the consumer research company \uc2 Forrester\uc2 . The expectation is even more pronounced among younger social media users, according to research from \uc2 Sprout Social\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 When George Floyd was killed by the police in 2020, public companies and their foundations committed over $49 billion to fighting racial inequality. Last year, after Georgia's Republican-led legislature restricted voter access, some chief executives, including from Coca-Cola and Delta Air Lines, criticized the law, and 72 Black business leaders published a letter urging corporate leaders to ''publicly oppose any discriminatory legislation.''\par \par \uc2 With \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , public opinion is a little different: \uc2 Forrester\uc2  found that fewer respondents believed companies should take a stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Polls have consistently found that a majority of Americans believe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all or most cases, but a recent survey by \uc2 Pew Research Center\uc2  found that people have wide-ranging views about morality on the issue. Companies fear the backlash that could come from taking a stance on the issue.\par \par \uc2 ''When it comes to the range of politicized issues within the sphere of a brand's impact, few are as divisive and deeply personal as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 '' said Mike Proulx, a vice president and research director at \uc2 Forrester\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Political engagement is rarely a straightforward choice for company leaders. \uc2 Disney\uc2 , which had long avoided partisan politics, faced internal backlash this year when it didn't take a strong stance on Florida's so-called ''Don't Say Gay'' law, but then Florida lawmakers revoked its special tax benefits when it did. \uc2 John Gibson\uc2 , the chief executive of the gaming company Tripwire Interactive, was swiftly replaced after speaking out in favor of Texas' ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after six weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 A 2020 study of 149 firms published in the Journal of Marketing found that corporate activism had a negative effect on a company's stock market performance, though it found a positive effect on sales if the activism was consistent with the values of the company's consumers.\par \par \uc2 Both engaging and deciding not to engage can come at a price.\par \par \uc2 ''You've got to be careful not to take the wrong lessons from some of those moments,'' said Mr. Fleet, of Edelman. ''It would be very easy to look at companies that made missteps and say 'well, we shouldn't say anything,' whereas in fact some clients not saying anything is the mistake that was made.''\par \par \uc2 Some companies warned staff on Friday to be careful how they discuss the ruling in the workplace. ''There will be an intense amount of public debate over this decision,'' \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 's head of human resources wrote to staff. ''Please remember that we must always treat each other respectfully, even when our opinions differ.''\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  said publicly on Friday that it would reimburse employees for travel expenses to get abortions. But the company then told its workers not to openly discuss the court's ruling on wide-reaching communication channels inside the company, according to three employees, citing a policy that put ''strong guardrails around social, political and sensitive conversations'' in the workplace.\par \par \uc2 But there are other companies that haven't shied away from more full-throated statements on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and they are urging other businesses to match their tone and commitment.\par \par \uc2 OkCupid sent a notification to app users in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions encouraging them to contact their elected officials in support of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Melissa Hobley, its global chief marketing officer, has been working behind the scenes to get other women business leaders to make commitments to support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''We had to say screw the risk,'' she said. ''This is an economic problem, this is a marketing problem. If you're in highly visible, highly competitive industries like tech, law, finance, you are all fighting after female talent.''\par \par \uc2 Jeremy Stoppelman\uc2 , the chief executive of \uc2 Yelp\uc2 , said he felt that it was important to speak out about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access whether or not there was a business case for doing so, though he knew that there would be users who opposed that decision.\par \par \uc2 ''Certainly when you speak out on these issues not everyone is going to agree,'' he said. ''As we looked at this, we felt quite strongly that it was the right thing to do,'' adding, ''it's been 50 years of settled law.''\par \par \uc2 Some business leaders said they were concerned about how \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions will affect their ability to recruit workers, especially those whose companies are based in the 13 states that will ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  immediately or very quickly with Roe overturned. Those states include Texas, where tech companies have flocked in recent years.\par \par \uc2 Research commissioned by the Tara Health Foundation found that two-thirds of college-educated workers surveyed would be discouraged from taking a job in Texas because of its restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law and would not apply for jobs in other states that passed similar laws.\par \par \uc2 ''\uc2 Employers\uc2  like us may be the last line of defense,'' said Sarah Jackel, chief operating officer of Civitech, a 55-person company based in Texas that builds technology tools for political campaigns. The company committed to covering travel expenses for employees in need of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  immediately after the passage of Texas' ban, S.B. 8.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Jackel said the policy had strong support from both employees and investors, though the company declined to share if anyone had used it.\par \par \uc2 ''It makes good business sense,'' she added. ''There's no reason we should be putting our employees in the position of having to choose between keeping their job or carrying out an unwanted pregnancy.''\par \par \uc2 Emily Flitter, Lauren Hirsch, Mike Isaac, Kate Kelly, Ryan Mac, Benjamin Mullin and Katie Robertson contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Emily Flitter, Lauren Hirsch, Mike Isaac, Kate Kelly, Ryan Mac, Benjamin Mullin and Katie Robertson contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Google is among companies that will cover travel expenses for abortions. (PHOTOGRAPH BY LAURA MORTON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Disney's announcement of coverage on Friday avoided the word ''\uc2 .'' (PHOTOGRAPH BY EVE EDELHEIT FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (B4) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220625ei6p00033\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
