\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Searching for an  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Clinic, and Finding Just the Opposite\b0\par\par\uc2 By Emma Cott, Nilo Tabrizy and Nailah Morgan \par \uc2 3,525 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The moment Stephanie Moore saw the positive line appear on her home pregnancy test in 2019, she fell to her knees and sobbed. She had recently graduated from college and knew she wasn't ready for a baby.\par \par \uc2 ''I felt like the world was closing in around me,'' Ms. Moore, 31, recently said. ''I don't think I've ever cried like that before.''\par \par \uc2 Then, she picked herself up, opened \uc2 Google\uc2  on her phone and typed: ''\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  near me.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Moore quickly found a site for the Grapevine Women's Clinic, just a short drive from her suburban Dallas home. ''Take Control. Start with a Free \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Consultation,'' the site read. She had recently been diagnosed with a blood disorder, and wanted to know how an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would physically affect her.\par \par \uc2 ''I didn't really need someone to go over my options,'' she said. ''I just needed more information as far as my health.''\par \par \uc2 While Texas recently enacted a strict law prohibiting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after about six weeks of pregnancy, the procedure was still largely available there at the time. When Ms. Moore called the number on the website, the person who picked up could not answer her questions. Instead, she said, the agent encouraged her to schedule an appointment to see if the pregnancy was ''viable,'' trying to steer her toward an ultrasound.\par \par \uc2 The woman on the phone did not work for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic, or even a licensed medical facility. She was a counselor for Human Coalition, a Christian nonprofit that is quietly orchestrating a national effort to end \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Using local numbers that route to anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  call centers and the promise of free ultrasounds, the organization seeks to intercept newly pregnant women and persuade them to keep their babies.\par \par \uc2 ''Studies show that the majority of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -minded women will choose LIFE after seeing a sonogram of their child,'' a page on Human Coalition's main website reads.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  organizations have existed for decades, setting up so-called crisis pregnancy centers near Planned Parenthood clinics, and advertising on billboards and in the Yellow Pages. Human Coalition helped shift the battle to people's phones and computers, where it relies on search engine optimization, targeted digital ads and an arsenal of websites that look like local health clinics to find women searching for abortions across the country -- including in places where it will remain legal if the Supreme Court reverses Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 ''The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -determined woman will not walk into a pregnancy center voluntarily,'' Brian Fisher, the organization's co-founder, said in 2017. ''They must be outreached to and found.''\par \par \uc2 As its reach has widened, Human Coalition has earned lucrative government contracts to provide alternatives to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Nearly half of Human Coalition's funding now comes from Republican-led states. At the same time, its lobbying arm shapes policy in states that are quickly moving to restrict or outlaw the procedure, and helped push through S.B. 8, the strict new law in Texas.\par \par \uc2 With a decision on Roe looming, the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  landscape across the United States is set to be fundamentally altered -- and organizations like Human Coalition are planning to seize the moment to expand.\par \par \uc2 ''In a post-Roe world, our work really begins in a lot of ways.'' Chelsey Youman, the director of public policy at Human Coalition, said in an interview. ''We have all of these women who are out there and they need help. We know them, we care for them, we love them.''\par \par \uc2 ''\uc2 Children\uc2  rescued''\par \par \uc2 Inside the glossy high-rise that houses Human Coalition's headquarters in Plano, Texas, large TV monitors count the number of ''children rescued'' nationwide. Floor-to-ceiling photographs of mothers and babies, brightly colored graphics and religious quotes fill walls throughout the office.\par \par \uc2 In an adjacent suite, dozens of call center staff talk to women around the country facing unexpected pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 Much of Human Coalition's work happens in that suite. The nonprofit said that in 2021, it served 18,000 clients via telecare and saw 2,500 people at its seven brick-and-mortar facilities across the United States. About two-thirds of its clients are considering \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it said.\par \par \uc2 For two days in February, The Times visited Human Coalition headquarters and conducted limited interviews with executives. During that time, the number of ''children rescued'' ticked up on the TV monitors by 22, to reach 22,740.\par \par \uc2 When it was founded in 2009, the organization -- first called Media Revolution Ministries and later Online for Life -- operated as a data-driven online marketing and referral service for crisis pregnancy centers. It eventually grew to run its own C.P.C.s, forming a national network that includes partnerships with independent facilities -- allowing it to reach women across the country through targeted online ads.\par \par \uc2 Crisis pregnancy centers are core to the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement now, and outnumber actual \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers by more than 3:1, according to Andrea Swartzendruber and Danielle Lambert, researchers at the University of Georgia's College of Public Health, whose team has mapped C.P.C.s across the country.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this year, Times reporters went to all of Human Coalition's brick-and-mortar centers in five states. There was very little foot traffic, and the few women who agreed to speak with reporters said they had always planned to keep their pregnancies. They said they received free baby supplies and health care referrals, and were happy with their services so far.\par \par \uc2 But in interviews with nearly a dozen women who called or visited C.P.C.s linked to Human Coalition, many described feeling deceived into thinking that they would receive abortions, or at least unbiased advice about how to get one. Several of them later shared their experiences publicly in \uc2 Google\uc2  reviews online.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Youman denied that the organization intentionally misleads women seeking abortions, pointing to disclaimers on its websites that say it does not provide or refer for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A Times review of the 56 websites linked to Human Coalition, however, found that the disclaimers are tucked into the legal terms of service, or other places where they could easily be missed.\par \par \uc2 Not a doctor's office\par \par \uc2 At first, Keri Boardman, 28, wasn't sure if she had come to the right location in Charlotte, N.C.\par \par \uc2 ''I had a hard time finding the place because it didn't say the same name that it said on the website,'' she said of her 2020 visit. ''So I rode past it like three times before I got in there.''\par \par \uc2 She had no idea that she had stumbled into a crisis pregnancy center run by Human Coalition, in part because the center goes by three different names, each with its own website: Pregnancy Resource Center of Charlotte, Charlotte Women's Clinic and Health for Her.\par \par \uc2 Using multiple names is common for crisis pregnancy centers, said Dr. Swartzendruber. ''They'll have one website that is directed toward potential clients that often hides their religious affiliation,'' she said, ''but their organizational website directed to volunteers or donors is much more transparent about their goals.''\par \par \uc2 Human Coalition employs registered nurses and social workers, but the centers themselves are not licensed or inspected by state health departments.\par \par \uc2 At the Charlotte center, Ms. Boardman was initially given a urine test, which showed that she was pregnant. Next, a counselor guided her to an ultrasound room, but the technician said that she couldn't see Ms. Boardman's pregnancy on the monitor, and asked her to come back two weeks later.\par \par \uc2 When Ms. Boardman returned, the technician printed an image showing a very early pregnancy. However, it was labeled 13 weeks and two days -- indicating that she was too far along to take pills to terminate her pregnancy. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills currently have federal approval for use up to 10 weeks.\par \par \uc2 A counselor then asked her what she knew about surgical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''That's when things went a little different,'' Ms. Boardman said.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Boardman had just moved to Charlotte, and was starting a new job while enrolling her two children in school. She was uncertain about the pregnancy, but said that her experience at Health for Her was convincing. She decided she could not go through with an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Two weeks later, during a checkup with her obstetrician, a new ultrasound image showed that Human Coalition had gotten it wrong. Ms. Boardman was actually only nine weeks pregnant. That would have put her at the very beginning of her pregnancy and eligible for the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill when she first visited Health for Her.\par \par \uc2 ''That part was when I really noticed that, medically, they don't know what they're doing,'' Ms. Boardman said of her ultrasound at Health for Her.\par \par \uc2 In an email to The Times, Human Coalition said its ultrasound technicians are all credentialed and overseen by a licensed doctor, and that it is confident in the accuracy of that work. But none of the women who spoke to The Times recalled seeing a doctor, and Human Coalition declined to elaborate on their role.\par \par \uc2 Several \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers who spoke to The Times said they do not accept ultrasounds from crisis pregnancy centers because they are often inaccurate.\par \par \uc2 ''Invasive'' messages\par \par \uc2 Stephanie Moore didn't expect to hear anything more from the Grapevine Women's Clinic after she called them in 2019. She hung up after the agent on the phone told her they do not provide abortions.\par \par \uc2 But for two weeks, the center continued to call and text her, asking her to follow up. The last text message shocked her:\par \par \uc2 ''It was just very invasive,'' said Ms. Moore, who shared voice mail and text messages with The Times. ''I didn't form a relationship. I wasn't a patient. There was nothing of the sort, and they were just so persistent.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Moore did not respond to the final text, which arrived the same day she took her first \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill at Planned Parenthood.\par \par \uc2 Had she responded, her answer would not have been protected by HIPAA, the federal patient privacy law. The law applies only to health care and other related providers who bill for their services. Most crisis pregnancy centers, including those run by Human Coalition, offer their services for free.\par \par \uc2 Human Coalition's leaders say the organization takes client privacy seriously, and that staff members are trained to protect sensitive information. The personal data it collects is wide-ranging, from the date of a woman's last period, to her \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  history and current pregnancy plans. Human Coalition records all phone calls that come into its call centers and says that its social services department sometimes works with clients over a period of years.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates worry that anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  centers could share private information with law enforcement agencies in order to help track or even prosecute women in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  becomes illegal.\par \par \uc2 ''We are expecting to see a huge increase in criminalization,'' said Kim Clark, a senior attorney at Legal Voice, a legal advocacy group that supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. ''Crisis pregnancy centers are in a perfect position to facilitate those types of investigations and criminal prosecutions because they collect massive amounts of very personal data about individual pregnant people.''\par \par \uc2 Ending \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  with taxpayer funds\par \par \uc2 In its early years, Human Coalition was entirely dependent on private donors, including the Thirteen Foundation, a conservative organization funded by the fracking billionaire \uc2 Farris Wilks\uc2  and his wife, JoAnn.\par \par \uc2 But as it grew, it needed to find new sources of income. In 2017, tax records show, Human Coalition received $600,000 from North Carolina for a two-year pilot project to ''assist women experiencing crisis pregnancies to continue their pregnancies to full term.''\par \par \uc2 Since then, Human Coalition has drawn more money from states seeking to curtail \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- including Texas, Georgia and Tennessee -- accounting for almost all of the organization's growth.\par \par \uc2 According to Human Coalition's 2021 annual report, government money made up 44 percent of the $18.7 million it generated in revenue. The organization recently applied to provide hotline services for the state of Arkansas, and hopes to win similar funding in Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 This increase fits a nationwide pattern. Public funding for crisis pregnancy centers across the United States has jumped from $17 million a decade ago to nearly $89 million this year, according to a tally of state budget figures by The Associated Press.\par \par \uc2 But as more state governments fund C.P.C.s through ''Alternatives to \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 '' programs, critics argue that the money is being spent with little oversight. In a 2019 report, the North Carolina Department of Health said that it was ''unable to determine the effectiveness or cost of expanding'' Human Coalition's work, after the organization failed to comply with reporting requirements. Despite that review, in 2021, the state's legislature allocated $6.4 million to Human Coalition in its new budget, more than a tenfold increase from 2017.\par \par \uc2 ''We haven't even shown that the money that they're spending already is doing anything,'' said Julie von Haefen, a Democratic state representative from North Carolina who opposed the funding. ''Now we want to increase the money? It's just bad government.''\par \par \uc2 The nuclear family, at all costs\par \par \uc2 Four days after Kathleen Cooper's partner was arrested for domestic violence in January 2020, a \uc2 Google\uc2  search for ''free pregnancy test'' led her to Cornerstone Pregnancy Services in Elyria, Ohio. Cornerstone is an independently run crisis pregnancy center that partners with Human Coalition.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Cooper was struggling. On top of the abuse, she was already an unemployed single mother of two. She was determined to end her pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 ''If I brought this child into the world, that child would be at risk of being abused,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Cornerstone was close to Ms. Cooper's home, and she'd been there before, when she was pregnant with her daughter years earlier. The center had persuaded her not to end her pregnancy then, and she knew it didn't provide abortions. But she couldn't afford the procedure, and thought Cornerstone might help her figure out how to qualify for public funding. The closest Planned Parenthood was 45 minutes away, and Ms. Cooper did not have a reliable car.\par \par \uc2 After confirming that she was pregnant, Ms. Cooper, 37, said that a counselor at the center interrogated her about her choice to terminate, and encouraged her to try couples counseling with her abusive partner.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Cooper's partner was later convicted, and sentenced to 90 days in jail, court records show.\par \par \uc2 Cornerstone's executive director, Cynthia Carter-Harris, did not respond to The Times's request for comment about how the center typically handles domestic violence cases. Dr. Joseph P. Spirnak, who was not present, but whose signature appears at the bottom of Ms. Cooper's pregnancy verification form, also declined an interview request.\par \par \uc2 In an email, Human Coalition said that it helps Cornerstone find and stay in touch with clients, but that it could not speak to its practices. Had she come to a Human Coalition facility, it would have offered her resources to ensure her safety, it wrote.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Cooper eventually made it to Planned Parenthood in Cleveland, where she had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  two weeks later with aid from an Ohio \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  fund. A counselor at Planned Parenthood also encouraged her to see a therapist.\par \par \uc2 She has since split from her partner, and said that her experience at Cornerstone inspired her to become an activist. She now volunteers with \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  organizations and speaks publicly about her \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Googling \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 For years, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates have called on \uc2 Google\uc2  to stop allowing crisis pregnancy centers to run misleading \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related ads on the search giant's platform. Because crisis pregnancy centers aren't selling anything, they do not fall under the purview of the \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2  Act, which prohibits unfair or deceptive advertising.\par \par \uc2 In response to the criticism, \uc2 Google\uc2  made a change in 2019, requiring advertisers in the United States, Britain and Ireland to be certified to use \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related keywords. A disclosure saying ''Does not provide abortions'' or ''Provides abortions'' appeared in gray under each paid ad.\par \par \uc2 ''These are design choices that \uc2 Google\uc2  is making, and algorithmic choices that \uc2 Google\uc2  is making,'' said Emily Peterson-Cassin, a lawyer with \uc2 Public Citizen\uc2 , a nonprofit consumer advocacy organization. ''That's really insufficient to depend on a small disclaimer that maybe folks are going to read. Maybe they're not.''\par \par \uc2 In an email, a \uc2 Google\uc2  spokesperson said that protecting users from harmful and misleading ads was a top priority. The company in June made a subtle design change to its \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related disclosures, which now appear above each paid ad.\par \par \uc2 ''We know that people come to \uc2 Google\uc2  looking for information they can trust during deeply personal moments,'' the spokesperson wrote. ''While we have heard this type of transparency has been enormously helpful since it was implemented in 2019, we recently updated these disclosures to make them even more visible.''\par \par \uc2 In 2020, the search giant also added a verification feature that allows users to click on any ad to see who is behind it.\par \par \uc2 But some lawmakers say the company needs to do much more.\par \par \uc2 In a letter sent June 17, a group of Democrats in Congress urged \uc2 Google\uc2  to better protect users from misleading \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related search results, including paid ads. The letter came after a recent study found that in some states, one in 10 \uc2 Google\uc2  searches for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  led to centers that oppose the procedure.\par \par \uc2 David Sumara, the co-owner of an online marketing agency that places ads for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and other clients, said crisis pregnancy centers shouldn't be allowed to advertise using the keyword ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 '' at all. He noted that the search giant already has a precedent for this kind of policy.\par \par \uc2 To prevent fraud, the company requires locksmiths and garage door repair businesses in the United States to become verified as providers of those services before they can run ads that use related keywords.\par \par \uc2 ''If the goal of \uc2 Google\uc2  is to protect searchers,'' said Mr. Sumara, ''then we would like to see \uc2 Google\uc2  apply those same regulations to something that's arguably more sensitive and more impactful to someone's life.''\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2 's spokesperson said the company allows ads that promote different perspectives, and that its policies are tailored to specific issues facing each industry.\par \par \uc2 ''The central question here is how come \uc2 Google\uc2  has this much power in the first place?'' Ms. Peterson-Cassin asked.\par \par \uc2 A post-Roe future\par \par \uc2 The evening that Politico published a leaked draft opinion from the Supreme Court indicating that it planned to overturn Roe v. Wade, Human Coalition's president, Jeff Bradford, sent an email to the organization's donors and supporters.\par \par \uc2 ''If the reversal becomes final, the pro-life movement is ready to care for these families,'' he wrote.\par \par \uc2 It was that kind of care that Keri Boardman was seeking when she called Health for Her again in 2021. She had miscarried the year before, and she saw this new pregnancy as a sign.\par \par \uc2 ''I was just like, OK, God's telling me I'm ready now,'' she said. ''I told him I wasn't ready before. I'm ready now.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Boardman was nervous, having just lost a pregnancy. Because of the pandemic, she said, her doctor couldn't see her until the end of the first trimester. Despite her prior experience with Health for Her, she figured a free ultrasound there could help soothe her nerves.\par \par \uc2 She called the same number as before, and was greeted by a friendly agent. But when she told her she planned to continue the pregnancy, she said the agent's tone became curt.\par \par \uc2 ''Right away I could hear the change as soon as I said 'I'm keeping my baby,''' Ms. Boardman said. ''She shut me down and was like, 'Call your doctor.'''\par \par \uc2 Human Coalition told The Times that it provides ''holistic care'' to all of its clients, and did not respond to questions about whether it prioritizes certain services for women seeking abortions.\par \par \uc2 In Ms. Boardman's case, she did not have time to understand why she wasn't offered an ultrasound this time. The agent had already hung up the phone.\par \par \uc2 Marina Trahan Martinez and Leslye Davis contributed reporting. Daniel Rogers, Susan C. Beachy, Ben Decker contributed research.\par \par \uc2 Marina Trahan Martinez and Leslye Davis contributed reporting. Daniel Rogers, Susan C. Beachy, Ben Decker contributed research.\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPHS BY LESLYE DAVIS FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Inside Human Coalition's headquarters in Plano, Texas. Television monitors track the number of ''children rescued.'' (PHOTOGRAPH BY ORA DEKORNFELD FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); OCT. 5, 2020: A Human Coalition ultrasound put Ms. Boardman's pregnancy at 13 weeks and two days.; OCT. 22, 2020: A later ultrasound from Ms. Boardman's doctor showed she was only nine weeks pregnant. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY KERI BOARDMAN) (A18);  (PHOTOGRAPH BY ORA DEKORNFELD FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); An Expanding Nationwide Network (PHOTOGRAPH BY ALIZA AUFRICHTIG AND REBECCA LIEBERMAN/THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A19) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220624ei6o0004c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
