\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Nine-Term Congressman Squeaks Past Challenger in South Texas Runoff\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jazmine Ulloa \par \uc2 906 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The victory by Representative \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  notched a narrow win for moderate Democrats who saw the race as a proxy battle over the party's broader direction.\par \par \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2 , the embattled nine-term congressman from South Texas in the political fight of his career, won his Democratic primary runoff against a young progressive opponent, overcoming fierce attacks on his record and an F.B.I. investigation with a last-minute push from top national Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Cuellar, whose victory was called by The Associated Press after a nail-bitingly close May 24 runoff led to a recount, defeated Jessica Cisneros, a lawyer who was once an intern for the congressman. Unofficial results released by the Texas Democratic Party showed he clinched the nomination by only 289 votes.\par \par \uc2 His survival in a rare primary runoff for the House seat representing Texas' 28th Congressional District notched a narrow win for moderate Democrats who had viewed his race as a proxy battle for a broader direction for the party.\par \par \uc2 The two candidates were backed by Democratic stars from their respective centrist and progressive corners, and they drew sharp ideological distinctions between each other on many of their party's top issues, including \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , immigration, the energy industry and the environment.\par \par \uc2 Soon after the recount totals were released, Alexandra Rojas, executive director of Justice Democrats, a progressive political action committee, expressed anger with House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  and top Democratic leaders for refusing to support Ms. Cisneros.\par \par \uc2 ''The party establishment would rather protect one of their own than deliver results for Democratic voters and South Texas families,'' Ms. Rojas said in a statement. ''It's disgraceful and I am deeply worried about November.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Cuellar, 66, the last anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Democrat in the House, represented the old guard. He emphasized the federal dollars he had brought to the area, his efforts to harden border security and his firm stance against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , citing his Catholic faith and the role it plays in his border region.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Cuellar once held an ''A'' rating from the N.R.A., but that dropped in 2020 after he voted in favor of expanded background checks. The runoff was held the same day that a gunman killed 19 children and two adults at a school in Uvalde, Texas, which lies in a bordering district.\par \par \uc2 He has supported more oil drilling and often votes with Republicans on energy policy. His tough talk on immigration sparked anger and concern among immigrant-rights activists, who said he was playing into dangerous right-wing characterizations about immigrants and the border.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this year, the F.B.I. raided Mr. Cuellar's home in the border city of Laredo as part of an ongoing investigation that appears to be tied to a probe into the political influence of Azerbaijan, the former Soviet republic.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Cisneros, 28, like Mr. Cuellar, is a Laredo native and the child of Mexican immigrants. She first challenged the longtime congressman in 2020 when she was fresh out of law school, and came within 3.6 percentage points of pushing him out. In March, Ms. Cisneros fell fewer than 1,000 votes short of Mr. Cuellar's total, forcing the two into a runoff.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Cisneros, who has worked with asylum-seeking migrant families, approached immigration and border policy from a humanitarian perspective. She framed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a health care issue, and championed progressive causes such as Medicare for All, a $15 minimum wage and the Green New Deal.\par \par \uc2 In early May, the leaked Supreme Court draft opinion overturning Roe v. Wade, which established a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , appeared to give Ms. Cisneros a bump of momentum late in the race. Mr. Cuellar was the only House Democrat to vote against the Women's Health Protection Act, which would have guaranteed the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  through federal law.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Cuellar's district runs from Laredo to San Antonio, and redistricting changes made it slightly more Democratic, with more voters from Bexar County. The district dropped from 76.9 percent Hispanic to 75.3 percent, but a slight rise in white voters was expected to help Ms. Cisneros if those new voters were San Antonio liberals.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Cisneros stumped with progressive firebrands -- Representative \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2  of New York, and Senators Bernie Sanders of Vermont and Elizabeth Warren of Massachusetts. Like Ms. Ocasio-Cortez, Ms. Cisneros was recruited by the Justice Democrats, a progressive political action committee that had placed a full-page ad in a local newspaper looking for candidates. Laredo community leaders submitted her name.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Cuellar received the endorsements of House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , who recorded robocalls for him, as well as Representative \uc2 James E. Clyburn\uc2  of South Carolina, the House majority whip, and Representative \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  of New York, the chairman of the House Democratic Caucus.\par \par \uc2 The race was contentious at times.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Cisneros criticized Mr. Cuellar as a corrupt politician. In the final stretch of the contest, billboards appeared in Laredo that made allegations about Ms. Cisneros's policy stances as well as her personal life, calling her a ''home wrecker.'' Mr. Cuellar denied he had anything to do with the billboards and said he did not condone them.\par \par \uc2 Representative Henry Cuellar, left, the last anti-\uc2  Democrat in the House, withstood fierce attacks by his rival. (PHOTOGRAPH BY KAYLEE GREENLEE FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220622ei6m0004k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
