\par\par \uc2 NONFICTION\par \uc2 Book Review Desk; SECTBR\par \b \uc2 Human Shield\b0\par\par\uc2 By Christina Cauterucci \par \uc2 859 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 BODIES ON THE LINE\par \par \uc2 At the Front Lines of the Fight to Protect \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in America\par \par \uc2 By Lauren Rankin\par \par \uc2 When the Supreme Court's 1973 decision in Roe v. Wade made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legal across the United States, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates had to pivot. They could no longer ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the statehouse, so they took their fight to a new arena: the doctor's office.\par \par \uc2 In ''Bodies on the Line,'' a new history of the contested territory that surrounds the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic, Lauren Rankin writes of militant anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups that barricaded clinic doors and extremists who carried out bombings, shootings and arson attacks. Even the peaceful protesters, who gathered outside (and sometimes inside) the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics that proliferated after Roe, were unrelenting, bent on exacting a toll from patients -- if not the physical punishment of forced childbirth, then the emotional punishment of petty insults and threats of fiery damnation. ''Their goal? To make it as difficult and traumatic as possible to access an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' Rankin writes.\par \par \uc2 ''Bodies on the Line'' tells the stories of communities that came together with the opposite goal in mind: to offer patients comfort and protection. During the worst of the blockades in the 1980s and '90s, volunteers known as clinic defenders formed human chains to create clear paths to clinic entrances. Ad hoc \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups that coalesced around besieged clinics became organized networks of clinic escorts, who accompanied patients past the protesters accusing them of murder. The simplicity of that work, which continues at many health centers today, belies the fortifying power of companionship in the face of harassment. One woman who became a clinic escort in 1989 told Rankin, ''Just by showing up, I was doing enough.''\par \par \uc2 Rankin, who spent years escorting at a clinic in New Jersey, charts the rise of this practice with evident admiration for the forerunners whose trials and errors shaped the tactics volunteers use today. The book is thick with testimonies from dozens of clinic escorts, but none of them stick around or stand out enough to give the text an emotional or narrative core. Details of the escorts' histories run together, in part because their work is, by nature, repetitive. (Laws restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access vary by state, but anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protesters are a constant.)\par \par \uc2 With the great breadth of case studies she conveys, Rankin leaves little room to explore the thornier questions she glances past. Aside from blockades, which a federal law made illegal in 1994, these demonstrations can present a conflict between one constitutionally protected activity (speech) and another (\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ). Rankin implies that lawmakers should impose greater penalties and restrictions on demonstrators, but she never lays out exactly what the ideal set of laws would be, or how they would balance the competing rights of patients and protesters.\par \par \uc2 But ''Bodies on the Line,'' like clinic escorting, is primarily concerned with the personal side of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care. As volunteers wrap their bodies around a patient to shield her face from a protester's camera, they are not thinking about the legislators who passed a law requiring her to make two separate appointments, 48 hours apart, for her procedure, in an effort to discourage her from following through. ''It's about responding, as a human being, to another human being's needs,'' Rankin writes -- an act of service that recognizes \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care ''not as a caustic political fight but as a matter of human dignity.''\par \par \uc2 As Rankin acknowledges, the book comes at a critical moment for the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the United States, as the Supreme Court prepares to hand down a decision on Mississippi's ban on abortions after 15 weeks. Given the court's conservative majority, the justices may take the opportunity to gut Roe or overturn it completely. But ''the law isn't the beginning or the end of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access,'' Rankin writes. The very need for clinic escort teams is a testament to the inability of current laws to secure safe, comfortable care for patients.\par \par \uc2 In an age of constitutional protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, those limits are discouraging. When such protections are threatened, the knowledge that legal rights were just one chapter of the playbook can offer something like hope. In Texas, where a 2021 law compelled clinics to cease providing abortions after as early as the sixth week of pregnancy, organizations have raised money to help people travel out of state for later abortions. Clinic escorts are repurposing their skills by offering other services for those patients: hotel booking, airport pickups and a friendly face in a town far from home.\par \par \uc2 None of that can reverse a Supreme Court ruling that would force people to carry unwanted pregnancies to term. What clinic escorts do is ''so much smaller than that,'' Rankin writes. ''And so much bigger.''\par \par \uc2 Christina Cauterucci is a senior writer at Slate and host of the podcast ''Outward.'' BODIES ON THE LINE At the Front Lines of the Fight to Protect \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in America By Lauren Rankin 290 pp. Counterpoint Press. $26.\par \par \uc2 A clinic escort outside a Planned Parenthood. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ZACH GIBSON/GETTY IMAGES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220619ei6j0002v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
