\par\par \uc2 JESSICA GROSE\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Pregnancy Can Affect the Body for a Lifetime\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jessica Grose \par \uc2 1,831 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 19\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Lately, I've been thinking a lot about Alexandra of Denmark, Great Britain's queen from 1901 to 1910. Called ''Alix'' by her loved ones, she was the wife of King Edward VII, the notoriously promiscuous son of Queen Victoria, called ''the playboy prince'' by the biographer Jane Ridley.\par \par \uc2 As with many royals before her, one of Alix's jobs was to produce heirs, which she did at a rapid pace: She had six live births between 1864 and 1871. Five of those babies lived to adulthood, but the sixth only lived for a day. Because she was royalty, we know more about her pregnancies and births than we do about those of almost any of her contemporaries: Despite having the best medical care available in the 19th century and a coterie of ladies-in-waiting attending to her, experiencing pregnancy and childbirth six times in less than a decade wreaked havoc on Alix's body.\par \par \uc2 Ridley wrote: ''Alix was only 26, but repeated pregnancies and premature births had worn her out. She was very ill, spitting blood.'' In a biography of Alice Keppel, one of Edward's several known mistresses, the writer Diana Souhami quoted the account of the author and critic Rebecca West bluntly describing Alix's experience: ''A terrible thing happened to that woman. She was raped of her youth.''\par \par \uc2 I'm thinking about Alix right now because the Supreme Court seems poised to strike down Roe v. Wade, and in its leaked draft opinion discussing the history of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the United States and referencing English common law, Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  posits that when the 14th Amendment was ratified in 1868 (the year Alix had her fourth child), ''three-quarters of the states made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a crime at all stages of pregnancy. The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  right is also critically different from any other right that this court has held to fall within the 14th Amendment's protection of 'liberty.''' He goes on to say:\par \par \uc2 Historical inquiries of this nature are essential whenever we are asked to recognize a new component of the ''liberty'' protected by the due process clause because the term ''liberty'' alone provides little guidance. ''\uc2 Liberty\uc2 '' is a capacious term. As Lincoln once said: ''We all declare for liberty; but in using the same word we do not all mean the same thing.\par \par \uc2 No, we don't. And I'm not sure the court has reckoned adequately with what overturning Roe would mean for liberty.\par \par \uc2 Though I wouldn't suggest that the overall state of women's health care would be knocked back to the 1860s if \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is outlawed in more parts of the United States, I would say that we aren't talking enough about the long-term effects of pregnancy and birth on women's bodies -- and that the deeply unequal nature of prenatal, postpartum and overall medical care in our country means that for some women, the level of care they are getting is nowhere near 21st-century standards.\par \par \uc2 And I'd say that requiring women to go through pregnancy and birth regardless of its impact on their long-term mental and physical health does, indeed, circumscribe liberty. It's one thing if motherhood is something affirmatively chosen. But making women carry unwanted pregnancies to term tilts toward treating us as incubators, rather than citizens with liberty interests. Further, as many of the state prohibitions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that will go into effect if Roe falls do not have exceptions for rape or incest, in some instances we are talking about women, some of whom are still legally children, who haven't consented to any part of this process.\par \par \uc2 The anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legal scholar Erika Bachiochi, who was recently interviewed by my colleague Ezra Klein, said that while ''many'' women have ''difficult or dangerous'' pregnancies, ''Most don't. Most pregnancies aren't dangerous and are natural experiences of bringing human life into the world.''\par \par \uc2 But let's unpack ''most.'' Last month, in New York magazine, Irin Carmon cataloged the many ways in which pregnancy and birth might affect the body that are at best painful and at worst life-threatening: sciatica, pica, preeclampsia (which can also happen postpartum), perineal trauma and gestational diabetes among them. When I talked to Courtney Schreiber, chief of the division of family planning in the department of obstetrics and gynecology at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania, she put it succinctly: ''The most important thing to recognize is really we consider pregnancy to be the ultimate stress test.''\par \par \uc2 ''Pregnancy changes the pregnant person's physiology completely. The lungs, all the organ systems, it's a true physiological load to be pregnant, even for the healthiest person,'' Dr. Schreiber said. She added that for those who have medical problems at baseline, such as diabetes or hypertension before pregnancy, those issues can have worsening trajectories after pregnancy and birth. The more pregnancies and births a woman has, the more dangerous the trajectory may be.\par \par \uc2 Having more than one pregnancy and birth within 18 months -- as Alix did -- is defined as a ''short interpregnancy interval'' and is associated with many adverse health outcomes for both mother and baby, including ''risk of preterm birth, low birth weight and preeclampsia,'' according to a 2013 article in the journal Obstetrics & Gynecology by Alison Gemmill and Laura Duberstein Lindberg. If, in the near future, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  becomes unavailable for millions of American women, we should worry about an increase in the number of short interpregnancy interval births.\par \par \uc2 Susan Groth, an associate professor at the University of Rochester School of Nursing who is working on a longitudinal study that collects data from women six, 12 and 36 months postpartum, told me that we don't even know the extent of how pregnancy and birth affect American women's bodies in the long term because the topic has been understudied and so many women of childbearing age don't have regular checkups.\par \par \uc2 This, even though we already know that premature birth and preeclampsia increase long-term risk of cardiovascular disease, which is the No. 1 killer of women. ''Regular medical care is not a priority because they're 'healthy,''' Groth told me. And mothers, many of whom have no paid leave and may have other children to care for, tend to put themselves last.\par \par \uc2 After the ultimate stress test, then, American women often don't become aware of, or get treatment for, the chronic health conditions that may become worse after birth. According to a 2018 committee opinion of the \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2 , ''As many as 40 percent of women do not attend a postpartum visit,'' which ''impedes management'' of such conditions.\par \par \uc2 Additionally, according to the Maternal Health Task Force at the \uc2 Harvard T.H. Chan School of Public Health\uc2 , ''Nearly 25 percent of all U.S. women start care late in pregnancy or do not receive the recommended number of prenatal visits; this number rises to 34 percent among African Americans and to 41 percent among American Indian or Alaska Native women.'' Black women also enter pregnancy with high rates of chronic hypertension, and they are three times as likely to die from pregnancy-related causes as white women.\par \par \uc2 Don't even get me started on pelvic floor disorders -- such as incontinence and organ prolapse -- which are exacerbated by the number of births a woman experiences; 32.4 percent of women with three or more children have pelvic floor disorders. Almost every mom I know jokes about peeing herself every time she jumps, but ''these are things that we just don't talk about'' publicly, said Angela Garbes, the author of ''Essential Labor'' and ''Like a Mother.'' ''So many people live with these private indignities,'' she said. ''It makes you feel really distanced from yourself.''\par \par \uc2 In his draft opinion, Alito repeats the argument that ''the costs of medical care associated with pregnancy are covered by insurance or government assistance.'' But considering what we know to be the lifelong physical effects of pregnancy and birth, and knowing that some of the states likely to outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are also states that declined to expand Medicaid, it could mean that without the protection of Roe, many women, particularly women without access to quality health coverage, will be at risk for worse health outcomes.\par \par \uc2 Thinking again about Alix, there seems to be a collective fantasy among some \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents that if there were not access to legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in today's world, there would be less extramarital sex. The thinking goes something like: Most abortions are a result of unwanted pregnancies, and if you must carry an unwanted pregnancy to term no matter the circumstances, it will be a deterrent for risky sexual encounters.\par \par \uc2 But Edward VII, a ''defender of the faith,'' had affairs throughout his life. Ridley described him as ''sexually rampant.'' She mentioned speculation that Edward could have contracted syphilis and passed it to Alix, possibly causing some of her health problems, though Ridley didn't reach that conclusion.\par \par \uc2 Per Ridley, Alix ''ached for more children'' and ''it was a permanent sadness'' that she couldn't have more. But she might have benefited from a bit more control over the timing of her pregnancies -- which was something she probably couldn't completely fathom in the 1860s, a decade before there was scientific consensus about ''the roles of eggs and sperm in fertilization,'' and a century before the pill. Now, as then, women deal with the bodily repercussions of pregnancy and birth. ''The moment of redemption is when it is a deeply wanted child,'' said Garbes. ''To be left possibly physically altered forever,'' she said, ''and emotionally scarred, in a situation where you didn't have a choice, that's the system crushing people.''\par \par \uc2 Want More?\par \par \uc2 What happens in a country where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is effectively banned? According to reporting from Katrin Bennhold and Monika Pronczuk in The Times: ''Poland offers a glimpse of a country where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is already practically out of reach even in the gravest circumstances. It has long been a showcase of the volatility and vicissitudes of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  battles -- and how the lives of women and their doctors are tossed about on shifting social and political tides.''\par \par \uc2 David Leonhardt explains that support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the United States is rising.\par \par \uc2 I was moved by this quote from Dana Stevens's review of ''The Janes,'' a new documentary about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists in the pre-Roe days. One of the Janes who is now a reverend had this response to religious anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  arguments: ''It's not a theological argument. That's a put-up job. To exclude women from ethical agency excludes us from humanity.''\par \par \uc2 In The New Yorker, Stephania Taladrid follows a 14-year-old who had to travel from Texas to New Mexico to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after Texas' restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law passed.\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY Eleanor Davis FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220618ei6i0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
