\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Expecting Decision on Roe, the White House Weighs Executive Actions\b0\par\par\uc2 By Charlie Savage \par \uc2 1,777 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Biden administration has been deluged with ideas to help mitigate a Supreme Court ruling expected to overturn \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, but many come with legal risks.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- President Biden's top aides are weighing whether he can or should take a series of executive actions to help women in Republican-controlled states obtain abortions if the Supreme Court eliminates a woman's right to end her pregnancy, according to senior administration officials.\par \par \uc2 Some of the ideas under consideration include declaring a national public health emergency, readying the Justice Department to fight any attempt by states to criminalize travel for the purpose of obtaining an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and asserting that \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  regulations granting approval to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medications pre-empt any state bans, the officials said.\par \par \uc2 Since a draft opinion was leaked last month indicating that the Supreme Court was prepared to overturn the landmark Roe v. Wade decision -- an action that would prompt at least 20 states to prohibit or severely curtail access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates have been lobbying the White House to take extraordinary steps to mitigate the effect.\par \par \uc2 ''We are at a crisis moment for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in this country, and officials at all levels of government must respond -- including the executive branch,'' said Marya Torrez, senior director of policy development and strategy at \uc2 Planned Parenthood Federation of America\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 No executive order can re-establish a constitutional right. It would take an act of Congress to restore a national legal standard barring states from outlawing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and proponents currently lack sufficient votes in the Senate, where Republicans can filibuster such a bill. But Mr. Biden has signaled that he wants to move on his own.\par \par \uc2 ''I don't think the country will stand for it,'' Mr. Biden told the talk show host Jimmy Kimmel last week in discussing the likely end of Roe v. Wade, adding: ''There's some executive orders I could employ, we believe. We're looking at that right now.''\par \par \uc2 The White House counsel, \uc2 Dana Remus\uc2 , the director of its gender policy council, Jennifer Klein, and the director of its domestic policy council, Susan Rice, are overseeing the legal and policy vetting of potential executive actions. Anita Dunn, a senior policy adviser to Mr. Biden, is in charge of broader planning, including communications strategy, officials said.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court is expected to issue a decision at the end of its term in about two weeks, and White House aides believe the ruling could touch off a political crisis, including mass protests. Further complicating matters, the decision may come down while Mr. Biden is in Europe for the Group of 7 summit.\par \par \uc2 The contingency planning is also said to include what to do if such a fiercely polarizing development leads to acts of violence. The administration has already heightened security for the Supreme Court justices after one man, apparently angered by anticipated conservative rulings on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and guns, traveled to suburban Washington from California intending to kill Justice \uc2 Brett M. Kavanaugh\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The ruling could reshape the political environment at a time when Democrats are considered likely to lose control of Congress in the November midterm elections. Against that backdrop, Mr. Biden's advisers have been grappling with both legal and political complexities as they develop a list of possible responses.\par \par \uc2 Part of the dilemma, according to people familiar with the internal deliberations, is that Mr. Biden's approach is likely to be seen as a litmus test by many centrist or liberal-leaning voters. This will put him under pressure to aggressively demonstrate a deep concern over the loss of the nearly 50-year-old right to reproductive freedom, and could make it preferable for him to go down fighting rather than demoralize certain voters.\par \par \uc2 In the past, Mr. Biden has adopted a position that his legal team warned him was unlikely to stand up in court, betting that the political benefits of his executive actions outweighed the legal risks. In August, as House Democrats urged him to reverse course on letting a pandemic-related ban on evicting renters expire, Mr. Biden unilaterally extended the measure.\par \par \uc2 The move won praise from the left, at a moment when he needed to hold his coalition together in order to advance his legislative agenda. But while Mr. Biden's decision bought a little more time for pandemic assistance funds to reach renters, its practical impact was limited because courts, as predicted, swiftly struck it down -- and his critics accused him of lawlessness.\par \par \uc2 In the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate, some of Mr. Biden's advisers both inside and outside the administration are wary of providing Republicans with similar fodder, allowing them to shift the political narrative from what their party has done to raising the alarm about the overreach of executive power.\par \par \uc2 Laurence Tribe, a Harvard Law School professor who has consulted with Ms. Remus's team, said in an interview that while he did not want ''to pour cold water on people's peaceful reactions to impending disaster,'' some of the proposals the White House was being lobbied to consider were unwise and implausible extensions of executive power.\par \par \uc2 ''It would take attention from the things that are really relevant -- that the Supreme Court is out of control and we ought to be very critical of it -- and shift the criticism to the president for responding in kind and doing things that are every bit as ungrounded in the Constitution as the court's overruling of Roe will be,'' Mr. Tribe warned.\par \par \uc2 Not every idea has elicited the same degree of caution. For example, the administration appears likely to ask the \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2  to push makers of apps that track menstrual cycles to warn users that the data could be used to identify women in the early stages of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 But administration officials see other suggestions as extremely risky. One calls for Mr. Biden to invite \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors to work at federal enclaves, like military bases, inside states that criminalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . State prosecutors lack jurisdiction in such zones, so the federal government handles crimes there, and it is not always clear whether criminal laws at the state level apply.\par \par \uc2 Doctors might still face challenges to their state medical licenses. And while the Justice Department under Mr. Biden could decline to pursue charges as a policy matter, control of the department could flip in the 2024 presidential election, and federal prosecutors could then charge people with crimes, like \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , that happened in the Biden era.\par \par \uc2 Several other proposals for executive actions raise questions about the scope of the Hyde Amendment, a law that generally prohibits paying for abortions with federal taxpayer funds. The Biden administration is said to have asked the \uc2 Justice Department's Office of Legal Counsel\uc2  whether the law also bars using those funds for expenses related to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , like travel.\par \par \uc2 Administration officials have signaled their confidence that the department would approve granting federal employees paid leaves of absence to travel to another state to terminate unwanted pregnancies. The same goes for using federal funds to help pay travel and lodging costs for poor women seeking abortions in states where the procedure remains legal.\par \par \uc2 Skeptics of the plan to pay for travel costs have argued that nonprofits are raising private money for that purpose; that it would prompt a vote in Congress to ban such spending, which would imperil Democrats in conservative-leaning districts; and that Republican states would sue before like-minded judges willing to interpret the Hyde Act more expansively.\par \par \uc2 ''Are we dealing with the law as we think it is, or the law as we think it is going to be once the right -- or wrong -- judges get their hands on it?'' said Stephen Vladeck, a \uc2 University of Texas, Austin\uc2 , law professor consulted by the White House. ''It's one thing to roll out a bunch of stuff that gets blocked by Republican judges if the goal is the symbolism of having tried. But if the goal is effective measures, that's not helpful.''\par \par \uc2 The administration is also studying ideas to help pave the way for women in states banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to obtain pills that can terminate a pregnancy during the first 10 weeks from out-of-state pharmacies. In December, the F.D.A. approved a regulation allowing such drugs to be prescribed in telemedicine visits and distributed by mail.\par \par \uc2 One complication is that doctors are licensed at the state level, and practicing medicine without a license in another state is a crime, although it can be difficult to decide where a doctor consulting virtually with an out-of-state patient is ''practicing.''\par \par \uc2 To provide doctors with legal cover, some supporters are urging the Biden administration to take several steps that would reimpose a degree of federal control over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law.\par \par \uc2 One idea is for the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  to declare a public health emergency based on expected patient surges at clinics in border states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains legal, and to use that emergency to invoke a 2005 law that shields doctors from legal liability for treating patients in a state where they are not licensed.\par \par \uc2 These advocates also want the F.D.A. to declare that its regulation approving the use of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills -- or a strengthened version of the rule -- pre-empts state laws banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Both moves would rely on aggressive interpretations of the power Congress granted those agencies, and are likely to draw immediate court challenges, raising the possibility of rulings that limit the government's flexibility under public health and drug safety laws.\par \par \uc2 Richard Fallon, another Harvard Law School professor the White House has consulted, noted that the Supreme Court's conservative majority has been deeply skeptical of the power of agencies to regulate major issues without explicit authorization from Congress. He cautioned against ''false hopes,'' saying that legally, ''the administration is in a very, very hard position.''\par \par \uc2 Melissa Murray, a \uc2 New York University\uc2  law professor who specializes in reproductive issues and has consulted with the administration, said that it may want to take some ''calculated risks'' on executive actions, but argued that its foremost goal should be prompting supporters to vote.\par \par \uc2 ''Everyone keeps asking me what we should do when the decision comes down,'' she said. ''You can cry or you can vote.''\par \par \uc2 A demonstration for \uc2  rights in May, after a leaked draft showed that the court was poised to overturn the right to an \uc2 .; Aides for President Biden are looking at using executive orders to help women in Republican-controlled states obtain abortions. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY KENNY HOLSTON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220617ei6h0003s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
