\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Even Before Court Rules on Roe, Some Clinics Have Ban in Place\b0\par\par\uc2 By Claire Cain Miller and Margot Sanger-Katz \par \uc2 1,328 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Women can no longer get a legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in two states.\par \par \uc2 Although Roe v. Wade remains the law of the land, women can no longer get a legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in two states, Oklahoma and South Dakota. In at least one other, Missouri, the only clinic is booked and not accepting new appointments. And in a fourth state, Wisconsin, clinics will not schedule abortions for after the end of the Supreme Court's term in late June.\par \par \uc2 Before May 2, when a draft Supreme Court opinion that would overturn Roe was leaked, there had been at least one \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic in every state. But in some states, health care providers aren't waiting for the actual decision to be issued to start operating as if Roe were overturned.\par \par \uc2 ''It's already happening,'' said Caitlin Myers, a professor of economics at \uc2 Middlebury College\uc2  who studies \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  accessibility. She is leading a national survey of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics and supplied data on her recent findings, which was verified by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The changes in the last few weeks suggest how quickly an overturning of Roe could reduce \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access across the South and Midwest, which would be a hard-fought victory for the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement. A survey published Wednesday by the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2  found that abortions had increased around the country between 2017 and 2020, reversing decades of declines. Around one in five pregnancies ended in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  during that period.\par \par \uc2 Roe guarantees a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until the point of fetal viability, around 23 weeks, and without it, around half of states are expected to ban the procedure. Many of those states already had limited access -- six had a single clinic, and three had two -- and various restrictions that made abortions harder to get. Now it is changing from hard to impossible, at least without crossing state lines.\par \par \uc2 In Oklahoma, clinics have stopped operating because the state passed a new ban, even though it clearly conflicts with Roe. ''We haven't had \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for two and a half weeks,'' said Susan Braselton, a clinic escort and a board member of the Roe Fund in Oklahoma, which helps patients finance abortions. Ms. Braselton said she had expected intervention from the courts to stop the law from being enforced: ''I would never have thought this would happen.''\par \par \uc2 Jim Olsen, a Republican state representative in Oklahoma who has been a leader on adding several \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions in the state, said, ''This is something that people have worked on and prayed for for 50 years, so it's a tremendous victory.''\par \par \uc2 The only clinic in South Dakota, Planned Parenthood in Sioux Falls, which also served patients from North Dakota and Minnesota, performed its last \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday, even though the state has not yet banned it. Abortions at the clinic were already sporadic. A Minnesota doctor flew in around once a month to provide them.\par \par \uc2 And in some other states, legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has become much more scarce. In Idaho there were four clinics, but Planned Parenthood in Boise closed June 1. At another, Compassionate \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Care in Boise, the only doctor is set to take a summer vacation, and staff members are telling patients that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not be legal by the time he returns. A third is offering only medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 At Planned Parenthood clinics across the country, phone recordings tell callers that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains legal and accessible, but some have stopped scheduling abortions.\par \par \uc2 A banner at the top of the website for Planned Parenthood of Wisconsin says: ''Our doors are open. Planned Parenthood of Wisconsin will continue to help patients get the care they need -- including safe and legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . No matter what.'' But the organization there decided to stop offering any new appointments for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after June 25, anticipating that the court might release its opinion two days later, on its last scheduled decision day. Wisconsin still has an 1849 law on the books criminalizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Dr. Allie Linton, the organization's associate medical director, said the decision was in part because of worries that it would be hard to contact patients if their appointments needed to be canceled. She also said the group's leaders were ''cognizant of the significant trauma that might come for patients and staff if we are in the middle of a procedure or the middle of a procedure day, and have to tell patients we cannot provide care.''\par \par \uc2 The Wisconsin clinics have made contingency plans on days this month when the Supreme Court has been expected to issue opinions. Many members of the staff plan to spend the week of June 27 training at new clinics in Illinois, anticipating they may need to relocate so that Wisconsin patients can travel there to obtain care.\par \par \uc2 At Planned Parenthood Great Northwest, which operates clinics in Hawaii, Alaska, Washington, Idaho, Indiana and Kentucky, five clinics closed last month as the organization started shuffling resources. Idaho, Indiana and Kentucky are expected to ban most abortions if Roe is overturned, so the organization is trying to expand telemedicine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the remaining states and to help patients get to out-of-state providers.\par \par \uc2 As clinics schedule new patients, they are warning them that the legal status of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be in flux. ''Our patient navigators, when they are scheduling, they give patients a heads up and say, 'Hey, just so you know, there may be a legal change,''' said Katie Rodihan, a spokeswoman for the clinics.\par \par \uc2 Danika Severino Wynn, the vice president for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access at the \uc2 Planned Parenthood Federation of America\uc2 , said in a statement that local affiliates were independently deciding what to do as the court's decision nears.\par \par \uc2 ''Planned Parenthood affiliates in these states that are extremely hostile to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access are being forced to make the difficult decision whether or not to suspend providing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services following the court's decision, due to their state's legal landscape,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Even in states that intend to ban abortions, many would allow the procedure to save the life of the mother or for pregnancies caused by rape or incest. But with clinics closing in states with bans, women eligible for such care may still need to travel to receive it.\par \par \uc2 Other clinics in states that are expected to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are fully open, like Red River Women's Clinic in Fargo, N.D. The state has a trigger ban that would make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  illegal 30 days after a court decision. ''We will continue to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in our state as long as it is legal,'' said Tammi Kromenaker, the clinic director. If Roe is overturned, she plans to open a new clinic across the river, in Moorhead, Minn., to continue to serve women in the region.\par \par \uc2 Already, clinic closures are leading to long wait lists at open clinics, which has caused some patients to travel far to receive care. Last week, the Red River clinic admitted three patients who had driven four hours from the Twin Cities, because they could be seen sooner that way.\par \par \uc2 The legal limbo has also been confusing for patients, said Dr. Erin King, executive director of the Hope Clinic for Women in Granite City, Ill.\par \par \uc2 ''Every time a restriction is enacted in a state, there's mass confusion about how to comply and what it means,'' she said. ''After the leak, we had patients calling to ask if \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was still legal.\par \par \uc2 ''Trying to get information out -- as providers and organizations who work directly with patients, that's a huge part of the role.''\par \par \uc2 She said that -- for now -- the message they give patients is: ''\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal in this country. It may be very difficult to access in some states, but it is legal.''\par \par \uc2 A patient at an Oklahoma City clinic last year. Legal \uc2  is no longer available in two states. (PHOTOGRAPH BY EVELYN HOCKSTEIN/REUTERS) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220616ei6g0004v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
