\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Common Questions About  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Access As Court Ruling Nears\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sean Plambeck, Claire Cain Miller, Margot Sanger-Katz, Fahima Haque, Dani Blum, Nicole Stock, Jesus Jim\u233\'00\'E9nez and Christina Caron \par \uc2 1,489 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The United States is on the verge of a monumental recasting of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, with many states poised to restrict or prohibit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should the Supreme Court overturn the precedent set in the landmark Roe v. Wade case. In May, a leaked draft of a Supreme Court opinion suggested that the justices will do away with Roe's standard, which does not allow states to ban the procedure before the point at which a fetus can survive outside the womb, which experts say is about 22 to 23 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 If the court were to overturn Roe, the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be abolished and it would be up to each state to set its own \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws. More than a dozen Republican-led states have legislation in place that would outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in most cases immediately, while some states where Democrats are in power have moved to retain and strengthen existing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights or expand on them.\par \par \uc2 Here are answers to some of the fundamental questions surrounding the impending changes, including where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will become illegal and where it will remain accessible by law.\par \par \uc2 Is \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  illegal now? Or might it be soon?\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains legal in some form in every state, though a law that came into effect in Oklahoma in May outlaws \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in all but a few limited circumstances.\par \par \uc2 The new Oklahoma law, the most restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in the country, bans nearly all abortions starting at fertilization and makes exceptions only in cases where an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is necessary to save the life of the mother, or in cases of rape or incest that have been reported to law enforcement. The law relies on everyday residents -- neighbors, family, friends -- to enforce it by filing lawsuits in civil court against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers or anyone who helps a woman get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . State officials cannot bring criminal charges.\par \par \uc2 Roe v. Wade established the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before the fetus is viable outside the womb, or about 22 to 23 weeks of pregnancy. Some states have since restricted the procedure to a few weeks earlier in pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Texas and Oklahoma are the only states whose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans take effect within the first trimester of a pregnancy, and both states have used a novel technique of civilian enforcement to circumvent legal challenges.\par \par \uc2 If Roe were overturned, other state laws would take effect. Some states would outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  immediately while others have laws in place that would keep access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legal.\par \par \uc2 SEAN PLAMBECK\par \par \uc2 Where would \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  become illegal if Roe is overturned?\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would probably become illegal in about half of the states, although forecasts differ.\par \par \uc2 According to the Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a group that fights \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions in court and closely tracks state laws, 25 states are likely to ban most abortions if they are allowed to. Those states are: Alabama, Arizona, Arkansas, Georgia, Idaho, Indiana, Kentucky, Louisiana, Michigan, Mississippi, Missouri, Nebraska, North Carolina, North Dakota, Ohio, Oklahoma, Pennsylvania, South Carolina, South Dakota, Tennessee, Texas, Utah, West Virginia, Wisconsin and Wyoming.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a research group focused on \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, says a slightly different group of states is likely to substantially limit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access: Its list of 26 states excludes North Carolina and Pennsylvania, but includes Florida, Iowa and Montana.\par \par \uc2 CLAIRE CAIN MILLER and MARGOT SANGER-KATZ\par \par \uc2 What states would retain or expand \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access?\par \par \uc2 California, Colorado, Connecticut, Delaware, Hawaii, Illinois, Maine, Maryland, Massachusetts, Nevada, New Jersey, New York, Oregon, Rhode Island, Vermont and Washington, as well as the District of Columbia, have laws that preserve access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  research group that supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Some states have also been positioning themselves as ''refuges'' for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, by offering support and legal aid to women who travel to their state for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  has said California would offer tax breaks to companies seeking to move from states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  may become illegal. In Oregon, the Legislature approved $15 million in March to help pay for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  expenses for patients coming from outside the state.\par \par \uc2 In New York, Democratic lawmakers have been working on a package of bills that would strengthen the state's already robust \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access laws. Some of those efforts have been focused on shielding providers from liability for patients coming from states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been criminalized. Others seek to help patients who travel to New York for \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care.\par \par \uc2 FAHIMA HAQUE\par \par \uc2 Would birth control still be legal if Roe is overturned?\par \par \uc2 Yes, birth control remains legal everywhere in the United States, though several states allow doctors and pharmacists to refuse to prescribe or dispense contraceptives, according to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 . The leaked draft of a Supreme Court opinion that would overturn Roe v. Wade does not indicate that the court would revisit past decisions about birth control.\par \par \uc2 However, some legal experts have raised concerns that justices could apply the argument for overturning Roe to limiting access to contraceptives. As a result, those who support birth control access worry that legislators could use a ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to make birth control less available.\par \par \uc2 ''We've seen folks falsely equating emergency contraceptives and IUDs with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' said Mara Gandal-Powers, director of birth control access and senior counsel at the \uc2 National Women's Law Center\uc2 . ''That's certainly something I'm concerned about.''\par \par \uc2 One concern that has been raised is whether laws, like one in Oklahoma, that ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the moment of fertilization would also outlaw intrauterine devices, or IUDs, which are designed to prevent fertilization but also can stop a fertilized egg from implanting in the uterus. (The Oklahoma law specifies that it does not apply to contraception, including Plan B or morning-after pills.)\par \par \uc2 The two forms of emergency contraception pills, Plan B and Ella, work in a different way to prevent fertilization from occurring. Both delay or prevent ovulation and allow sperm in the reproductive tract to die out.\par \par \uc2 DANI BLUM and NICOLE STOCK\par \par \uc2 What are trigger laws?\par \par \uc2 If the Supreme Court overturns Roe v. Wade, it would not outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Instead, states would be able to individually determine the procedure's legality.\par \par \uc2 Thirteen states across the country have signaled their readiness to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  by passing so-called trigger laws, which would ban most abortions almost immediately if they are allowed to by the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 ''Some states that are very strongly anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , having been frustrated that they couldn't ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  because of Roe v. Wade, decided to pass laws that would be on the books and operative immediately in the future event that the court ever removed the protections of Roe,'' said Donna Crane, an adjunct professor at \uc2 San Jose State University\uc2  with an expertise in women's rights and \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The 13 states that have trigger laws all have Republican-controlled legislatures: Arkansas, Idaho, Kentucky, Louisiana, Mississippi, Missouri, North Dakota, Oklahoma, South Dakota, Tennessee, Texas, Utah and Wyoming.\par \par \uc2 JESUS JIM\u201\'00\'C9NEZ\par \par \uc2 How are gestational weeks calculated?\par \par \uc2 A current Texas law bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after six weeks of pregnancy, and other states like Georgia, Ohio and South Carolina have passed legislation with the intent of prohibiting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after six weeks if Roe v. Wade were to be overturned. These bills use the six-week mark as the point when an ultrasound may be able to detect the pulsing of what will become the fetus's heart.\par \par \uc2 However, at six weeks many women have no idea they are pregnant.\par \par \uc2 Doctors measure the beginning of a pregnancy as being the first day of a woman's last period. Why? They are tracking the length of pregnancy using a nearly 200-year-old calculation called Naegele's Rule, named after Franz Karl Naegele, the German obstetrician who is credited with creating it in the 1800s.\par \par \uc2 The rule is somewhat confusing because conception usually does not occur until around 14 days after the first day of a woman's period, assuming she has a 28-day cycle (which many women do not for a variety of reasons). The reason doctors still use the last menstrual cycle as a benchmark is because it is difficult to know exactly when the sperm fertilized the egg.\par \par \uc2 So when doctors say a woman is six weeks pregnant, it typically means the embryo started developing about four weeks earlier.\par \par \uc2 The heart, which can be seen flickering on an ultrasound, is still maturing and cannot be heard until several weeks later.\par \par \uc2 Perhaps this is the simplest way to say it: Six weeks pregnant is two weeks after a woman misses her period.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA CARON\par \par \uc2 In May, a leaked draft of a Supreme Court opinion suggested that the justices would overturn Roe v. Wade. (PHOTOGRAPH BY EVELYN HOCKSTEIN/REUTERS) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220616ei6g0004k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\