\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 U.S. Abortions Rise After 30-Year Drop, Report Says; All Regions See Increases\b0\par\par\uc2 By Pam Belluck \par \uc2 1,151 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A new survey of the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers found increases in every region of the country from 2017 to 2020.\par \par \uc2 The number of abortions in the United States has increased, reversing what had been a three-decade decline, according to a new report.\par \par \uc2 The uptick began in 2017 and, as of 2020, one in five pregnancies, or 20.6 percent, ended in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to the report by the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a research organization that supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. In 2017, 18.4 percent of pregnancies ended in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The institute, which collects data by contacting every known \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider in the country, reported that the number of abortions increased to 930,160 in 2020, from 862,320 in 2017. The number increased in every region of the country: by 12 percent in the West, by 10 percent in the Midwest, by 8 percent in the South and by 2 percent in the Northeast.\par \par \uc2 The new data was released as the Supreme Court prepares to issue a ruling that could effectively overturn the Roe v. Wade standard that has made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legal in the United States for nearly 50 years. If the court's final ruling is similar to a draft opinion that leaked last month, about half of the states are expected to quickly ban or sharply restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while other states are gearing up to expand access for patients from states that make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  unavailable.\par \par \uc2 Overall, the new report found, the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate rose in 2020 to 14.4 per 1,000 women ages 15 to 44 from 13.5 abortions per 1,000 women of that age group in 2017, a 7 percent increase.\par \par \uc2 During this period, the report said, births nationwide declined by 6 percent, meaning that ''fewer people were getting pregnant and, among those who did, a larger proportion chose to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 The report, which found that the number and rate of abortions increased in 33 states and the District of Columbia, said ''there were no clear patterns'' to explain each state's trajectory. It suggested several reasons for the national increase, including trends that directly affected low-income people, the population most likely to seek abortions in recent years: Some states expanded Medicaid coverage for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and funds that provide financial assistance to patients seeking abortions expanded.\par \par \uc2 Another factor may have been the Trump administration policy of preventing programs that received federal family planning money, known as Title X funds, from mentioning the option of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to patients. That rule caused Planned Parenthood and several state governments to decline to accept Title X funding, which may have reduced access to other family planning services, including contraception, for low-income people, and led to more unintended pregnancies, the Guttmacher report said. The Biden administration has since rescinded the Trump-era policy.\par \par \uc2 The rise in abortions came at a time when many conservative states were placing new restrictions on the procedure. But the report said that while 25 states enacted 168 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions from 2017 to 2020, some were stopped by legal challenges and many were enacted by states that already had significant restrictions, so the new laws might not have prevented many more abortions.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, other states enacted 75 provisions to protect or expand \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, including by requiring insurance to cover \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and by allowing nurse practitioners, physician assistants and certified nurse midwives to provide some \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services, the report said.\par \par \uc2 ''It shows that people in every state seek abortions despite restrictive measures in many states,'' said Leslie Reagan, a historian of American medicine and public health at the \uc2 University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The data included most of the first year of the coronavirus pandemic. The report said that while \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access was disrupted in some states during that time, both because of attempted bans and because of outbreaks and limits on in-person medical care, some states maintained access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In addition, for part of 2020, a judge's ruling allowed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, which account for more than half of the country's abortions, to be mailed to patients -- a practice that the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  made permanent in December 2021.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Reagan, who wrote a book titled ''When \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Was a Crime,'' said she believed ''the severe unemployment and food insecurity that followed the shutdown'' during the pandemic had also fueled the increase in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ''Many people couldn't feed themselves and their existing families and knew they could not support a child.''\par \par \uc2 Marjorie Dannenfelser, president of Susan B. Anthony Pro-Life America, issued a statement excoriating the increase in abortions. ''The pro-life movement must continue educating fellow Americans, and pro-life lawmakers at both the state and federal level must be as ambitious as possible in building consensus,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 A news release from her group said that the new report found ''substantial increases in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in states like California, Illinois and New York, which have expanded their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies in recent years and where there are virtually no limits on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up to birth.''\par \par \uc2 In fact, the percentage increase in the number of abortions in those states was less than in some conservative states, like Mississippi, where the number of abortions increased 40 percent from 2017 to 2020, and Oklahoma, where the number of abortions rose 103 percent during that time.\par \par \uc2 The growth in Mississippi is striking because it is a 2018 Mississippi law banning most abortions later than 15 weeks into a pregnancy that is currently before the Supreme Court. The Guttmacher report said that Mississippi residents used to seek abortions in surrounding states, but because those states enacted restrictions that led clinics to close, more patients went to Mississippi's one \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic.\par \par \uc2 Oklahoma just passed the nation's toughest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, but from 2017 to 2020 the increase in abortions there was likely because many patients had traveled there from Texas, which has many restrictive laws. Still, the new report showed that the number of abortions also increased in Texas during those years -- by 5 percent, the same percentage increase as in New York.\par \par \uc2 In the 17 states where the number and rate decreased, by far the biggest decline was in Missouri, which had 4,710 abortions in 2017 and 170 in 2020. The report said this drop was because patients from Missouri had sought abortions in Illinois, where the number of abortions increased 25 percent during that time.\par \par \uc2 A handful of states in each region of the country experienced decreases, including five of the nine states in the Northeast, a reflection of the complex influences that determine who seeks abortions and where they obtain them.\par \par \uc2 An \uc2  patient left a clinic with \uc2  medication in Oklahoma City. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Evelyn Hockstein/Reuters FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220616ei6g0003f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
