\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 What Does the Rising Support for  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Access Tell Us?\b0\par\par\uc2 By David Leonhardt \par \uc2 997 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 19\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The thermostat theory of politics, developed by the political scientist \uc2 Christopher Wlezien\uc2 , holds that public opinion often moves in the opposite direction as government policy.\par \par \uc2 When policy begins changing, many people worry that the shift will be too radical, and their views move the other way -- much as a thermostat regulates a house's temperature. During \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's presidency, public attitudes moved left on immigration. During \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 's presidency, attitudes moved right on gun control and taxes.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy now seems to be offering the latest example of the theory. As more states have enacted laws restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the last few years, support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access has risen. It may have risen even more in the last few weeks, with the Supreme Court potentially on the verge of overturning Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Shortly after Politico reported in May that the court had tentatively decided to overturn Roe, a \uc2 University of Chicago\uc2  research group conducted a poll for The Wall Street Journal, asking about Americans' attitudes toward \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The poll is especially useful because it has been asking the same questions since the 1970s. Last month, it found that 57 percent of Americans said they favored legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if a woman wanted one for any reason, up from 54 percent last year and only 44 percent in 2016.\par \par \uc2 Polls by the \uc2 Pew Research Center\uc2  and NBC News have also found rising support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the last few years. These increases fit a historical pattern, noted Andrew Beveridge, a co-founder of Social Explorer, a demographic research tool. Support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  also rose in the late 1980s and early 1990s, after two Supreme Court decisions that allowed states to impose more restrictions.\par \par \uc2 ''When the ability to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was under threat, support for it went up,'' Beveridge said. ''When it was not under threat, support declined.''\par \par \uc2 The Democratic Hope\par \par \uc2 With the country possibly on the verge of its biggest change in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy since the 1970s, many Democrats see a major political opportunity. They think the court's decision -- if, in fact, it overturns Roe, which remains uncertain -- can help the party do better in this year's midterms than many analysts have been predicting. In the long term, Democrats hope that they can channel public opinion to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access into law.\par \par \uc2 ''You have a right that is wildly popular with the electorate, and you have an electorate that has not really grappled with the fact that it could be taken away,'' Jessica Floyd, president of American Bridge, a Democratic political action committee, told Reuters. ''The dynamic here is fairly unprecedented.''\par \par \uc2 In an Opinion essay in, \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , Josh Marshall argued that Democratic candidates would help themselves by signing a pledge promising both to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and to break a filibuster to do so.\par \par \uc2 Three Caveats\par \par \uc2 Democrats may be right about the power of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year. As Floyd noted, the political environment seems to be unprecedented, which introduces significant uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 But there are also at least three reasons to wonder whether \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will prove to be as potent as Democrats think.\par \par \uc2 First, the recent Wall Street Journal poll suggesting that most Americans oppose any \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions may be an outlier. For years, other polls have repeatedly shown that a majority of people -- women and men -- support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in many cases but also want significant restrictions (such as after the first trimester).\par \par \uc2 As a recent Pew report put it, ''Relatively few Americans on either side of the debate take an absolutist view on the legality of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- either supporting or opposing it at all times, regardless of circumstances.'' The Democratic Party's position is near one end of that spectrum, which can make it harder for the party to win over swing voters on the issue.\par \par \uc2 Second, the politics of gender identity are dividing Democrats, which may make it harder for them to agree on a clear message. Historically, Democrats have described \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access as a matter of women's equality. But some progressives now oppose using the word ''women'' when talking about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , because a small percentage of pregnant people are transgender men.\par \par \uc2 Michael Powell described this debate in The Times last week, quoting some experts who argued that language like ''pregnant people'' alienated many people. ''Activists are adopting symbols and language that are off-putting not just to the right but to people in the center and even liberals,'' Steven Greene of \uc2 North Carolina State University\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 Third, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not be the main issue on most voters' minds, even if the Supreme Court overrules Roe. ''It is a very huge issue for the base of the party,'' Chuck Rocha, a Democratic strategist and former union organizer, told Politico. ''But it doesn't have the same sway as we think it does.'' That may be especially true when inflation is high and President Biden's approval rating is low.\par \par \uc2 Mark Murray\uc2  of NBC News recently summarized both sides of the dynamic.\par \par \uc2 ''Support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights has reached a record high, and nearly two-thirds of Americans oppose the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  overturning Roe v. Wade, according to a new national NBC News poll conducted after the leak of a draft opinion that would strike down the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Murray wrote, adding that the poll also found that ''this Supreme Court draft opinion hasn't substantially altered the overall political environment heading into November's elections.''\par \par \uc2 For years, many Americans have been willing to vote for candidates who have different \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  views than the voters themselves do. Perhaps that dynamic will continue in 2022. Or perhaps the Supreme Court will focus people's attention on the issue in a way that has not previously been the case. ''We've never been in a situation like this,'' Christine Matthews, a pollster who has worked for Republicans, told The Times.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220614ei6e0004b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
