\par\par \uc2 Foreign Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Pregnant in Poland, Where  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Is Banned\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katrin Bennhold and Monika Pronczuk \par \uc2 2,879 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban has had many unintended consequences. One is that doctors are sometimes afraid to remove fetuses or administer cancer treatment to save women's lives.\par \par \uc2 PSZCZYNA, Poland -- It was shortly before 11 p.m. when Izabela Sajbor realized the doctors were prepared to let her die.\par \par \uc2 Her doctor had already told her that her fetus had severe abnormalities and would almost certainly die in the womb. If it made it to term, life expectancy was a year, at most. At 22 weeks pregnant, Ms. Sajbor had been admitted to a hospital after her water broke prematurely.\par \par \uc2 She knew that there was a short window to induce birth or surgically remove the fetus to avert infection and potentially fatal sepsis. But even as she developed a fever, vomited and convulsed on the floor, it seemed to be the baby's heartbeat that the doctors were most concerned about.\par \par \uc2 ''My life is in danger,'' she wrote in a string of distressed text messages to her mother and husband that was shared with \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  by her family's lawyer.\par \par \uc2 ''They cannot help as long as the fetus is alive thanks to the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law,'' she wrote only hours before she died. ''A woman is like an incubator.''\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has seized the United States' attention anew with the prospect that, as early as this month, the Supreme Court could overturn Roe v. Wade, the decision that has made the procedure terminating a pregnancy legal for nearly 50 years. If Roe is overturned, half of American women stand to lose legal access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Poland offers a glimpse of a country where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is already practically out of reach even in the gravest circumstances. It has long been a showcase of the volatility and vicissitudes of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  battles -- and how the lives of women and their doctors are tossed about on shifting social and political tides.\par \par \uc2 The long battle over Poland's 29-year-old ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has intensified over the past 17 months after the elimination of the last significant exception permitting the procedure: fetal abnormalities.\par \par \uc2 Since the exception's elimination, deaths like Ms. Sajbor's have been rare -- one of three cited by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates -- but they have become a touchstone of grievance for those who say they demonstrate the risks to women posed by restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws.\par \par \uc2 Only one in 10 Poles support the stricter ban, which was enabled by a decision by the country's highest court, dominated by judges loyal to a deeply conservative government. The rest of the population is roughly split between reverting to milder restrictions and legalizing terminations.\par \par \uc2 Today, Poland and Malta, both staunchly Catholic, are the only \uc2 European Union\uc2  countries where abortions are effectively outlawed.\par \par \uc2 The consequences in Poland have been far-reaching: \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights activists have been threatened with prison for handing out \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills. The number of Polish women traveling abroad to get abortions, already in the thousands, has swelled further. A black market of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills -- some fake and many overpriced -- is thriving.\par \par \uc2 Technically, the law still allows abortions if there is a serious risk to a woman's health and life. But critics say it fails to provide necessary clarity, paralyzing doctors.\par \par \uc2 ''This law creates problems for doctors and patients,'' Jan Kochanowicz, a doctor who is also the director of the University Clinical Hospital in Bialystok, the largest city in northeastern Poland, said in an interview. ''There is no clear and straightforward answer to what constitutes a threat to a woman's health and life. Doctors are afraid to make decisions.''\par \par \uc2 Defenders of the Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban say these are extreme cases, caused not by the law but by doctors' poor judgment.\par \par \uc2 ''The law should not have a chilling effect because legislation on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  when a woman's health or life is in danger has not changed,'' said Katarzyna Gesiak, the head of the center for medical law and bioethics at Ordo Iuris, a Catholic organization that lobbied for the new ban.\par \par \uc2 She agreed with opponents of the law that in its current form, the measure was ''too general'' and ''too open to interpretation,'' but her concern was that it still gave doctors too much latitude to perform abortions.\par \par \uc2 For \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights supporters, clarity is less the issue than what they say has been a steady erosion of women's autonomy since the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban nearly three decades ago.\par \par \uc2 Just this month, the government required Poland's central health care system to log pregnancies. Opponents called it a ''pregnancy register'' that could be used to track down illegal terminations.\par \par \uc2 ''Once you start chipping away at the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it's hard to go back,'' said Krystyna Kacpura, the president of the Federation for Women and Family Planning, or Federa, a Warsaw-based advocacy group. ''We are now at a point where the risks to women's physical and mental health have reached a new quality.''\par \par \uc2 Eliminating the Exceptions\par \par \uc2 Poland was once a destination for women seeking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Under Communism, the Catholic Church was marginalized and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legalized in 1956. Women were encouraged to work and granted sweeping \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  that Western democracies embraced only decades later.\par \par \uc2 With abortions in Poland cheap and available practically on demand, women from all over Western Europe flocked there until well into the 1980s.\par \par \uc2 But that changed after the Communist government collapsed in 1989. Bowing to pressure from a newly assertive Catholic Church, which had supported the fight against Communism, the new Parliament proposed an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 ''No one knew at the time that the period of democratization would mean such backlash for women's rights,'' said Magdalena Sroda, a professor of ethics at the \uc2 University of Warsaw\uc2 . ''It was a return to the discourse of traditional women's roles as wives and mothers.''\par \par \uc2 It would become one of the defining culture wars of the new democracy for decades to come.\par \par \uc2 Women's groups organized protests and signed petitions. Three in four Poles told pollsters at the time that they preferred the issue to be settled by a referendum, not by Parliament. The country was almost evenly split, with 53 percent in favor of the liberal status quo.\par \par \uc2 Even so, Parliament outlawed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1993 with three exceptions: danger to the health or life of the mother; rape or incest; fetal abnormalities.\par \par \uc2 After the ban, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  swiftly moved underground. Doctors who had previously offered free terminations in public hospitals charged handsomely for terminations in private clinics. They offered gynecological services through classified ads with only a phone number and code words like ''anesthesia'' and ''safe.'' While legal abortions dropped to about a thousand a year, the actual number of terminations has stayed around 150,000, advocacy groups like Federa estimate.\par \par \uc2 ''Everyone just made do,'' Ms. Sroda said. ''It was a bigger problem for women from smaller towns and villages, who did not have sufficient means for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a private clinic.''\par \par \uc2 Or, as Ms. Kacpura put it: ''The ban has done a lot of things. What it hasn't done is stop abortions.''\par \par \uc2 In 1996, a left-leaning Parliament passed a law restoring \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights only to have it struck down by the Constitutional Court a few months later. Citing Article 38 of the Polish Constitution, which protects ''the life of every human being,'' the court ruled that this included unborn life.\par \par \uc2 It was a harbinger of a broader shift in the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate. Over the next two decades, public schools were obliged to teach children about ''responsible parenthood'' and ''life in the prenatal phase.'' \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  became a taboo.\par \par \uc2 And an emboldened anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement led by an alliance of Catholic organizations started lobbying to take away the most-used exception to the ban -- fetal abnormalities -- which had been used for almost all of the 1,000 legal abortions a year in Poland.\par \par \uc2 After the nationalist Law and Justice party won power in 2015, it embraced a near-absolute ban as part of its traditionalist agenda.\par \par \uc2 A wave of mass protests ensued. Legislation promoting the ban twice failed to pass in Parliament.\par \par \uc2 But it took effect anyway last year after Poland's highest court intervened again.\par \par \uc2 Aborting a fetus with abnormalities, said the court's president, \uc2 Julia Przylebska\uc2 , constituted ''eugenic practices'' and ''a directly forbidden form of discrimination.''\par \par \uc2 In nearly three decades, there have been only a handful of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related trials, and no doctors, women or activists have so far been convicted, Ms. Kacpura said. But the political climate has become progressively more intimidating, she and other activists say.\par \par \uc2 Underground abortions have largely stopped, forcing women to either import pills or travel to clinics just over the borders with the Czech Republic, Germany and Slovakia. Few now openly call for a full return to the liberal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights of Communist times. The anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  camp holds regular ''family marches,'' and billboard campaigns with outsize fetuses are a common sight across the country.\par \par \uc2 Though two exceptions to the Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban remain, in practice, neither is used much: Rape victims require a certificate from a prosecutor, which takes time to obtain.\par \par \uc2 And as Ms. Sajbor's case illustrates, ''serious risk'' to the mother's health now competes with that to the fetus.\par \par \uc2 A Legal Trap\par \par \uc2 Ms. Sajbor, a 30-year-old hairdresser from the small town of Pszczyna in southern Poland, had been thrilled to be pregnant. She wanted a sibling for her 9-year-old daughter, Maja.\par \par \uc2 It was a shock when in her 14th week she learned that doctors suspected her fetus had Edwards syndrome, a serious chromosomal abnormality, and diagnosed other malformations. Instead of a nose, there was only cartilage. The feet were deformed. One heart chamber was dysfunctional.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Sajbor would have had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if it had still been available in Poland, said her sister-in-law and close friend Barbara Skrobol. She even asked her local doctor about it last summer, but was told it was not an option.\par \par \uc2 At that point, ''She looked into having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  abroad, but then her water broke,'' Ms. Skrobol recalled in an interview.\par \par \uc2 When Ms. Sajbor was admitted to a hospital last Sept. 21, her mother and husband were not allowed to be with her because of Covid restrictions.\par \par \uc2 ''I have to give birth to a dead baby,'' she texted her mother. ''Thanks to PiS I am lying down and waiting,'' she wrote, referring to the governing party by its Polish acronym.\par \par \uc2 By the time the baby's heartbeat had stopped and the doctors took Ms. Sajbor into the operating room the next morning, her limbs had already gone blue.\par \par \uc2 She died at 7:30 a.m.\par \par \uc2 When Ms. Sajbor's family went public with her case in November, it prompted nationwide protests. It was the first time that a woman was thought to have died because of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, Ms. Kacpura said. The hospital issued a statement afterward defending the doctors' actions, saying that they had done ''everything in their power'' to save her life and had acted in accordance with Polish law.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Polish Health Ministry\uc2  responded to Ms. Sajbor's death by issuing new guidelines reminding doctors that ''they should not be afraid to take obvious decisions'' if there were a threat to the woman's life.\par \par \uc2 But doctors and supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights say the law still leaves doctors wary of prosecution.\par \par \uc2 ''The law has a chilling effect on doctors,'' said Jolanta Budzowska, a Krakow-based lawyer who is representing Ms. Sajbor's family and three others in negligence cases related to the new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law.\par \par \uc2 With the new law, said Dr. Kochanowicz, the hospital director, doctors ''risk not only losing the right to practice, but also criminal liability.''\par \par \uc2 ''All decisions are burdened with anxiety,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Since Ms. Sajbor's death, other seemingly similar cases have emerged and are being investigated by the authorities.\par \par \uc2 In January, a 37-year-old woman carrying twins died after one of her fetuses had died and doctors did not remove it for seven days. The family accused the doctors of waiting for fear of harming the other fetus and being subject to possible prosecution.\par \par \uc2 Another woman, who was 19 weeks pregnant when her water broke, developed an infection and nearly died after doctors waited four days until her fetus's heartbeat had stopped before removing it.\par \par \uc2 Threat of Prosecution\par \par \uc2 For women seeking abortions in Poland -- and those trying to assist them -- the routes have become treacherous. In February 2020, Justyna Wydrzynska, a prominent \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights activist, got a panicked message from one of them.\par \par \uc2 The woman was in an abusive relationship. Her partner was watching her every move and would report her to the police if she went abroad for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''Please help me,'' she pleaded in a hushed phone call, ''because I will no longer survive.''\par \par \uc2 For Ms. Wydrzynska, the case felt personal. In 2006, already a mother of three, she had been living with a violent husband and had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  herself.\par \par \uc2 ''I know what it means to have to choose between the safety of your child and your own safety,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Though Ms. Wydrzynska works openly out of a headquarters in central Warsaw, the activists must carefully skirt around Poland's anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law.\par \par \uc2 Under the law, a woman cannot be prosecuted for taking the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, but you can go to jail for helping someone else get them. So Ms. Wydrzynska normally provides only instructions on how to buy and use the pills.\par \par \uc2 But that day, the woman's desperation compelled her to send her a package of pills. The woman's partner, who was reading her text messages and emails, reported Ms. Wydrzynska to the police.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Wydrzynska is now standing trial and faces up to three years in prison. A verdict is expected in September.\par \par \uc2 Her team is part of a Europe-wide network called \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Without Borders that helps Polish women terminate pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 One challenge is to protect women from exploitation. ''\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics are like petrol stations,'' said Zuzanna Dziuban, a member of Ciocia Basia -- or Auntie Barbara -- a feminist collective in Germany. ''The closer you get to the border, the more expensive they become.''\par \par \uc2 Like Going Back in Time\par \par \uc2 In a light-filled, sixth-floor clinic in a leafy corner of central Berlin, Dr. Sabine M\u252\'00\'FCller, who has been a gynecologist for more than 25 years, reserves half of her \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  slots for Polish women every week.\par \par \uc2 ''The demand has gone up,'' Dr. M\u252\'00\'FCller said, sitting in her consulting room one recent morning. ''And the stories have gotten worse.''\par \par \uc2 ''We have a lot of Polish women with cancer who are told, 'No, we can't give you cancer treatment because you're pregnant and it could hurt the baby,''' Dr. M\u252\'00\'FCller said.\par \par \uc2 Recently, a 39-year-old woman with metastasizing bronchial cancer came for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after her Polish doctor had delayed her chemotherapy for six weeks.\par \par \uc2 ''Cancer spreads extremely fast during pregnancy,'' Dr. M\u252\'00\'FCller said. ''A six-week delay is almost a death sentence.''\par \par \uc2 Since last year's ban on abortions of fetuses with abnormalities, the demand for late-term abortions has surged, too.\par \par \uc2 About 80 Polish women a year used to visit Heemstede, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic near Amsterdam that specializes in late-term abortions, which are allowed in the Netherlands until the 24th week. Last year, of 3,000 abortions performed there, 400 were provided to Polish women.\par \par \uc2 ''We used to be a clinic for unwanted pregnancies,'' said Femke van Straaten, the clinic's manager. ''The Polish women that come here now are a very different group.''\par \par \uc2 Many wanted to have a child and were heartbroken to discover well into their second trimesters that their fetuses had serious defects.\par \par \uc2 To comfort Polish patients, the clinic's staff is learning Polish, and the clinic started cooperating with the local cemetery, which runs a memorial garden where, once a month, ashes of fetuses are scattered.\par \par \uc2 When Heemstede opened in 1971, the Dutch justice minister tried to shut it down, and women's groups routinely clashed with riot police officers just outside its gates.\par \par \uc2 Now, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protesters are showing up again.\par \par \uc2 ''It's like going 50 years back in time,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Barbara Skrobol with her niece. The girl's mother, Izabela Sajbor, died at 22 weeks pregnant. (A1); JUSTYNA WYDRZYNSKA, above, who had an \uc2  while in a violent relationship. Ms. Wydrzynska, a prominent activist, faces up to three years in prison for sending a woman \uc2  pills. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY ANNA LIMINOWICZ FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); ''They cannot help as long as the fetus is alive thanks to the anti-\uc2  law,'' Izabela Sajbor wrote. ''A woman is like an incubator.''; Dr. Sabine M\u252\'00\'FCller, a Berlin gynecologist who has practiced for more than 25 years, reserves half of her \uc2  slots for Polish women every week. ''The demand has gone up,'' she said. ''And the stories have gotten worse.'' (PHOTOGRAPH BY LENA MUCHA FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A6); BARBARA SKROBOL, above, visiting the grave of her sister-in-law Izabela Sajbor and Ms. Sajbor's unborn child in Cwiklice, Poland. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ANNA LIMINOWICZ FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A7) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220613ei6d0004g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
