\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Vanishing Word in the  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Debate: Women\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michael Powell \par \uc2 1,342 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Progressive groups and medical organizations have adopted inclusive language, which has led to terms like ''pregnant people'' and ''chestfeeding.''\par \par \uc2 The American Civil Liberties Union\uc2 , whose advocacy on \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  is of more than a half-century vintage, recently tweeted its alarm about the precarious state of legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 ''\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans disproportionately harm: Black Indigenous and other people of color. The L.G.B.T.Q. community. Immigrants. Young people. Those working to make ends meet. People with disabilities. Protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access is an urgent matter of racial and economic justice.''\par \par \uc2 This tweet encompassed so much and so many and yet neglected to mention a relevant demographic: women.\par \par \uc2 This was not an oversight, nor was it peculiar to the language favored by the A.C.L.U. Language has been changing fast, even as the Supreme Court appears poised to overturn a constitutional guarantee to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and progressives face the task of spearheading opposition.\par \par \uc2 From Planned Parenthood to \uc2 NARAL Pro-Choice America\uc2  to the \uc2 American Medical Association\uc2  to city and state health departments and younger activists, the word ''women'' has in a matter of a few years appeared far less in talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Driven by allies and activists for transgender people, medical, government and progressive organizations have adopted gender-neutral language that draws few distinctions between women and transgender men, as well as those who reject those identities altogether.\par \par \uc2 This speed of change is evident: In 2020, NARAL issued a guide to activists on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that stressed they should talk about a ''woman's choice.'' Two years later, the same guide emphasized the need for ''gender-neutral language.''\par \par \uc2 Last year, the editor of The Lancet, a British medical journal, apologized for a cover that referred to ''bodies with vaginas'' rather than women.\par \par \uc2 Today, ''pregnant people'' and ''birthing people'' have elbowed aside ''pregnant women.''\par \par \uc2 The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2  has a section on ''Care for Breastfeeding People,'' the governor of New York issued guidance on partners accompanying ''birthing people'' during Covid, and city and some state health departments offer ''people who are pregnant'' advice on ''chestfeeding.''\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Cleveland Clinic\uc2 , a well-known nonprofit hospital, posed a question on its website: ''Who has a vagina?'' Its answer begins, ''People who are assigned female at birth (AFAB) have vaginas.'' The \uc2 American Cancer Society\uc2  website recommends cancer screenings for ''people with a cervix.''\par \par \uc2 This reflects a desire by medical professionals to find a language that does not exclude and gives comfort to those who give birth and identify as nonbinary or transgender. No agency appears to collect data on transgender and nonbinary pregnancies, but Australia has reported that about 0.1 percent of all births involve transgender men.\par \par \uc2 Ti-Grace Atkinson got on the phone from her home in Cambridge, Mass., and sighed. She has counted herself a radical feminist for most of her 83 years. She quit the \uc2 National Organization for Women\uc2  in the 1960s when it refused to aggressively push for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 She is wearied by battles over gender and language, which she said are pushed by transgender activists and eager progressives and no less eagerly opposed by right-wing politicians. It is distant from the urgent needs of women, who make up 50.8 percent of the population.\par \par \uc2 ''I want to see material change,'' she said. ''Taking away our \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  is going to sharpen the battle. This is about women and our rights; it's not a language game.''\par \par \uc2 Last year, Dr. Sara Dahlen wrote an editorial for a British medical journal in which she noted the pressure for clinicians in Britain, where questions of gender are no less charged, to use phrases such as ''human milk'' rather than ''breast milk.'' She cautioned they risked losing a larger audience.\par \par \uc2 ''If the aim is to maximize respect for every person's sense of self, it must follow that female patients who simply understand themselves as women cannot either be expected to 'go along silently with language in which they do not exist,''' she wrote, quoting advocates of gender-neutral language.\par \par \uc2 For those who fight in the trenches of reproductive politics, the surprise is that a turn to gender-neutral language surprises. Louise Melling, a deputy legal director for the A.C.L.U., noted that not long ago male pronouns and terms such as ''mankind'' were considered sufficient to cover all women. Language is a powerful instrument, she said, and helps to determine political consciousness.\par \par \uc2 ''Language evolves and it can exclude or it can include,'' Ms. Melling noted in an interview. ''It's really important to me that we think about pregnant people. It's the truth: Not only women give birth, not only women seek \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 NARAL punctuated this point in a tweet last year defending its use of ''birthing people'': ''We use gender neutral language when talking about pregnancy, because it's not just cis-gender women that can get pregnant and give birth.''\par \par \uc2 Feminists such as Ms. Atkinson and the writer J.K. Rowling have been outspoken in stating that women have a right to their spaces -- locker rooms, domestic abuse shelters, prisons -- that are separate from men and transgender women.\par \par \uc2 These and other pointed criticisms angered transgender activists and their allies, who denounced them as transphobes. Some object as well to the language of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights movement, which talks of a ''war on women.'' ''It's really difficult,'' a transgender activist wrote, ''to be present in a movement that is so incredibly cissexist.''\par \par \uc2 In New York City, the progressive Working Families Party and Democratic Socialists of America are political powers. When Politico obtained a draft opinion that indicated the Supreme Court was primed to overturn Roe v. Wade, which provides a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , these parties issued ringing denunciations -- in studiously gender-neutral language.\par \par \uc2 The world of mainstream Democratic politics gives voice to these sentiments in a more familiar argot, one aimed at voters rather than activists.\par \par \uc2 Last year the Biden administration put out budget documents that reflected the gendered discourse of progressives and referred to ''birthing people.'' Conservatives pounced.\par \par \uc2 But this month, when word leaked of a potential Supreme Court turnabout, President Biden was unequivocal and practiced in his language choices. ''I believe that a woman's right to choose is fundamental,'' he said. ''Basic fairness and the stability of our law demand that it not be overturned.''\par \par \uc2 A few left-leaning congressional representatives have adopted movement language. Representative \uc2 Cori Bush\uc2 , Democrat of Missouri, testified last year about ''birthing people.'' But it is far more common to hear senators and congressional representatives, female and male alike, refer to women. ''We cannot go back to the days when women had to risk their lives to end an unwanted pregnancy,'' said Senator Bernie Sanders, a democratic socialist who represents Vermont.\par \par \uc2 Prof. Laurel Elder of Hartwick College and Prof. Steven Greene of \uc2 North Carolina State University\uc2  have studied the growth of feminist identity by age and education. Many young activists, Professor Elder noted, reject male and female distinctions altogether. ''But,'' she said, ''the reality is that the larger society is not there yet.''\par \par \uc2 Professor Greene questioned the wisdom of activists in insisting that a mass-based movement discard its base and core sexual identity. Why not, for instance, insist that women and transgender men are each embattled when it comes to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 ''Activists are adopting symbols and language that are off-putting not just to the right but to people in the center and even liberals,'' he noted.\par \par \uc2 For this reason he was not surprised when most Democratic politicians declined to echo the language of progressive organizations. ''You don't become a candidate for the presidency or speaker of the House by being dumb about what works in politics,'' he said. ''Democrats were not going to be afraid to use the word 'women.'''\par \par \uc2 Medical professionals are seeking to find a language that includes those who give birth and identify as nonbinary or transgender. (PHOTOGRAPH BY SCOTT OLSON/GETTY IMAGES) (A11) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220609ei690004n\par }\par \b \uc2 Search Summary\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\b